entError` when no cleanup owner exists and still prioritizes a retained owner | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ9`; output/JUnit/stdout regressions return exit 1 with fixed safe text and existing owner-priority tests remain green |\n| `MCP170-TARGET-KIND-STRUCTURE` | A custom target's arbitrary `describe().kind` can be reinserted raw after descriptor redaction and expose target-owned credential material | Fixed and pushed; recurring credential-boundary family | round 8 | round 8 | `dfaeef0d1…`: validate the dynamic kind through the connected client's shared structural-preservation enforcement and fail the projection closed on collision while preserving the established incomplete-reason contract | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTA`; focused regression proves the raw kind is absent and the bounded report is incomplete |\n| `MCP170-CONFORMANCE-RUNTIME-OUTPUT` | Conformance stdout, stderr, or report persistence failures after execution are misclassified as CLI usage errors | Fixed and pushed; recurring CLI runtime-output family | round 8 | round 8 | `dfaeef0d1…`: supply a fixed safe conformance failure message to the cleanup-aware output wrapper without weakening cleanup-owner priority | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTI`; report/stdout/stderr regressions return exit 1 and omit raw callback/filesystem details |\n| `MCP170-PROJECTION-FAILURE-TAXONOMY` | Non-credential descriptor/serialization projection failures are mislabeled as credential-redaction failures | Actionable; report-evidence sibling | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: track credential projection failure separately from generic projection/serialization failure | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwtNJ`; existing hostile-descriptor and kind-collision regressions must retain distinct safe reasons |\n| `MCP170-PERSISTENT-STDERR-CLASSIFICATION` | A persistently rejecting stderr sink can replace a classified conformance runtime failure | Actionable; recurring CLI runtime-output family | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: make classified runtime diagnostic writes best effort without changing cleanup-owner priority or usage-error handling | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwvKs`; regression must reject every stderr invocation and still resolve to exit 1 |\n| `MCP170-STRUCTURAL-KEY-COLLISION` | The shared artifact scrubber exempts credential patterns that collide with mandatory schema keys | Actionable; recurring credential-boundary family | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: reject fixed-key collisions with a distinct signal and prevent suite fallback serialization when no safe typed envelope exists | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwwv0`; direct and target-suite evidence must prove a `timeline` credential is never serialized |\n| `MCP170-DIAGNOSTIC-TARGET-STRUCTURE` | Client event/diagnostic targets redact the required `kind` key/value as payload without omission accounting | Actionable; recurring credential-boundary family | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: validate and reconstruct target descriptors through the client structural-safety boundary, including safe omission fallbacks | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwwv5`; direct event/diagnostic regressions must cover value collision and no-safe-key fallback |\n\n## Round 1 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 8 unresolved GitHub threads, deduplicated to 4 code/test root causes, plus 1 Sonar required-check root cause. All five root causes are fixed by `45785a27458b9f42e20e1fc3023489a0a7d3998d`; the captured threads were not resolved or refetched.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 71 unresolved GitHub threads (29 current-hunk and 42 outdated-hunk) trace to current implementations/tests that already enforce the reported behavior. Outdated status was not used as proof; current producer/consumer paths were inspected.\n- Not Attempted: the 35 non-gating Sonar maintainability/reliability findings remain recorded under `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY`; no GitHub review item is unassessed.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 GitHub comment is invalid based on the passing observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks. Analyzer `throw this` findings target classes extending `Error`; the reported Basic-token regex operates only after the 8 KiB credential-value bound, so these specific diagnostics are not unbounded runtime defects.\n- Unassessed: 0 GitHub review threads; 0 supplementary review bodies/comments.\n\n## Round 1 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: 17 check contexts were terminal: 13 successful, 1 failed (SonarCloud security rating), 1 neutral, and 2 skipped. `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted in this round.\n\n## Round 1 convergence\n\n- Trend: improving locally: five selected blocking root-cause families were repaired and verified, but the round is not clean because it changed/pushed the head, the only allowed check snapshot predates the push and contained a failure, and one broad non-gating analyzer-maintainability family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 5 / 1 deferred non-gating analyzer-maintainability family; all selected functional and required-check root causes are fixed locally.\n- New defects: OAuth token cache-envelope validation, inaccurate regression evidence, and CI wheel-only bootstrap.\n- Recurring families: cleanup ownership and credential payload redaction.\n- Fix-induced regressions: none found after whole-batch review and the release gate. One reviewer claim about an incorrect authenticated-artifact assertion was disproved by execution and classified Invalid.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: broad Sonar maintainability inventory from the PR's earlier commits; security-rating failure is selected for this round because it is the only reported failed gate condition.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by actionable findings, a code change/push, the changed PR head, the failed immediate check snapshot, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: snapshot collection was stable at `1e45b794d946722409dc86681a1ee9a34bfe7f2f`; the authorized round then advanced local/remote/PR head together to `45785a27458b9f42e20e1fc3023489a0a7d3998d`.\n\n## Round 1 verification evidence\n\n- Focused: 35 client tests, 28 inspector authenticated-artifact tests, 50 hosted-authorization/mock-server tests, and 37 CLI tests passed.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1833},"formatted_output":"# PR 170 recurring remediation ledger\n\n## Workflow identity\n\n- Hosted skill: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`\n- Revision: `4`\n- Digest: `sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022`\n- Automation: `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82`\n- Repository / PR: `21nCo/super-functions#170`\n- Head branch: `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`\n\n## Current round\n\n- Round: `9`\n- Mode: `assessment` (credential-structure, report-failure taxonomy, and CLI runtime-output families recurred; coordinated reassessment completed before mutation)\n- Local pre-snapshot HEAD: `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad`\n- Snapshot / PR head: `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad`\n- Snapshot consistency: stable across all eleven review-thread pages, five review pages, fifteen raw-inline pages, two issue-comment pages, PR metadata, and the immediate check snapshot; all observed heads matched and every nested thread-comment connection was terminal\n- Review surfaces: 325 threads, 220 review records, 64 issue comments, and 364 raw inline comments\n- Automation state: disabled and verified after an initial retrieval-tool discovery failure; this manually retried round uses the subsequently retrieved and verified pinned workflow, but the schedule was not silently re-enabled\n- Commit / push budget: unused before the round-9 repair batch\n- Round commit: pending\n- Push: pending\n- One allowed post-push head comparison: pending\n\n## Broader subsystem assessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing MCP operations must preserve retryable resource ownership until cleanup succeeds, never expose opaque credential material at any consumer-visible boundary, and keep authored report/event structure intact. Conformance evidence must reject insecure OAuth token envelopes, regression tests must assert the behavior they claim, and CI bootstrap must not execute source-distribution setup code.\n- Producers and consumers: credential providers and authenticated targets produce leases and target-owned redaction; `McpFnClient` owns transports/events/diagnostics; testing suites and official conformance produce bounded reports; inspector produces snapshots/scenarios; `runCli` persists operator artifacts; `bin.ts` is the only process-termination boundary. Hosted OAuth servers produce redirect/token responses consumed by the authorization regression runner. GitHub Actions consumes Python bootstrap packages.\n- Failure boundaries: acquisition/open/initialize, inventory and scenario execution, payload/envelope projection, report serialization, protocol/transport/revoke/dispose cleanup, programmatic CLI error propagation, executable stderr flush/termination, authorization-code exchange and refresh, and package bootstrap in CI.\n- Coordinated repair: (1) persist any bounded cleanup-failure artifact, then propagate the owning cleanup error through `runCli`; retry once at the outer library boundary; only `bin.ts` prints a safe diagnostic and terminates for all three retryable cleanup error types; (2) force payload mode for every inspector snapshot projection; (3) validate JSON plus `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Pragma: no-cache` for both exchange and refresh success responses; (4) correct misleading/incorrect regressions; (5) require binary Python distributions in the failing Sonar-gated bootstrap step. These are complete enforcement points for the current findings rather than commented-line patches.\n- Verification criteria: focused old-behavior regressions for cleanup retry failure/success, executable diagnostics, snapshot payload collisions, key-only scenario redaction, exchange/refresh cache headers, and misleading retry configuration; then package typechecks/tests, the Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and one pre-push review of the complete diff. Hosted checks are observed only in the already captured immediate snapshot.\n\n### Round 2 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: consumer-visible reports and snapshots must never silently remain passing when credential safety or evidence completeness cannot be guaranteed, and executable termination must not discard already-produced evidence.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated target credential leases and the shared scrubber produce protected values and redacted artifacts; target suites, inspector and JUnit consume authored structural envelopes; official conformance produces captured streams; `serializeBoundedReport` persists bounded operator evidence; `runCli` writes reports; `bin.ts` owns forced process termination.\n- Failure boundaries: credential text colliding with authored discriminator values, final JSON recomposition, report-size truncation, asynchronous stdout backpressure, cleanup-owner propagation into the executable boundary, and non-enumerable `Error.message` / `cause` fields in regressions.\n- Coordinated repair: reject structural-value credential collisions at the shared scrubber while preserving authored schema keys; mark truncated conformance reports explicitly incomplete and force exit 1; await every CLI output write before cleanup retry/termination; assert cleanup error messages and reports separately.\n- Verification criteria: collision regressions for `pass` / `complete` and existing structural values; a successful oversized conformance result must persist as bounded `status: incomplete`, `ok: false`, exit 1; delayed stdout must complete before cleanup retry can reject; the cleanup assertion must inspect both message and report. Then package typechecks/tests, the full Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 3 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: a retryable cleanup failure must retain the exact resource owner until cleanup succeeds; later output, diagnostic, persistence, or sanitization failures may be reported only after ownership is safely preserved. Public-client OAuth fixtures must distinguish protocol-compliant token rejection from an authentication challenge.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated conformance and target suites produce retryable cleanup-owner errors; `McpFnInspector` owns an `McpFnClient` transport; command actions produce stdout/stderr and persisted reports; `runCli` performs the one bounded retry; `bin.ts` is the final process boundary. Hosted authorization fixtures produce token exchange/refresh rejections consumed by the conformance runner.\n- Failure boundaries: inspector close after operation/output failure, conformance and target-suite output/report persistence after a cleanup artifact exists, outer diagnostic output before cleanup classification/retry, and unauthenticated token exchange/refresh status validation.\n- Coordinated repair: add a retryable inspector cleanup owner; prioritize every cleanup owner over secondary output/persistence/diagnostic failures; preserve that owner through the bounded retry and executable termination boundary; and require HTTP 400 for `invalid_client` from public-client exchange and refresh fixtures.\n- Verification criteria: regressions must show inspector ownership survives two failed closes and can later be retried; conformance and target-suite owners must survive rejecting output callbacks and report persistence failures; executable classification must include inspector cleanup; public-client `invalid_client` must pass at 400 and fail at 401 for exchange and refresh. Then focused tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 4 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: while a target owns credentials, every consumer-visible dynamic value—including reconstructed schema discriminators—must be proven safe by that target's redactor before emission; if mandatory structure collides with a credential, the producer must fail closed or emit a safe marked omission. Any abstraction that exposes `close()` must retain and return the retry owner when close fails.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient` produces diagnostic and client-event envelopes consumed directly and by `McpFnInspector`; the inspector reconstructs snapshot, timeline, and scenario discriminators for library and CLI consumers. `McpFnTestClient` owns a production `McpFnClient`; direct callers and `mcpfn test` consume its public `close()` contract, while `runCli` already recognizes `McpFnTestClientCleanupError`.\n- Failure boundaries: structural values reconstructed after payload redaction (`kind`, `phase`, `outcome`, `clientState`, target kind, scenario side effect), safe omission fallback construction, inspector snapshot/export assembly, and post-connect local test-client shutdown after successful operations.\n- Coordinated repair: add one client structural-safety enforcement point backed by the active target redactor; apply it to event/diagnostic discriminators and all inspector-reconstructed discriminators, dropping only when no schema-safe omission can be produced; then make `McpFnTestClient.close()` wrap retained session cleanup in the existing retryable owner while preserving connection-failure cause semantics during failed connect cleanup.\n- Verification criteria: credentials equal to client/inspector structural values must never appear in direct events, timelines, snapshots, or scenario exports; safe omissions must be marked and counted, and mandatory snapshot/export collisions must reject with credential-free errors. A post-connect test-client close must retain the same owner through repeated failure and later succeed, and the CLI's existing owner retry boundary must remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 5 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every emitted event or diagnostic must preserve both credential confidentiality and schema semantics. A timestamp may be emitted only as the exact generated ISO string after the active target redactor proves that string safe; redaction must never turn it into a malformed timestamp.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient.emitEvent()` and diagnostic dispatch produce `at`; direct event/diagnostic listeners, `McpFnInspector` timelines/snapshots, target-suite timelines, CLI serialization, and mixed package consumers retain it.\n- Failure boundaries: timestamp generation, active-target structural safety validation, construction of a marked omission fallback, and the no-safe-fallback case where every ISO timestamp collides with a credential.\n- Coordinated repair: enforce one ISO timestamp structural validator in the shared client producer; use it for normal client events, normal diagnostics, and both omission fallbacks. On collision, route through existing omission accounting; emit a marked fallback only with a safe valid ISO timestamp, otherwise drop the artifact while retaining the omission count.\n- Verification criteria: an exact timestamp credential must never appear and must produce a marked omission whose `at` round-trips through `Date.toISOString()`; a credential present in every ISO representation must emit no malformed artifact and must increment the appropriate omission counter. Then client tests/typecheck, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 6 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every terminal cleanup failure must retain both its retry owner and its actual failure phase. Transport shutdown is `transport-close`; OAuth revocation is `token-revocation`; disposal after successful revocation returns to `transport-close`. A generic outer cleanup boundary must not overwrite a more specific trusted phase.\n- Producers / consumers: the authenticated HTTP target owns the credential lease and produces target diagnostics; `McpFnClient` coordinates handle/target cleanup and produces retryable client errors; `McpFnTestClient` retains the close owner; target-suite diagnostics, bounded reports, JUnit, CLI, and direct diagnostic consumers classify the resulting phase and failure layer.\n- Failure boundaries: raw transport close before credential release, OAuth `revoke()`, provider `dispose()`, client cleanup-drain wrapping, test-client error wrapping, and final suite report normalization.\n- Coordinated repair: throw a phase-bearing safe cleanup error from the credential lease; emit the lease phase only after transport shutdown succeeds; validate and preserve `token-revocation` through the client cleanup coordinator; and normalize the retained inner cleanup error in the target suite so a later generic close diagnostic cannot replace the terminal phase.\n- Verification criteria: direct target diagnostics must report transport failure as `transport-close`, OAuth revoke failure as `token-revocation`, and post-revocation disposal failure as `transport-close`; suite reports must map those phases to `authorization-server` and `mcpfn-preflight` respectively while retaining a retryable cleanup owner. Then client/testing focused tests and typechecks, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 7 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential cleanup errors may expose only a trusted phase, safe fixed text, and retry ownership; raw provider failures must not cross the lease, target, client, suite, inspector, or CLI boundary. Every independently reflectable credential component, including structured Cookie values, must be registered before artifacts are emitted. Target-suite post-execution artifact I/O failures are runtime failures and must not replace a retained cleanup owner.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers produce header material and revoke/dispose failures; `credentialValues` and target secret scopes produce the redaction set; credential leases and authenticated targets own retryable cleanup; `McpFnClient` coordinates target/handle cleanup; suites, JUnit, inspector, and CLI serialize consumer-visible evidence. The target suite produces reports consumed by `runCli`, whose output writers and executable boundary classify failures.\n- Failure boundaries: structured Cookie parsing, pre-handle header validation/open failure, revoke/dispose rejection, target-level pending-release retries, client cleanup-drain/close wrapping, report/JUnit/stdout persistence, and final exit-code classification.\n- Coordinated repair: extract and validate Cookie credential components at the shared secret-discovery point; sanitize lease cleanup failures and client cleanup wrappers while preserving the trusted phase separately; rethrow the retained safe rejection from target-level cleanup instead of synthesizing `transport-close`; and convert target-suite artifact/output failures into a safe runtime error without displacing any cleanup owner.\n- Verification criteria: a reflected Cookie value must be absent from direct redaction and JSON/JUnit artifacts; a provider error containing a credential must not be reachable through cleanup cause chains; repeated OAuth revoke failure during a pre-handle open failure must remain `token-revocation` through client/suite reporting and later retry; target report/JUnit/stdout write failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with safe text, while owner-priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 8 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every target-controlled value that occupies authored report structure must be proven unchanged by the active live-session redactor before emission; a structural collision must make the report safely incomplete. Every post-execution conformance output or persistence failure is an operational failure with fixed safe text and exit 1, while an existing retryable cleanup owner remains authoritative.\n- Producers / consumers: custom targets produce arbitrary `describe().kind` values and target-owned redaction; the live `McpFnClient` owns the active redactor and structural-preservation check; the target suite reconstructs bounded JSON/JUnit artifacts consumed by libraries and `mcpfn test-target`. Official conformance produces captured streams and bounded reports consumed by `runCli`, stdout/stderr callbacks, and report persistence.\n- Failure boundaries: suite projection after scenario execution but before credential cleanup; reinsertion of the raw target discriminator after descriptor payload redaction; final JSON/JUnit serialization; conformance stdout/stderr callbacks and report-file writes; cleanup-owner priority over secondary I/O failures.\n- Coordinated repair: validate the dynamic target kind with the live client's shared `preserveArtifactStructure` enforcement before reconstructing the descriptor so a collision sets the existing projection-failure path; apply the existing cleanup-aware safe runtime-output wrapper to the complete conformance stdout/stderr/report operation with a conformance-specific fixed message.\n- Verification criteria: a custom target whose redactor treats its arbitrary kind as a credential must return a bounded incomplete report with the raw kind absent; conformance report, stdout, and stderr failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with fixed safe text and omit raw I/O details, while the existing cleanup-owner priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks/builds, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 9 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing artifacts may preserve authored schema only when both structural keys and discriminator values are proven safe; if a credential collides with mandatory structure and no safe typed artifact exists, the producer must reject with credential-free runtime failure rather than emit the credential as a key. Redaction/serialization failures must retain truthful safe taxonomy, and a failing diagnostic sink must never replace an already classified runtime outcome.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers and target secret scopes produce protected variants; the shared testing scrubber preserves report/inspector envelope keys; `McpFnClient` produces event and diagnostic target descriptors; suites and inspector/CLI serialize consumer-visible JSON/JUnit; `runCli` classifies operational failures and writes diagnostics through an injectable stderr sink.\n- Failure boundaries: fixed-key traversal and final serialized-output validation; live target descriptor reconstruction for events/diagnostics and omission fallbacks; suite projection of hostile descriptors versus target redaction collisions; impossible-to-serialize structural-key collisions; and the outer CLI diagnostic write after an output failure is already classified.\n- Coordinated repair: reject fixed-key credential collisions at the shared scrubber with a distinguishable structural-collision signal; ensure suite callers do not replace that impossible artifact with another colliding fallback; reconstruct client target descriptors only after validating the authored `kind` key and dynamic kind value, routing failures through omission accounting; separate credential projection failure from generic descriptor/serialization failure; and make runtime-error diagnostics best effort while leaving usage-error behavior distinct.\n- Verification criteria: direct redaction and target-suite consumers must reject a credential equal to `timeline` without serializing it; event/diagnostic target-kind and target-key collisions must emit marked safe omissions or drop them while incrementing counts; hostile descriptors must retain generic safe-serialization wording while credential collisions retain credential-redaction wording; a persistently rejecting conformance stderr sink must still resolve to exit 1 without leaking its rejection. Then affected package tests/typechecks/builds, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n## Defect-family ledger\n\n| Stable ID | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Attempted repairs | Regression / verification evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-CREDENTIAL-BOUNDARY` | Credential-safe redaction across reconstructed targets, diagnostics, and emitted artifacts | Fixed in round 7 batch; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 7 | Prior `1e45b794…` plus round 7 structured-Cookie component extraction/fail-closed parsing | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ0`; direct reflected Cookie values are now removed and ambiguous syntax is rejected; testing suite and release gate pass |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-PHASE` | Dynamic revoke/dispose cleanup phase and safe CLI failure handling | Fixed in round 7 batch; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 7 | Prior `1e45b794…` and `a9fc7f4b…`; round 7 preserves the safe release rejection through target-level pre-handle cleanup retries | Threads `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvje4` and `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jv3uO`; failed-open OAuth cleanup remains `token-revocation` / `authorization-server` and the retry owner succeeds later |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-OWNER` | Retryable suite/test/conformance cleanup errors must retain their owning client/lease through `runCli`; only `bin.ts` may terminate, after a diagnostic is flushed | Fixed locally; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 1 | Prior `1e45b794…` fixed some paths; round 1 persists reports, propagates all owning errors, retries at the library boundary, and flushes executable diagnostics | Four current comments deduplicate to this root cause; CLI regressions prove report persistence and a second caller-owned retry after the bounded retry fails |\n| `MCP170-SNAPSHOT-PAYLOAD-MODE` | Inspector standalone descriptor/server/capability/inventory projections must use payload redaction, not envelope preservation | Fixed locally; recurring credential-boundary sibling | pre-ledger | round 1 | Round 1 passes `preserveKeys: false` for all snapshot projections | Two current comments deduplicate to this root cause; mode-sensitive snapshot regression passes |\n| `MCP170-OAUTH-TOKEN-CACHE` | Successful exchange and refresh responses must have a JSON media type and both mandated non-cache headers | Fixed locally | round 1 | round 1 | Round 1 validates both directives and adds them to hosted and mock producers | Exchange/refresh regressions cover each missing directive; auth and testing suites pass |\n| `MCP170-TEST-EVIDENCE` | Regression fixtures must not claim retry behavior they cannot exercise | Fixed locally | round 1 | round 1 | Removed dead `connectRetries` from the initialization-cleanup regression | One current comment. The separate key-redaction assertion comment was disproved by execution: `${MCPFN_SECRET}` is credential-shaped and its associated value is intentionally redacted |\n| `MCP170-CI-SUPPLYCHAIN` | Python bootstrap in the required CI workflow must install wheel-only packages | Fixed; round 2 Sonar snapshot successful | round 1 | round 1 | Added `--only-binary :all:` to the pip/build upgrade step | Sonar security rating failed in round 1; round 2 SonarCloud Code Analysis and Quality Gate passed |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` | External analyzer inventory contains 36 additional maintainability/reliability findings | Not Attempted (non-gating broad refactor inventory); preserve for reassessment | round 1 | not attempted | None | Round 4 Sonar Quality Gate passed, but its summary increased from 35 to 36 open issues; individual analyzer details were not part of the captured GitHub review surfaces and require a separately coordinated behavior-preserving assessment |\n| `MCP170-STRUCTURAL-COLLISION` | Registered credential variants must not be silently exempted when they collide with authored artifact discriminator values | Fixed locally; recurring credential-boundary sibling | round 2 | round 2 | Shared scrubber now rejects structural-value collisions while retaining fixed schema keys; suite callers convert the failure to a non-passing incomplete artifact | `pass`, `complete`, target-kind, side-effect and version collisions fail closed; 113 focused testing regressions pass |\n| `MCP170-CONFORMANCE-CAP` | A size-truncated official conformance artifact must be explicitly incomplete and non-passing | Fixed locally | round 2 | round 2 | Bounded serializer records `status: incomplete`, an explicit reason, `ok: false`, and exit 1 | Oversized-success regression persists within 1025 bytes and returns exit 1 |\n| `MCP170-CLI-OUTPUT-DRAIN` | Cleanup-owner propagation to `bin.ts` must not force-exit before queued stdout evidence is flushed | Fixed locally; cleanup-owner sibling | round 2 | round 2 | CLI output callbacks may be async; default process writes resolve on their callbacks; every call site awaits completion | Delayed-stdout regression proves the cleanup retry does not begin until stdout resolves; CLI suite and release gate pass |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-EVIDENCE` | Cleanup leak regression must inspect non-enumerable error text as well as the enumerable report | Fixed locally | round 2 | round 2 | Assertion checks `error.message` and `JSON.stringify(error.report)` separately | Focused suite regression passes and distinguishes the previously unobserved field |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-OWNER-PRIORITY` | Conformance/target cleanup ownership must survive later report, stdout, stderr, and diagnostic failures | Fixed and pushed; recurring cleanup-owner family | round 3 | round 3 | `9371beeb…`: cleanup-aware output/persistence wrapper preserves the owner; outer diagnostics are best-effort before the bounded retry | Two current comments deduplicate to this root cause; output and persistence replacement regressions pass for conformance and target suites |\n| `MCP170-INSPECT-CLEANUP-OWNER` | Inspector close failures must retain a retryable client/transport owner through `runCli` and `bin.ts` | Fixed and pushed; recurring cleanup-owner family | round 3 | round 3 | `9371beeb…`: retained `McpFnInspectorCleanupError`, bounded library retry, and executable-boundary classification | Inspector regression proves two failed closes retain the owner and a later caller retry closes successfully |\n| `MCP170-OAUTH-PUBLIC-CLIENT-STATUS` | Unauthenticated public-client token exchange/refresh `invalid_client` rejection must require HTTP 400, not accept a 401 client-authentication challenge | Fixed and pushed | round 3 | round 3 | `9371beeb…`: token rejection validation now requires HTTP 400 for all public-client errors | Exchange and refresh regressions pass at 400 and fail envelope validation at 401; requests assert no Authorization header |\n| `MCP170-INSPECTOR-STRUCTURAL-COLLISION` | Direct client/inspector artifacts reconstruct discriminator values after payload redaction and can expose an equal credential | Fixed and pushed; recurring credential-boundary family | round 4 | round 4 | `afcf360f…`: shared client structural-safety enforcement covers events/diagnostics and inspector snapshots/scenarios; omission counters preserve evidence when no safe envelope can be emitted | One current thread names `clientState`; regressions cover authenticated state, target/root kinds, event/diagnostic kinds, scenario kind/side effect, reports, and mixed ESM/CJS consumers |\n| `MCP170-TEST-CLIENT-CLOSE-OWNER` | A successfully connected `McpFnTestClient` can lose its retained session when public `close()` fails | Fixed and pushed; recurring cleanup-owner family | round 4 | round 4 | `afcf360f…`: public `close()` returns the existing retryable owner; target-suite composition retains that exact inner owner | One current thread; regression proves two failed close attempts keep the same owner and a later retry succeeds; CLI owner tests and release gate pass |\n| `MCP170-TIMESTAMP-STRUCTURE` | Credential redaction can turn generated client/diagnostic ISO timestamps into malformed consumer-visible values | Fixed and pushed; recurring credential-boundary family | round 5 | round 5 | `30e7ff8e…`: safe exact ISO timestamps are enforced at the shared client producer and collisions use omission accounting | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jutb6`; exact-collision and no-safe-ISO regressions pass, as does the full release gate |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-ERROR-SANITIZATION` | Raw provider revoke/dispose or target cleanup errors can escape through public client cause chains | Fixed in round 7 batch; cleanup/credential-boundary sibling | round 7 | round 7 | Lease and client cleanup wrappers retain safe fixed text, phase, and retryability without attaching raw cleanup causes | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvjew`; direct lease and client regressions prove provider secrets are absent and public cleanup causes are undefined |\n| `MCP170-STRUCTURED-CREDENTIAL-REDACTION` | Cookie credential components may reflect independently of the complete request header | Fixed in round 7 batch; credential-boundary sibling | round 7 | round 7 | Shared credential discovery extracts unquoted and quoted Cookie values and rejects ambiguous structured syntax | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ0`; direct redaction regressions distinguish the previously leaking values |\n| `MCP170-CLI-RUNTIME-OUTPUT` | Target-suite JSON, JUnit, or stdout failures after execution are misclassified as CLI usage errors | Fixed in round 7 batch | round 7 | round 7 | Target-suite output boundary now raises a safe `McpFnClientError` when no cleanup owner exists and still prioritizes a retained owner | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ9`; output/JUnit/stdout regressions return exit 1 with fixed safe text and existing owner-priority tests remain green |\n| `MCP170-TARGET-KIND-STRUCTURE` | A custom target's arbitrary `describe().kind` can be reinserted raw after descriptor redaction and expose target-owned credential material | Fixed and pushed; recurring credential-boundary family | round 8 | round 8 | `dfaeef0d1…`: validate the dynamic kind through the connected client's shared structural-preservation enforcement and fail the projection closed on collision while preserving the established incomplete-reason contract | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTA`; focused regression proves the raw kind is absent and the bounded report is incomplete |\n| `MCP170-CONFORMANCE-RUNTIME-OUTPUT` | Conformance stdout, stderr, or report persistence failures after execution are misclassified as CLI usage errors | Fixed and pushed; recurring CLI runtime-output family | round 8 | round 8 | `dfaeef0d1…`: supply a fixed safe conformance failure message to the cleanup-aware output wrapper without weakening cleanup-owner priority | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTI`; report/stdout/stderr regressions return exit 1 and omit raw callback/filesystem details |\n| `MCP170-PROJECTION-FAILURE-TAXONOMY` | Non-credential descriptor/serialization projection failures are mislabeled as credential-redaction failures | Actionable; report-evidence sibling | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: track credential projection failure separately from generic projection/serialization failure | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwtNJ`; existing hostile-descriptor and kind-collision regressions must retain distinct safe reasons |\n| `MCP170-PERSISTENT-STDERR-CLASSIFICATION` | A persistently rejecting stderr sink can replace a classified conformance runtime failure | Actionable; recurring CLI runtime-output family | round 9 | round 9 | Planned: make classified runtime diagnostic writes best effort without changing cleanup-owner priority or usage-error handling | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwvKs`; regression must reject every stderr invocation and