permissions or a full filesystem), this plain filesystem error reaches the outer handler and is classified as usage exit code 2 because it is not one of the recognized runtime error classes. This occurs after argument parsing and target execution, and the analogous `inspect` path explicitly wraps output failures as runtime failures, so this path should likewise return the documented test/runtime exit code 1.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTI\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"isResolved\": false,\n      \"currentCount\": 1,\n      \"latest\": {\n        \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:25:27Z\",\n        \"outdated\": false,\n        \"line\": 293,\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047120637\",\n        \"body\": \"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Classify conformance output failures as test failures**\\n\\nWhen `conformance --report` cannot write its file, or an injected stdout/stderr sink rejects, this call supplies no `failureMessage`, so `preserveCleanupOwner()` rethrows the raw I/O error and the outer handler returns usage exit code 2. These failures occur after valid argument parsing and conformance execution, while the documented code 2 is reserved for invalid configuration/source/usage; wrap this path as an `McpFnClientError` so operational report-output failures return code 1.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jyW3y\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/inspector/src/index.ts\",\n      \"isResolved\": false,\n      \"currentCount\": 1,\n      \"latest\": {\n        \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-18T15:03:35Z\",\n        \"outdated\": false,\n        \"line\": 251,\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047930609\",\n        \"body\": \"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Validate top-level scenario keys before export**\\n\\nWhen an active target credential equals a top-level scenario key such as `name`, this mapping redacts only the corresponding value and copies the key verbatim. Consequently, `exportScenario()` returns an artifact containing the exact credential even though nested argument keys are scrubbed; validate the fixed scenario keys with the target redactor or fail the export on a structural collision.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jyW37\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"isResolved\": false,\n      \"currentCount\": 1,\n      \"latest\": {\n        \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-18T15:03:35Z\",\n        \"outdated\": false,\n        \"line\": 460,\n        \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047930620\",\n        \"body\": \"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Reject parameterized cache-prevention directives**\\n\\nFresh evidence after adding the token-response header checks is that `hasHeaderDirective()` accepts malformed values such as `Cache-Control: no-store=false` and `Pragma: no-cache=false` by discarding everything after `=`. Those directives do not satisfy the required `no-store`/`no-cache` response values, yet the hosted regression reports the token exchange as passed; require these argument-free directives to have no parameter.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jyasF\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\",\n      \"isResolved\": false,\n      \"currentCount\": 1,\n      \"latest\": {\n        \"author\": \"pullfrog\",\n        \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-18T15:06:27Z\",\n        \"outdated\"