ts:107\tExtract this nested ternary operation into an independent statement.\t5min\nMAJOR\ttypescript:S3696\tmcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:94\tExpected an error object to be thrown.\t20min\nMAJOR\ttypescript:S3358\tmcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:221\tExtract this nested ternary operation into an independent statement.\t5min\nMAJOR\ttypescript:S3358\tmcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:222\tExtract this nested ternary operation into an independent statement.\t5min\nMAJOR\ttypescript:S3358\tmcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:399\tExtract this nested ternary operation into an independent statement.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S6353\tmcpfn/cli/src/index.ts:557\tUse concise character class syntax '\\w' instead of '[A-Za-z0-9_]'.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S7758\tmcpfn/inspector/src/index.ts:38\tPrefer `String.fromCodePoint()` over `String.fromCharCode()`.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S7780\tmcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts:168\t`String.raw` should be used to avoid escaping `\\`.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S2486\tmcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts:417\tHandle this exception, don't catch it at all, or explain in a comment why it is ignored.\t1h\nMINOR\ttypescript:S1199\tmcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts:472\tNested block is redundant.\t10min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S6653\tmcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts:587\tUse 'Object.hasOwn()' instead of 'Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call()'.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S7744\tmcpfn/testing/src/reports.ts:72\tThe empty object is useless.\t5min\nMINOR\ttypescript:S6606\tmcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:306\tPrefer using nullish coalescing operator (`??=`) instead of an assignment expression, as it is simpler to read.\t5min\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae59b9e90087d081dc8bf4371a2bc8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b90d-72f4-76d0-8550-1d4d61d74375"},"summary":[{"text":"**Summarizing new code complexity hotspots**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Detailing new code smell categories and impacts**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Differentiating actionable code risk categories**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae59c46ef487d0b5306cf1b227e186","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b90d-72f4-76d0-8550-1d4d61d74375"},"summary":[{"text":"**Summarizing file complexity and hotspots**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Confirming no memory citation needed**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The deferred Sonar family currently contains **38 open code smells**, not 36. The ledger’s count is stale; the latest analysis added two findings after round 9.\n\nSummary:\n\n| Category | Count |\n|---|---:|\n| Cognitive complexity | 8 |\n| Nested ternaries | 10 |\n| Throwing non-`Error` values | 4 |\n| Nested template literals | 2 |\n| Potential regex backtracking | 2 |\n| Other single-rule findings | 12 |\n\nSeverity:\n\n- 9 critical\n- 21 major\n- 8 minor\n- Estimated remediation effort: 578 minutes, roughly 9h 38m\n\nMain hotspots:\n\n- `mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts`: 13\n- `mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts`: 6\n- `mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts`: 5\n- `mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts`: 3\n- `mcpfn/testing/src/reports.ts`: 3\n- Eight remaining findings across inspector, conformance, targets, test support, and the external HTTP example.\n\nThe more behavior-sensitive findings are:\n\n- Two potentially super-linear regular expressions in `remote-target.ts`\n- One swallowed exception in `remote-target.ts`\n- Four locations throwing values that Sonar does not recognize as `Error` objects\n- Very high complexity in credential/redaction paths—reported complexities include 79 and 85 against an allowed 15\n\nMost others are maintainability/readability suggestions: extracting ternaries, splitting authorization-mode logic, removing redundant blocks, preferring `Object.hasOwn`, default parameters, `String.raw`, and similar changes.\n\nThe two newest findings introduced by the latest analysis are:\n\n- `mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts:123`: cognitive complexity 18 versus 15\n- `mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts:399`: nested ternary\n\nSo the deferred item is not one coherent defect. It is an umbrella covering **18 distinct Sonar rule families across 11 files**, with a mixture of potentially meaningful runtime concerns and mechanical refactoring advice. That aggregate requires assessment and deduplication before it can be considered resolved for convergence.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae59d7fe0c87d0bd21c0f7b74c06c7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789811156.212469,"turn_id":"01a0b90d-72f4-76d0-8550-1d4d61d74375"},"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"proceed with the next recommended step","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b914-b440-7902-aa9f-04757b7c4d66","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789811536.960287,"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae5b53fdbc87d0987950828cc6198a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"summary":[{"text":"**Assessing and deduplicating Sonar issues**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Preparing thorough Sonar code assessment**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Querying current Sonar issues for classification**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae5b5da05087d0a69409dac6bcb313","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"summary":[{"text":"**Inspecting Sonar S8786 token regex and error handling**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning Sonar issues JSON extraction and verification**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"I’ll perform the recommended read-only assessment: refresh the Sonar inventory at the current PR head, inspect each affected implementation, deduplicate the 38 findings into real repair families, and identify which are behavior-sensitive versus mechanical. I won’t edit or start another hosted review round during this assessment.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae5b64912087d0ac5ed2c66e126c9d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789811538.195034,"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_QmWBmZSt7xnxwBFXPn1RTIrs","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae5b65ec3087d0b1eed306973b138b","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sonarqube/2.5.0/skills/sonar-list-issues/SKILL.md\\nprintf '\\\\n--- memory lookup ---\\\\n'\\nrg -n \\\"PR #170|MCP170-SONAR|remote-target.ts|host-authorization.ts\\\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -n 30\\nprintf '\\\\n--- current head ---\\\\n'\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit rev-parse HEAD\\ngit status --short\\nprintf '\\\\n--- sonar auth ---\\\\n'\\nsonar auth status\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789811538.195034,"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_QmWBmZSt7xnxwBFXPn1RTIrs","id":"ctco_01a0b915-185c-74b3-8db9-030470e5bdf8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789811562.588687,"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.3 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---\r\nname: sonar-list-issues\r\ndescription: Search and filter SonarQube issues for a project, branch, or pull request via sonarqube-cli (`-p` is always required on the CLI; resolve the key from user arguments or sonar-project.properties)\r\nargument-hint: \"[project-key?] [--severities values] [--statuses values] [--branch name]\"\r\nallowed-tools: Read, Grep, Bash(sonar:*)\r\n---\r\n\r\n# SonarQube — List Issues\r\n\r\nSearch for issues in a SonarQube project using the `sonarqube-cli`.\r\n\r\nUnlike SonarQube MCP tools (which may use a default project from integration), **`sonar list issues` always requires `-p \u003cproject-key\u003e`**. Resolve the key from the user-provided arguments or `sonar-project.properties` before running the CLI.\r\n\r\n## Usage\r\n\r\n```\r\nsonar-list-issues                                          # issues in the current project\r\nsonar-list-issues my-project                               # issues in a specific project key\r\nsonar-list-issues my-project --severities CRITICAL,BLOCKER # filter by severities\r\nsonar-list-issues my-project --statuses OPEN,CONFIRMED     # filter by status\r\nsonar-list-issues my-project --branch main                 # on a specific branch\r\nsonar-list-issues my-project --pr 42                       # on a pull request\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Prerequisites\r\n\r\nThis skill uses the `sonarqube-cli` command. The CLI must be installed and authenticated before proceeding.\r\n\r\n**Before proceeding**, verify that `sonar` is available on your PATH and authenticated. If it is not, do not attempt to call any alternative commands or invent alternatives, and show the user:\r\n\r\n\u003e Unable to list issues.\r\n\u003e\r\n\u003e **Possible causes:**\r\n\u003e - `sonarqube-cli` not installed or not authenticated — invoke the sonar-integrate skill\r\n\u003e - Project key is wrong or missing — `-p` is mandatory for `sonar list issues`; invoke the sonar-list-projects skill or set `sonar.projectKey` in `sonar-project.properties`\r\n\r\nThen ask the user (yes/no) whether to run the sonar-integrate skill now. Briefly explain what it does: it checks the SonarQube setup on their machine — installing or updating `sonarqube-cli` and verifying authentication — and re-configures the integration for this agent, including the SonarQube MCP server and secrets-scanning hooks. If they confirm, invoke the sonar-integrate skill yourself and follow it end-to-end in this session, then re-check and continue; if they decline, stop.\r\n\r\n## Instructions\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Resolve the project key\r\n\r\nThis flow uses **`sonar list issues`** (CLI), not MCP. The CLI **always** needs **`-p \u003cproject-key\u003e`** — do not invoke it without a resolved key.\r\n\r\n- If the user provided a project key, use it.\r\n- Otherwise look for `sonar.projectKey` in `sonar-project.properties` at the repo root.\r\n- If still not found, **do not run** `sonar list issues`. Tell the user: *\"Invoke the sonar-list-projects skill to find your project key, then re-run with that key,\"* or add `sonar.projectKey` to `sonar-project.properties`. (MCP integration defaults do **not** apply to this CLI command.)\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Parse optional flags from the user-provided arguments\r\n\r\n| Flag                     | Maps to CLI option |\r\n| ------------------------ | ------------------ |\r\n| `--severities \u003cvalues\u003e`  | `--severities`     |\r\n| `--statuses \u003cvalues\u003e`    | `--statuses`       |\r\n| `--branch \u003cname\u003e`        | `--branch`         |\r\n| `--pr \u003cid\u003e`              | `--pull-request`   |\r\n\r\n\u003e `sonar list issues` does **not** support filtering by issue type, rule, tag, or component, nor a `--resolved` flag. Only the options above (plus `--format`, `--page`, and `--page-size`) exist. To filter by rule/type/tag/component or to drill into a single file, use the MCP-based skills (e.g. sonar-analyze for a file, or `mcp__sonarqube__search_sonar_issues_in_projects`).\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Validate arguments\r\n\r\nBefore building the command, validate each user-supplied value against the following rules. If any value fails validation, stop and tell the user what was rejected and why — do not run the command. Validate the resolved project key (from args or `sonar-project.properties`) against the project-key pattern before running the CLI.\r\n\r\n| Argument       | Allowed pattern                                                                       |\r\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\r\n| project key    | `^[a-zA-Z0-9_\\-\\.:]+$`                                                                |\r\n| `--severities` | comma-separated subset of: `INFO`, `MINOR`, `MAJOR`, `CRITICAL`, `BLOCKER`, `HIGH`, `MEDIUM`, `LOW` |\r\n| `--statuses`   | comma-separated subset of: `OPEN`, `CONFIRMED`, `FALSE_POSITIVE`, `ACCEPTED`, `FIXED` |\r\n| `--branch`     | `^[a-zA-Z0-9_\\-\\./]+$`                                                                |\r\n| `--pr`         | digits only                                                                           |\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Run `sonar list issues`\r\n\r\nBuild and run the command using a shell command. **Always** pass **`-p`** with the key resolved in Step 1.\r\n\r\n```bash\r\nsonar list issues -p \u003cproject-key\u003e --format toon [--severities \u003cvalues\u003e] [--statuses \u003cvalues\u003e] [--branch \u003cname\u003e] [--pull-request \u003cid\u003e]\r\n```\r\n\r\nOnly include optional flags that were provided.\r\n\r\n### Step 5: Format the results\r\n\r\n**If issues are found**, present a summary line then a table sorted by severity then line number:\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n## SonarQube Issues — `my-project` (branch: `main`)\r\n\r\nFound **12 issue(s)**:\r\n\r\n| File                 | Line | Severity  | Rule         | Message                       |\r\n| -------------------- | ---- | --------- | ------------ | ----------------------------- |\r\n| src/auth/login.py    | 12   | 🔴 Blocker | python:S2077 | SQL injection risk            |\r\n| src/utils/helpers.py | 34   | 🟠 High    | python:S2259 | Null dereference              |\r\n| src/api/routes.py    | 67   | 🟡 Medium  | python:S3776 | Cognitive complexity too high |\r\n```\r\n\r\nSeverity icons (the label depends on the server version):\r\n- 🔴 Blocker\r\n- 🟠 Critical / High\r\n- 🟡 Major / Medium\r\n- 🔵 Minor / Low\r\n- ⚪ Info\r\n\r\n**If no issues are found**:\r\n\r\n```markdown\r\n## SonarQube Issues — `my-project`\r\n\r\n✅ No issues found.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Step 6: Next steps\r\n\r\n- To fix a specific issue: *\"Ask me to fix `\u003crule\u003e` at `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline\u003e`.\"*\r\n- To check the quality gate: *\"Invoke the sonar-quality-gate skill.\"*\r\n\n--- memory lookup ---\n68:- git worktree list --porcelain, gh pr list, GH_CONFIG_DIR, 21nCo/super-functions, 21nCo/superfunctions, docs/http-decode-params, sfns-4-rex-upstream, prunable-worktree, PR #188, PR #179, PR #170\n78:- At the September 17 snapshot, `superfunctions-dev`/`docs/http-decode-params` matched clean PR #188; SFNS-4/`sfns-4-rex-upstream` matched dirty PR #179; `/private/tmp/pr170-refetch-fix.h2yq4m` was missing and prunable, associated with PR #170. [Task 1]\n309:# Task Group: September 15 Superfunctions PR #170 bounded remediation and dirty-dev reconciliation\n310:scope: Use for live hosted `pr-refetch-fix` work on McpFn PR #170, or scoped publication/reconciliation from a dirty, stale Superfunctions dev checkout.\n313:## Task 1: Harden PR #170 redaction/export invariants in a bounded hosted round, success\n323:- PR #170, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gate:mcpfn-release, McpFnRemoteCredentialLease, cleanupPhase, redaction, 8b23aaa, 1e45b794\n337:- when the user invokes `use-skillplane PR #170 pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e retrieve the exact live hosted workflow; make one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, verify the required head once, then stop without refetching