sions: none found by focused client tests, consumer/package checks, complete diff review, or the Node 22 release gate. One initial focused invocation used unsupported Vitest flag `--runInBand` and failed before collection; the configured package command superseded it and passed.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, two pending hosted checks, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `afcf360f7bd5bfc348bdf689a7f2b483c1243190`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 5 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests passed. The new exact-collision regression proves emitted omission timestamps remain canonical ISO and credential-free; the no-safe-ISO regression proves malformed artifacts are dropped while both omission counters advance. These assertions distinguish the prior behavior, which emitted redacted non-ISO values.\n- Typecheck: `@mcpfn/client` passed.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole two-file code/test batch and downstream timestamp consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 5 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 6 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 1 new unresolved GitHub thread, deduplicated to the recurring `MCP170-CLEANUP-PHASE` root cause. The root cause is fixed in the reviewed local batch; the thread was not resolved and review surfaces will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 80 older unresolved threads retain prior current-code evidence because the assessed head is exactly the round-5 pushed head outside this batch. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary still reports 36 open maintainability/reliability findings although its Quality Gate passed. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 6 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. SonarCloud reported a passing Quality Gate and 36 open issues. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 6 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled. The one selected code root is repaired locally, but cleanup-phase attribution recurred and the preserved 36-item analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 1 selected code root cause plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: authenticated-target revocation failures were attributed to transport shutdown in direct diagnostics and suite reports.\n- Recurring families: cleanup-phase classification recurred from the historical pre-ledger repair; the reset-trigger assessment extended the phase contract through target, client, retained-owner, and report boundaries before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first testing run exposed a stale built-client consumer boundary plus changed stable cleanup wording; after rebuilding, an ownership regression showed failed-connect cleanup had replaced the original connection cause. The batch now retains the phase separately while preserving the original cause and established report message. Final focused suites and the release gate found no remaining regression.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `a9fc7f4b1e6b549caf1a5f80498980167429482a` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 6 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests and all 221 `@mcpfn/testing` tests passed. The new assertions distinguish raw transport failure (`transport-close`), OAuth revoke failure (`token-revocation` / `authorization-server`), and disposal after successful revocation (`transport-close` / `mcpfn-preflight`) while proving the cleanup owner remains retryable.\n- Typechecks: `@mcpfn/client` and `@mcpfn/testing` passed; the client package was rebuilt before the consumer suite.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole four-file code/test batch and existing conformance/report phase consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 6 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 7 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 5 new unresolved GitHub threads, deduplicated to 4 root causes. Two threads report the same pre-handle cleanup-phase loss; the other roots are cleanup-error sanitization, structured-Cookie redaction, and target-suite runtime-output classification. All four are fixed in the reviewed local batch; threads remain unresolved and will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 81 older unresolved threads. The round-6 cleanup-phase thread is already repaired at the assessed head; 80 retain prior current-code evidence. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the preserved Sonar maintainability/reliability inventory remains 36 findings under `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY`. The captured Sonar analysis check is successful, but individual analyzer details were not substituted for the GitHub review snapshot or declared fixed.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 7 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. CodeRabbit, Cubic, Pullfrog, CodeQL, SonarCloud Code Analysis, GitGuardian, and the applicable security checks were successful. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 7 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled across scheduled convergence. Four selected roots are repaired locally, but cleanup-phase and credential-