d (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/29/rollout-2026-08-29T20-15-30-01a04dfb-9d6e-7000-a3ce-3006e2ed565c.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-01T15:20:17+00:00, thread_id=01a04dfb-9d6e-7000-a3ce-3006e2ed565c, 31 intended files pushed once; full integration retained unrelated attribution failures and PR-head convergence was unverified)\n2393:- Full integration retained two caller-attribution expectation failures (`Authenticated OAuth client`/`unknown` versus `Codex`/`gpt-5.6`); treat them as outside the SKI-13 delta unless another fix round is authorized. After pushing, local/remote matched `a52f368` but the immediate PR API still showed `cbac7dd`; do not claim PR-head convergence without a permitted later check. [Task 1]\n2529:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #567, PR #568, 69723719, 9c576dc, 41e1efbb, reviewThreads, legacy-product-keys, svelte-check\n3333:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #3, SKI-1, d398cba6, reviewThreads, outdated, repeatable-read, applicationCommit\n3364:- `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` uses unresolved GraphQL threads plus review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments as one snapshot; after an authorized push, verify heads once and do not refetch/poll. An unresolved thread can be historical: inspect `outdated`, current code/commit, and addressed markers. [Task 1]\n3425:- PR #116, SFNS-2, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, reviewThreads, nodeType, undefined versus null, authfn/docs/static/llms-full.txt, MCPFn, UIFn, d27cceb2\n4094:- Green package build/typecheck/test/pack gates are not release proof when browser, IME, clipboard, accessibility, SSR/hydration, corpus, fuzzing, convergence, and migration matrices are absent. [Task 2]\n4519:- when the user invokes `pr-refetch-fix`, check unresolved GraphQL review threads first, then review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments; do not filter by timestamps only. [Task 3]\n"},"started_at_ms":1789819800495,"completed_at_ms":1789819800539}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T12:10:01.000Z","ordinal":5092,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0b992-cda8-75d3-883e-53c9d60aff54","call_id":"call_XA7vsyBJsImND41j04vgQ5P0","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.4 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"---\nname: use-skillplane\ndescription: Use skillplane to resolve skill\n---\n\n# Skillplane\n\nSkillplane is user's hosted skills platform. Retrieve user's skill using the slug or skill name provided and use that to execute the requested task.\n\nNOTE: Always retrieve and read the hosted skill first before working on the task. If the skill is not found, return an error message indicating the skill could not be located.\n\n## Using skillplane\nTry these in this order to communicate with user's Skillplane for skill retrieval, creation, amend etc.\n1. Direct Skillplane MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Direct Skillplane CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n\n## Using tools mentioned in skillplane skills\nWhen executing a skill retrieved from Skillplane, use the tools referenced within the skill's definition to complete the task. Example tools: Linear, Notion, etc. Tool/Integrations resolution order:\n1. Direct integrations or MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Via Orca CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n---\nname: composio-cli\ndescription: Help users operate the published Composio CLI to find the right tool, connect accounts, inspect schemas, execute tools, subscribe to trigger events with `composio listen`, script workflows with `composio run`, and call authenticated app APIs with `composio proxy`. Use when the user asks how to do something with `composio`, wants to run a known tool slug, needs to discover a slug with `composio search`, fix a missing connection with `composio link`, inspect tool inputs with `--get-schema` or `--dry-run`, troubleshoot top-level CLI flows, or explicitly needs `composio dev` guidance.\n---\n\n<!-- AUTO-GENERATED: edit skills-src/composio-cli/index.ts and rebuild -->\n<!-- release-channel: stable -->\n\n# Composio CLI\n\n## Default Workflow\n\n1. Start with `composio execute <slug>` whenever the slug is known.\n2. If several independent tool calls must happen at once, use `composio execute -p/--parallel` with repeated `<slug> -d <json>` groups.\n3. If `execute` says the toolkit is not connected, run `composio link <toolkit>` and retry.\n4. If the arguments are unclear, run `composio execute <slug> --get-schema` or `--dry-run` before guessing.\n5. Reach for `composio search \"<task>\"` only when the slug is unknown. `search` accepts one or more queries, so batch related discovery work into a single command when useful.\n\n## `execute` - Run A Tool\n\nUse `execute` when the tool slug is already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER -d '{}'\n```\n\nInspect required inputs without executing:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --get-schema\n```\n\nPreview safely:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --skip-connection-check --dry-run -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug report\", body: \"Steps to reproduce...\" }'\n```\n\nPass data from a file or stdin:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d @issue.json\ncat issue.json | composio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d -\n```\n\nUpload a local file:\n```bash\ncomposio execute SLACK_UPLOAD_OR_CREATE_A_FILE_IN_SLACK \\\n  --file ./image.png \\\n  -d '{ channels: \"C123\" }'\n```\n\nRun independent tool calls in parallel:\n```bash\ncomposio execute --parallel \\\n  GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL -d '{ recipient_email: \"a@b.com\", subject: \"Hi\" }' \\\n  GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug\" }'\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--get-schema`: Inspect required arguments without executing the tool.\n- `--dry-run`: Preview the request shape without performing the action.\n- `--file`: Inject a local file path into a tool that exposes exactly one uploadable file argument.\n- `--parallel`: Execute multiple independent tool calls in the same invocation.\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id when multiple accounts exist for the same toolkit.\n\n- `--file` only works when the tool exposes a single uploadable file input. Otherwise use explicit `-d` JSON.\n\n## `search` - Find The Slug\n\nUse `search` only when the tool slug is not already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio search \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"send an email\" --toolkits gmail\ncomposio search \"send an email\" \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"my emails\" \"my github issues\" --toolkits gmail,github\n```\n\n- Batch related discovery work into one `search` invocation, then move back to `execute` once the correct slugs are known.\n\n## `link` - Connect An Account\n\nUse `link` when `execute` reports that a toolkit is not connected, or when the user explicitly wants to authorize an account.\n\n```bash\ncomposio link gmail\ncomposio link googlecalendar --no-browser\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--alias`: Assign an alias to the connected account. Required when creating an additional account for the same toolkit.\n\n- Retry the original `execute` command after linking succeeds.\n\n## `proxy` - Raw API Access\n\nUse `proxy` when a toolkit supports a raw API operation that is easier than finding a dedicated tool slug.\n\n```bash\ncomposio proxy https://api.github.com/user --toolkit github --account work --method GET </dev/null\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id.\n\n## `run` - Scripting, LLMs, and Programmatic Workflows\n\nFor programmatic calls, loops, output plumbing, or anything beyond a single tool call, prefer `composio run`.\n\n`composio run` executes an inline ESM JavaScript/TypeScript snippet with authenticated `execute()`, `search()`, `proxy()`, and the experimental `experimental_subAgent()` helper pre-injected. No SDK setup required.\n\nChain multiple tools:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const me = await execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\");\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 1 });\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, fetchedEmails: !!emails.data });\n'\n```\n\nFan out with Promise.all:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const [me, emails] = await Promise.all([\n    execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\"),\n    execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 }),\n  ]);\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, emailCount: emails.data.messages?.length });\n'\n```\n\nFeed tool output into an LLM and get structured JSON back:\n```bash\ncomposio run --logs-off '\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 });\n  const brief = await experimental_subAgent(\n    `Summarize these emails and count them.\\n\\n${emails.prompt()}`,\n    { schema: z.object({ summary: z.string(), count: z.number() }) }\n  );\n  console.log(brief.structuredOutput);\n'\n```\n\n- Use top-level `execute --parallel` instead when the user only needs a few independent tool calls and does not need script logic.\n\n## Auth\n\n```bash\ncomposio whoami   # check current session\ncomposio login    # authenticate if whoami fails\n```\n\n## Escalate Only When Needed\n\nIf the user is stuck on top-level commands or needs fallback inspection commands, load [references/troubleshooting.md](references/troubleshooting.md).\n\nIf the user explicitly asks about developer projects, auth configs, connected accounts, triggers, logs, orgs, or projects, load [references/composio-dev.md](references/composio-dev.md). `composio dev` is not the default end-user path.\n---\nname: using-21nco-github\ndescription: Uses the thyaravind GitHub account for repositories under dev/n or with a 21nCo origin. Use for any GitHub CLI, pull request, issue, release, or repository operation in those repositories.\n---\n\n# Using 21nCo GitHub\n\nFor any repository whose path is under `dev/n` or whose Git remote belongs to `21nCo`:\n\n- Run every `gh` command with `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`.\n- Verify `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh auth status` reports `thyaravind` as the active account before making changes.\n- Never use the default GitHub account or silently fall back to another account.\n- Use the existing `21nCo` remote for pushes. If `thyaravind` lacks permission, stop and report the denial instead of creating a fork or alternate remote.\n---\nname: orca-cli\ndescription: >-\n  Operate Orca-managed worktrees, folder contexts, terminals, repos, automations, artifacts,\n  skill sharing, worktree comments, and Orca's embedded browser through the `orca` CLI. Use\n  when the user says \"$orca-cli\", \"Orca worktree\", \"child worktree\", \"spawn codex/claude in a\n  worktree\", \"read/wait/send Orca terminal\", \"handoff\" / \"handover\" / \"give this to another\n  agent\", \"Orca browser\", \"orca artifacts\", or \"share skills\". Prefer it over raw git\n  worktree, ad hoc PTYs, or Computer Use when Orca state is involved. Use Computer Use only\n  for external windows or desktop UI that needs OS-level control, and Playwright or CDP for\n  external pages.\n---\n\n# Orca CLI\n\nThis discovery stub loads the version-matched guide from the Orca executable used for this session.\n\n## Resolve the CLI for this session\n\nChoose the executable once and reuse it for every later command:\n\n- If the `ORCA_CLI_COMMAND` environment variable is set, use its value. Orca exports this\n  for managed WSL sessions.\n- Otherwise, in a dev checkout whose session exposes `ORCA_DEV_REPO_ROOT`, use `orca-dev`.\n- Otherwise, on Linux outside an Orca-managed terminal, use `orca-ide`. Never run bare\n  `orca` there — outside Orca's terminals it normally resolves to the\n  GNOME Orca screen reader (`/usr/bin/orca`) and starts speech on the user's machine.\n- Otherwise, use `orca`.\n\nBelow, `ORCA` is a placeholder for the executable you resolved. Substitute it before\nrunning anything; do not create a shell variable or run `ORCA` literally. This works the\nsame way in POSIX shells, PowerShell, and cmd.exe.\n\nIf the selected executable cannot run, report its exact error and stop. Do not fall through\nto another executable, which could silently target a different Orca build.\n\n## Load the version-matched guide before running Orca commands\n\n```text\nORCA skills get orca-cli\n```\n\nPrefer `--json`. Use the selected executable's `--help` for commands or flags the guide does\nnot cover. If a command reports that Orca is not running, start it with `ORCA open --json`\nand retry. If `skills get` is unknown, explain that updating Orca restores the guide; use\n`--help` for read-only discovery and do not guess unsupported commands.\n68:- git worktree list --porcelain, gh pr list, GH_CONFIG_DIR, 21nCo/super-functions, 21nCo/superfunctions, docs/http-decode-params, sfns-4-rex-upstream, prunable-worktree, PR #188, PR #179, PR #170\n78:- At the September 17 snapshot, `superfunctions-dev`/`docs/http-decode-params` matched clean PR #188; SFNS-4/`sfns-4-rex-upstream` matched dirty PR #179; `/private/tmp/pr170-refetch-fix.h2yq4m` was missing and prunable, associated with PR #170. [Task 1]\n221:- SFNS-4, PR #179, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, adapterNamespace, Retry-After, tenantId, SecFn readJson, e200f50d, 511c02a\n309:# Task Group: September 15 Superfunctions PR #170 bounded remediation and dirty-dev reconciliation\n310:scope: Use for live hosted `pr-refetch-fix` work on McpFn PR #170, or scoped publication/reconciliation from a dirty, stale Superfunctions dev checkout.\n313:## Task 1: Harden PR #170 redaction/export invariants in a bounded hosted round, success\n323:- PR #170, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gate:mcpfn-release, McpFnRemoteCredentialLease, cleanupPhase, redaction, 8b23aaa, 1e45b794\n337:- when the user invokes `use-skillplane PR #170 pr-refetch-fix` -> retrieve the exact live hosted workflow; make one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, verify the required head once, then stop without refetching reviews, polling CI, or merging. [Task 1]\n343:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub commands. Page every GraphQL `reviewThreads` cursor: PR #170’s first 100-thread result had `hasNextPage: true`; only a three-page, 284-thread snapshot was complete. [Task 1]\n605:- PR #23, reviewThreads(first:100), GH_CONFIG_DIR, pr-refetch-fix, projections, SQLite writer lock, SkillEditor, CompositionPanel, SonarQube, 7715373, 83058246\n778:- splane, PR #174, PR #177, SFNS-3, OAuth invalid_grant, Composio CLI, 21n/pr-quality-analysis@1.0.0, pr-refetch-fix, gate:reviewfn-release, package-lock, d4de3791\n1043:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-10T14-20-26-2rMD-mcp1_transport_neutral_conformance_pr_170.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/10/rollout-2026-09-10T19-50-26-01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-10T15:17:36+00:00, thread_id=01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3, PR #170 local release gate passed; hosted CI queued)\n1544:# Task Group: September 8-15 Superfunctions PR quality, issue-grounded review, and PR #170 convergence\n1545:scope: Use for broad read-only Superfunctions PR review, comparative findings/quality reporting, follow-up assessment, and root-cause remediation convergence across DataFn/UIFN/McpFn.\n1548:## Task 1: Review requested live Superfunctions PRs and diagnose remediation convergence, partial\n1552:- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/07/rollout-2026-09-07T14-45-31-01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T07:43:24+0