ngs already repaired at the assessed head; 76 retain earlier current-code evidence. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary increased from 35 to 36 open maintainability/reliability findings while its Quality Gate remained passing. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 4 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful (including the successful CodeRabbit status context), 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. SonarCloud reported a passing Quality Gate and 36 open issues. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 4 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled across scheduled convergence despite a decreasing within-round code count. Both selected roots are repaired locally, but credential-boundary and cleanup-ownership siblings recurred again and the deferred analyzer inventory grew from 35 to 36.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 2 selected code root causes plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: direct structural credential collisions in client/inspector artifacts and lost post-connect test-client cleanup ownership; the Sonar summary also reports one additional unclassified issue.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary and cleanup-ownership defects both recurred; the reset-trigger subsystem assessment expanded enforcement to their shared producers before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first full gate exposed lost post-close omission evidence, which was repaired with shared omission counters; the next gate exposed a mixed