e bounded reports; inspector produces snapshots/scenarios; `runCli` persists operator artifacts; `bin.ts` is the only process-termination boundary. Hosted OAuth servers produce redirect/token responses consumed by the authorization regression runner. GitHub Actions consumes Python bootstrap packages.\n- Failure boundaries: acquisition/open/initialize, inventory and scenario execution, payload/envelope projection, report serialization, protocol/transport/revoke/dispose cleanup, programmatic CLI error propagation, executable stderr flush/termination, authorization-code exchange and refresh, and package bootstrap in CI.\n- Coordinated repair: (1) persist any bounded cleanup-failure artifact, then propagate the owning cleanup error through `runCli`; retry once at the outer library boundary; only `bin.ts` prints a safe diagnostic and terminates for all three retryable cleanup error types; (2) force payload mode for every inspector snapshot projection; (3) validate JSON plus `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Pragma: no-cache` for both exchange and refresh success responses; (4) correct misleading/incorrect regressions; (5) require binary Python distributions in the failing Sonar-gated bootstrap step. These are complete enforcement points for the current findings rather than commented-line patches.\n- Verification criteria: focused old-behavior regressions for cleanup retry failure/success, executable diagnostics, snapshot payload collisions, key-only scenario redaction, exchange/refresh cache headers, and misleading retry configuration; then package typechecks/tests, the Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and one pre-push review of the complete diff. Hosted checks are observed only in the already captured immediate snapshot.\n\n### Round 2 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: consumer-visible reports and snapshots must never silently remain passing when credential safety or evidence completeness cannot be guaranteed, and executable termination must not discard already-produced evidence.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated target credential leases and the shared scrubber produce protected values and redacted artifacts; target suites, inspector and JUnit consume authored structural envelopes; official conformance produces captured streams; `serializeBoundedReport` persists bounded operator evidence; `runCli` writes reports; `bin.ts` owns forced process termination.\n- Failure boundaries: credential text colliding with authored discriminator values, final JSON recomposition, report-size truncation, asynchronous stdout backpressure, cleanup-owner propagation into the executable boundary, and non-enumerable `Error.message` / `cause` fields in regressions.\n- Coordinated repair: reject structural-value credential collisions at the shared scrubber while preserving authored schema keys; mark truncated conformance reports explicitly incomplete and force exit 1; await every CLI output write before cleanup retry/termination; assert cleanup error messages and reports separately.\n- Verification criteria: collision regressions for `pass` / `complete` and existing structural values; a successful oversized conformance result must persist as bounded `status: incomplete`, `ok: false`, exit 1; delayed stdout must complete before cleanup retry can reject; the cleanup assertion must inspect both message and report. Then package typechecks/tests, the full Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 3 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: a retryable cleanup failure must retain the exact resource owner until cleanup succeeds; later output, diagnostic, persistence, or sanitization failures may be reported only after ownership is safely preserved. Public-client OAuth fixtures must distinguish protocol-compliant token rejection from an authentication challenge.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated conformance and target suites produce retryable cleanup-owner errors; `McpFnInspector` owns an `McpFnClient` transport; command actions produce stdout/stderr and persisted reports; `runCli` performs the one bounded retry; `bin.ts` is the final process boundary. Hosted authorization fixtures produce token exchange/refresh rejections consumed by the conformance runner.\n- Failure boundaries: inspector close after operation/output failure, conformance and target-suite output/report persistence after a cleanup artifact exists, outer diagnostic output before cleanup classification/retry, and unauthenticated token exchange/refresh status validation.\n- Coordinated repair: add a retryable inspector cleanup owner; prioritize every cleanup owner over secondary output/persistence/diagnostic failures; preserve that owner through the bounded retry and executable termination boundary; and require HTTP 400 for `invalid_client` from public-client exchange and refresh fixtures.\n- Verification criteria: regressions must show inspector ownership survives two failed closes and can later be retried; conformance and target-suite owners must survive rejecting output callbacks and report persistence failures; executable classification must include inspector cleanup; public-client `invalid_client` must pass at 400 and fail at 401 for exchange and refresh. Then focused tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 4 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: while a target owns credentials, every consumer-visible dynamic value—including reconstructed schema discriminators—must be proven safe by that target's redactor before emission; if mandatory structure collides with a credential, the producer must fail closed or emit a safe marked omission. Any abstraction that exposes `close()` must retain and return the retry owner when close fails.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient` produces diagnostic and client-event envelopes consumed directly and by `McpFnInspector`; the inspector reconstructs snapshot, timeline, and scenario discriminators for library and CLI consumers. `McpFnTestClient` owns a production `McpFnClient`; direct callers and `mcpfn test` consume its public `close()` contract, while `runCli` already recognizes `McpFnTestClientCleanupError`.\n- Failure boundaries: structural values reconstructed after payload redaction (`kind`, `phase`, `outcome`, `clientState`, target kind, scenario side effect), safe omission fallback construction, inspector snapshot/export assembly, and post-connect local test-client shutdown after successful operations.\n- Coordinated repair: add one client structural-safety enforcement point backed by the active target redactor; apply it to event/diagnostic discriminators and all inspector-reconstructed discriminators, dropping only when no schema-safe omission can be produced; then make `McpFnTestClient.close()` wrap retained session cleanup in the existing retryable owner while preserving connection-failure cause semantics during failed connect cleanup.\n- Verification criteria: credentials equal to client/inspector structural values must never appear in direct events, timelines, snapshots, or scenario exports; safe omissions must be marked and counted, and mandatory snapshot/export collisions must reject with credential-free errors. A post-connect test-client close must retain the same owner through repeated failure and later succeed, and the CLI's existing owner retry boundary must remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 5 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every emitted event or diagnostic must preserve both credential confidentiality and schema semantics. A timestamp may be emitted only as the exact generated ISO string after the active target redactor proves that string safe; redaction must never turn it into a malformed timestamp.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient.emitEvent()` and diagnostic disp