inator values actually present, after the live target redactor proves each exact structural string safe; a projection failure must never bypass transport cleanup, and analyzer-only refactors must preserve credential, lifecycle, ownership, and report semantics.\n- Producers / consumers: inspector scenario export produces replay artifacts; custom targets and live client sessions produce redacted descriptors, diagnostics, scenario results, and cleanup ownership; target-suite projection/finalization produces direct-library, JSON, JUnit, and CLI evidence; shared credential scrubbers and report normalizers feed all of those consumers.\n- Failure boundaries: optional scenario fields that are validated but never emitted; custom redaction state cleared during close; fallback descriptor access through hostile getters after connect; structural collisions in fixed report keys/discriminators; base64 comparison, recursive scrub traversal, failure normalization, and suite orchestration maintainability changes.\n- Coordinated repair: validate optional inspector keys only at their emission branches; capture live custom-redactor safety for exact report structural candidates before close and enforce it against only the final emitted structure; place projection and structural capture behind unconditional cleanup; then repair the eight valid current Sonar findings without changing observable contracts. A structural collision must retain any cleanup owner and reject safely when no typed report exists.\n- Verification criteria: focused inspector optional-key regressions, pre-connect and connected custom-redactor structural collisions, hostile post-connect getter cleanup, report/redaction regressions, affected package tests/typechecks/builds, complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, whole-batch diff review, and one final local/remote/PR-head comparison. Hosted review/check surfaces will not be fetched again this round.\n\n### Round 11 defect families\n\n| Stable family | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Repair / evidence state |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` | Inspector rejects a safe complete export when a credential equals an optional key that is not emitted | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Exact-emission key validation selected |\n| `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` | A custom target that fails before connection can bypass its live redactor, exposing a credential through fixed report structure | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Pre-close structure-safety snapshot and final exact-structure enforcement selected |\n| `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` | A hostile target getter can throw during post-connect fallback construction before the sole close call | Actionable; recurring cleanup-ownership family | round 11 | round 11 | Unconditional projection-finalization cleanup selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-BASE64-PADDING` | Two trailing-padding regex operations have avoidable super-linear analysis risk | Valid analyzer finding | round 11 | round 11 | Deterministic padding-strip helper selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REDACTION-DECOMPOSITION` | Credential scrub traversal remains too complex and contains a nested role-selection conditional and escaped replacement literal | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Role selection and traversal helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-SUITE-ORCHESTRATION` | Cleanup retry callback and suite orchestration exceed analyzer maintainability limits | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Identity-preserving throw callback and report-building helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REPORT-NORMALIZATION` | Failure normalization exceeds analyzer complexity threshold | Valid analyzer finding; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Cause/detail extraction helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-THROW-THIS` | Three remaining findings flag intentional retry-owner self-rejection | Invalid analyzer interpretation | round 10 | round 11 | Owner identity is the cleanup token; preserve behavior |\n\n- Snapshot dispositions before mutation: 3 new unresolved current review threads deduplicate to 3 actionable root causes. Eleven current Sonar issues deduplicate to 8 valid maintainability roots and 3 invalid retry-owner findings. Older valid blockers and dispositions remain preserved; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Required-check snapshot: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. Sonar Quality Gate passed; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n- Convergence state: not clean. Three new/actionable roots, recurring structural-safety and cleanup families, and eight valid analyzer findings reset the clean streak to `0` despite the stable snapshot head and terminal successful required checks.\n\n## Round 11 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Fixed: 3 new review root causes and 8 valid Sonar findings. `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` now validates `status`, `incompleteReason`, and `variables` only in the branches that emit them. `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` snapshots exact fixed-string safety through the live target scrubber and validates only the final emitted report structure, including bounded/fallback changes. `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` places projection and structure capture behind unconditional close and safely handles a hostile post-connect `kind` getter. The valid analyzer set received deterministic Base64 padding removal, `String.raw` replacement syntax, role/traversal helpers, an identity-preserving throw callback, suite orchestration decomposition, and report-normalization helpers.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 90 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions (30 current and 60 outdated-only at this snapshot). Outdated status and one new Pullfrog acknowledgement comment were not counted as proof or new root causes.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 remaining Sonar `throw this` findings are invalid because the exact error object is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots and all 8 valid current Sonar findings were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer/analyzer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\n\n## Round 11 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 36 target-suite tests, 88 remote-target tests, 10 report tests, and 31 authenticated-inspector artifact tests passed (165 total). New cases prove absent optional scenario keys do not overreject, pre-connect custom fallback structure fails closed, a post-connect hostile kind getter cannot bypass close, and fixed report-key/discriminator collisions reject safely.\n- Complete affected-package suites: all 236 `@mcpfn/testing` tests and all 41 `@mcpfn/inspector` tests passed. Both packages typechecked and built successfully under Node 22.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported package test/typecheck/build, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check` and the staged diff check passed. Whole-batch review covered all six code/test files. The ledger remained untracked and was never staged or committed.\n- Required-check snapshot: the single pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate passed while its current issue inventory contained 8 valid and 3 invalid findings. No hosted checks or reviews were fetched after push.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first focused run exposed four stale expectations that permitted unproven fixed structure; those regressions now assert safe rejection. The broader remote-target run exposed that a safely projected dynamic-kind fallback was being rejected before fallback selection; finalization now validates the actually emitted fallback, preserving the established incomplete-report contract. All subsequent focused, complete-package, and release-gate runs passed.\n- Commit / push: one commit `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` (`fix(mcpfn): close structural report gaps`) and one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`.\n- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, remote branch head, and PR 170 head all equal `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remained open.\n\n## Round 11 convergence\n\n- Trend: improving but nonterminal. All selected review and valid analyzer roots have coordinated repairs, no local regression remains, and the immediate check snapshot was terminal/successful; hosted reviewers and analyzers have not yet assessed the new head.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots plus 8 valid analyzer findings / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted verification pending a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- New defects: optional-key overvalidation, custom-redactor fallback structure bypass, and projection-before-cleanup escape.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and cleanup ownership recurred; both were repaired at shared producer/finalization boundaries rather than at individual symptom sites.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed PR head after assessment).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1` during assessment; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 11 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled clean assessment must verify the new head, review dispositions, and analyzer/check state; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\n- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61","create_time":1789980008.971871}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
