utput);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821263.368785,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_40rYb37IVWacf8QmrSwwiRL7","id":"ctco_01a0b9a9-3d46-7ee2-bf86-e7c9d596770d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821271.366387,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.6 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"local=9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\nremote=9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\npr={\"headRefName\":\"codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910\",\"headRefOid\":\"9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\",\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170\"}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae8158e13487d09a78ed5df9f9c5ed","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"summary":[{"text":"**Updating ledger with final dispositions**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Detailing bucket resolutions and test updates**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Applying attempted repairs patch**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The single push succeeded. The one allowed comparison now shows local, origin, and PR head all at `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remains open. I’m recording the round result in the untracked ledger and then stopping—no review refetch or CI poll will follow.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae8161338487d0a26eef216ccf8206","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789821272.016449,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_RR0faupLUWUaN32h461RVakm","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae816240c887d09e7d5a4288dd0987","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170/.conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\\n@@\\n - Convergence state: not clean. Three new/actionable roots, recurring structural-safety and cleanup families, and eight valid analyzer findings reset the clean streak to `0` despite the stable snapshot head and terminal successful required checks.\\n+\\n+## Round 11 snapshot dispositions\\n+\\n+- Fixed: 3 new review root causes and 8 valid Sonar findings. `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` now validates `status`, `incompleteReason`, and `variables` only in the branches that emit them. `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` snapshots exact fixed-string safety through the live target scrubber and validates only the final emitted report structure, including bounded/fallback changes. `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` places projection and structure capture behind unconditional close and safely handles a hostile post-connect `kind` getter. The valid analyzer set received deterministic Base64 padding removal, `String.raw` replacement syntax, role/traversal helpers, an identity-preserving throw callback, suite orchestration decomposition, and report-normalization helpers.\\n+- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 90 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions (30 current and 60 outdated-only at this snapshot). Outdated status and one new Pullfrog acknowledgement comment were not counted as proof or new root causes.\\n+- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 remaining Sonar `throw this` findings are invalid because the exact error object is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\\n+- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots and all 8 valid current Sonar findings were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer/analyzer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\\n+- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\\n+\\n+## Round 11 verification evidence\\n+\\n+- Focused regressions: 36 target-suite tests, 88 remote-target tests, 10 report tests, and 31 authenticated-inspector artifact tests passed (165 total). New cases prove absent optional scenario keys do not overreject, pre-connect custom fallback structure fails closed, a post-connect hostile kind getter cannot bypass close, and fixed report-key/discriminator collisions reject safely.\\n+- Complete affected-package suites: all 236 `@mcpfn/testing` tests and all 41 `@mcpfn/inspector` tests passed. Both packages typechecked and built successfully under Node 22.\\n+- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported package test/typecheck/build, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\\n+- `git diff --check` and the staged diff check passed. Whole-batch review covered all six code/test files. The ledger remained untracked and was never staged or committed.\\n+- Required-check snapshot: the single pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate passed while its current issue inventory contained 8 valid and 3 invalid findings. No hosted checks or reviews were fetched after push.\\n+- Fix-induced regressions: the first focused run exposed four stale expectations that permitted unproven fixed structure; those regressions now assert safe rejection. The broader remote-target run exposed that a safely projected dynamic-kind fallback was being rejected before fallback selection; finalization now validates the actually emitted fallback, preserving the established incomplete-report contract. All subsequent focused, complete-package, and release-gate runs passed.\\n+- Commit / push: one commit `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` (`fix(mcpfn): close structural report gaps`) and one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`.\\n+- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, remote branch head, and PR 170 head all equal `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remained open.\\n+\\n+## Round 11 convergence\\n+\\n+- Trend: improving but nonterminal. All selected review and valid analyzer roots have coordinated repairs, no local regression remains, and the immediate check snapshot was terminal/successful; hosted reviewers and analyzers have not yet assessed the new head.\\n+- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots plus 8 valid analyzer findings / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted verification pending a future separately scheduled assessment.\\n+- New defects: optional-key overvalidation, custom-redactor fallback structure bypass, and projection-before-cleanup escape.\\n+- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and cleanup ownership recurred; both were repaired at shared producer/finalization boundaries rather than at individual symptom sites.\\n+- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed PR head after assessment).\\n+- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1` during assessment; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` in the one allowed comparison.\\n+\\n+## Round 11 termination\\n+\\n+- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled clean assessment must verify the new head, review dispositions, and analyzer/check state; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\\n+- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\\n+- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821272.016449,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_RR0faupLUWUaN32h461RVakm","id":"ctco_01a0b9a9-cf23-7711-85ad-90526fa7a729","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821308.707304,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{}","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae817f884087d088f6ecfed0241385","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"summary":[{"text":"**Verifying local git status post-ledger update**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_lj0flEwwOpBltTefeFBIZ0N2","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae818203ac87d091e032d786ee56cc","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"git status --short \u0026\u0026 if git diff --cached --quiet; then echo 'index=clean'; else echo 'index=dirty'; fi \u0026\u0026 tail -n 55 .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":15000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821309.571008,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_lj0flEwwOpBltTefeFBIZ0N2","id":"ctco_01a0b9a9-eb47-72e1-850b-4db98d516fba","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789821315.911216,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.7 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"?? .conduct/\nindex=clean\n- Producers / consumers: inspector scenario export produces replay artifacts; custom targets and live client sessions produce redacted descriptors, diagnostics, scenario results, and cleanup ownership; target-suite projection/finalization produces direct-library, JSON, JUnit, and CLI evidence; shared credential scrubbers and report normalizers feed all of those consumers.\n- Failure boundaries: optional scenario fields that are validated but never emitted; custom redaction state cleared during close; fallback descriptor access through hostile getters after connect; structural collisions in fixed report keys/discriminators; base64 comparison, recursive scrub traversal, failure normalization, and suite orchestration maintainability changes.\n- Coordinated repair: validate optional inspector keys only at their emission branches; capture live custom-redactor safety for exact report structural candidates before close and enforce it against only the final emitted structure; place projection and structural capture behind unconditional cleanup; then repair the eight valid current Sonar findings without changing observable contracts. A structural collision must retain any cleanup owner and reject safely when no typed report exists.\n- Verification criteria: focused inspector optional-key regressions, pre-connect and connected custom-redactor structural collisions, hostile post-connect getter cleanup, report/redaction regressions, affected package tests/typechecks/builds, complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, whole-batch diff review, and one final local/remote/PR-head comparison. Hosted review/check surfaces will not be fetched again this round.\n\n### Round 11 defect families\n\n| Stable family | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Repair / evidence state |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` | Inspector rejects a safe complete export when a credential equals an optional key that is not emitted | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Exact-emission key validation selected |\n| `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` | A custom target that fails before connection can bypass its live redactor, exposing a credential through fixed report structure | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Pre-close structure-safety snapshot and final exact-structure enforcement selected |\n| `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` | A hostile target getter can throw during post-connect fallback construction before the sole close call | Actionable; recurring cleanup-ownership family | round 11 | round 11 | Unconditional projection-finalization cleanup selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-BASE64-PADDING` | Two trailing-padding regex operations have avoidable super-linear analysis risk | Valid analyzer finding | round 11 | round 11 | Deterministic padding-strip helper selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REDACTION-DECOMPOSITION` | Credential scrub traversal remains too complex and contains a nested role-selection conditional and escaped replacement literal | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Role selection and traversal helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-SUITE-ORCHESTRATION` | Cleanup retry callback and suite orchestration exceed analyzer maintainability limits | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Identity-preserving throw callback and report-building helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REPORT-NORMALIZATION` | Failure normalization exceeds analyzer complexity threshold | Valid analyzer finding; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Cause/detail extraction helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-THROW-THIS` | Three remaining findings flag intentional retry-owner self-rejection | Invalid analyzer interpretation | round 10 | round 11 | Owner identity is the cleanup token; preserve behavior |\n\n- Snapshot dispositions before mutation: 3 new unresolved current review threads deduplicate to 3 actionable root causes. Eleven current Sonar issues deduplicate to 8 valid maintainability roots and 3 invalid retry-owner findings. Older valid blockers and dispositions remain preserved; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Required-check snapshot: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. Sonar Quality Gate passed; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n- Convergence state: not clean. Three new/actionable roots, recurring structural-safety and cleanup families, and eight valid analyzer findings reset the clean streak to `0` despite the stable snapshot head and terminal successful required checks.\n\n## Round 11 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Fixed: 3 new review root causes and 8 valid Sonar findings. `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` now validates `status`, `incompleteReason`, and `variables` only in the branches that emit them. `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` snapshots exact fixed-string safety through the live target scrubber and validates only the final emitted report structure, including bounded/fallback changes. `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` places projection and structure capture behind unconditional close and safely handles a hostile post-connect `kind` getter. The valid analyzer set received deterministic Base64 padding removal, `String.raw` replacement syntax, role/traversal helpers, an identity-preserving throw callback, suite orchestration decomposition, and report-normalization helpers.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 90 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions (30 current and 60 outdated-only at this snapshot). Outdated status and one new Pullfrog acknowledgement comment were not counted as proof or new root causes.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 remaining Sonar `throw this` findings are invalid because the exact error object is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots and all 8 valid current Sonar findings were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer/analyzer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\n\n## Round 11 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 36 target-suite tests, 88 remote-target tests, 10 report tests, and 31 authenticated-inspector artifact tests passed (165 total). New cases prove absent optional scenario keys do not overreject, pre-connect custom fallback structure fails closed, a post-connect hostile kind getter cannot bypass close, and fixed report-key/discriminator collisions reject safely.\n- Complete affected-package suites: all 236 `@mcpfn/testing` tests and all 41 `@mcpfn/inspector` tests passed. Both packages typechecked and built successfully under Node 22.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported package test/typecheck/build, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check` and the staged diff check passed. Whole-batch review covered all six code/test files. The ledger remained untracked and was never staged or committed.\n- Required-check snapshot: the single pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate passed while its current issue inventory contained 8 valid and 3 invalid findings. No hosted checks or reviews were fetched after push.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first focused run exposed four stale expectations that permitted unproven fixed structure; those regressions now assert safe rejection. The broader remote-target run exposed that a safely projected dynamic-kind fallback was being rejected before fallback selection; finalization now validates the actually emitted fallback, preserving the established incomplete-report contract. All subsequent focused, complete-package, and release-gate runs passed.\n- Commit / push: one commit `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` (`fix(mcpfn): close structural report gaps`) and one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`.\n- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, remote branch head, and PR 170 head all equal `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remained open.\n\n## Round 11 convergence\n\n- Trend: improving but nonterminal. All selected review and valid analyzer roots have coordinated repairs, no local regression remains, and the immediate check snapshot was terminal/successful; hosted reviewers and analyzers have not yet assessed the new head.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots plus 8 valid analyzer findings / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted verification pending a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- New defects: optional-key overvalidation, custom-redactor fallback structure bypass, and projection-before-cleanup escape.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and cleanup ownership recurred; both were repaired at shared producer/finalization boundaries rather than at individual symptom sites.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed PR head after assessment).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1` during assessment; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 11 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled clean assessment must verify the new head, review dispositions, and analyzer/check state; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\n- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae81859ea087d08f4fe959825ed25f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"summary":[{"text":"**Finalizing result buckets and automation status**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Composing detailed final report with memory citation**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Confirming no hosted check after push**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"Round 11 completed and pushed as commit `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`.\n\n- **Fixed:** 3 review root causes and 8 valid Sonar findings.\n- **Skipped (Already Fixed/Stale):** 90 older unresolved threads retained their prior dispositions.\n- **Not Attempted:** 0 valid code root causes.\n- **Invalid / Accepted Risk:** 3 intentional cleanup-owner `throw this` findings and one older payload-key finding remain invalid; no accepted risks.\n- **Unassessed:** 0.\n- **Verification:** 165 focused tests, 236 complete testing-package tests, 41 inspector tests, typechecks, builds, and the complete Node 22 release gate passed.\n- **Heads:** local, origin, and PR head all match `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remains open.\n- **Convergence:** nonterminal, clean streak `0` because this round changed the head. Automation remains disabled and was not silently re-enabled.\n\nThe untracked ledger was updated at [.conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md](/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170/.conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md).\n\nOne review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\n\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:309-347|note=[PR 170 bounded workflow and live verification requirements]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n01a0a522-f7e4-7422-a0fb-002c30b622cc\n01a0aa3d-a004-7df3-b812-85a576729d2d\n01a0a496-fa30-7f70-ac42-1e7080963f28\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aae818de5fc87d0b7136004d1cf62fb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789821316.642011,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c31e-70a0-7731-96e3-a8145486556a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789979947.168672,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed304fcc87d09e45ad06360eaa26","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning skill retrieval and inspection**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Confirming pr-refetch-fix skill version**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"I’m using the Skillplane resolver to fetch the live `pr-refetch-fix` workflow before touching PR 170. I’ll follow its current boundaries exactly; repository or automation changes will wait until the hosted identity and instructions are verified.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed35397887d08a2909fd220dd7f5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789979949.389087,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_rC70buy3a2yY0B6A1IG5wXLJ","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed36f2cc87d08b15c45b26f0f7ef","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/skillplane/SKILL.md\\nsed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/composio-cli/SKILL.md\\nsed -n '1,260p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/using-21nco-github/SKILL.md\\nrg -n \\\"PR #170|pr-refetch-fix|convergence|remediation ledger|Skillplane\\\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -n 80\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979949.389087,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_rC70buy3a2yY0B6A1IG5wXLJ","id":"ctco_01a0c31e-aded-7d30-adcb-a9fe2c0c074b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979962.861042,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.8 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---\nname: use-skillplane\ndescription: Use skillplane to resolve skill\n---\n\n# Skillplane\n\nSkillplane is user's hosted skills platform. Retrieve user's skill using the slug or skill name provided and use that to execute the requested task.\n\nNOTE: Always retrieve and read the hosted skill first before working on the task. If the skill is not found, return an error message indicating the skill could not be located.\n\n## Using skillplane\nTry these in this order to communicate with user's Skillplane for skill retrieval, creation, amend etc.\n1. Direct Skillplane MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Direct Skillplane CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n\n## Using tools mentioned in skillplane skills\nWhen executing a skill retrieved from Skillplane, use the tools referenced within the skill's definition to complete the task. Example tools: Linear, Notion, etc. Tool/Integrations resolution order:\n1. Direct integrations or MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Via Orca CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n---\nname: composio-cli\ndescription: Help users operate the published Composio CLI to find the right tool, connect accounts, inspect schemas, execute tools, subscribe to trigger events with `composio listen`, script workflows with `composio run`, and call authenticated app APIs with `composio proxy`. Use when the user asks how to do something with `composio`, wants to run a known tool slug, needs to discover a slug with `composio search`, fix a missing connection with `composio link`, inspect tool inputs with `--get-schema` or `--dry-run`, troubleshoot top-level CLI flows, or explicitly needs `composio dev` guidance.\n---\n\n\u003c!-- AUTO-GENERATED: edit skills-src/composio-cli/index.ts and rebuild --\u003e\n\u003c!-- release-channel: stable --\u003e\n\n# Composio CLI\n\n## Default Workflow\n\n1. Start with `composio execute \u003cslug\u003e` whenever the slug is known.\n2. If several independent tool calls must happen at once, use `composio execute -p/--parallel` with repeated `\u003cslug\u003e -d \u003cjson\u003e` groups.\n3. If `execute` says the toolkit is not connected, run `composio link \u003ctoolkit\u003e` and retry.\n4. If the arguments are unclear, run `composio execute \u003cslug\u003e --get-schema` or `--dry-run` before guessing.\n5. Reach for `composio search \"\u003ctask\u003e\"` only when the slug is unknown. `search` accepts one or more queries, so batch related discovery work into a single command when useful.\n\n## `execute` - Run A Tool\n\nUse `execute` when the tool slug is already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER -d '{}'\n```\n\nInspect required inputs without executing:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --get-schema\n```\n\nPreview safely:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --skip-connection-check --dry-run -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug report\", body: \"Steps to reproduce...\" }'\n```\n\nPass data from a file or stdin:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d @issue.json\ncat issue.json | composio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d -\n```\n\nUpload a local file:\n```bash\ncomposio execute SLACK_UPLOAD_OR_CREATE_A_FILE_IN_SLACK \\\n  --file ./image.png \\\n  -d '{ channels: \"C123\" }'\n```\n\nRun independent tool calls in parallel:\n```bash\ncomposio execute --parallel \\\n  GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL -d '{ recipient_email: \"a@b.com\", subject: \"Hi\" }' \\\n  GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug\" }'\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--get-schema`: Inspect required arguments without executing the tool.\n- `--dry-run`: Preview the request shape without performing the action.\n- `--file`: Inject a local file path into a tool that exposes exactly one uploadable file argument.\n- `--parallel`: Execute multiple independent tool calls in the same invocation.\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id when multiple accounts exist for the same toolkit.\n\n- `--file` only works when the tool exposes a single uploadable file input. Otherwise use explicit `-d` JSON.\n\n## `search` - Find The Slug\n\nUse `search` only when the tool slug is not already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio search \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"send an email\" --toolkits gmail\ncomposio search \"send an email\" \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"my emails\" \"my github issues\" --toolkits gmail,github\n```\n\n- Batch related discovery work into one `search` invocation, then move back to `execute` once the correct slugs are known.\n\n## `link` - Connect An Account\n\nUse `link` when `execute` reports that a toolkit is not connected, or when the user explicitly wants to authorize an account.\n\n```bash\ncomposio link gmail\ncomposio link googlecalendar --no-browser\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--alias`: Assign an alias to the connected account. Required when creating an additional account for the same toolkit.\n\n- Retry the original `execute` command after linking succeeds.\n\n## `proxy` - Raw API Access\n\nUse `proxy` when a toolkit supports a raw API operation that is easier than finding a dedicated tool slug.\n\n```bash\ncomposio proxy https://api.github.com/user --toolkit github --account work --method GET \u003c/dev/null\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id.\n\n## `run` - Scripting, LLMs, and Programmatic Workflows\n\nFor programmatic calls, loops, output plumbing, or anything beyond a single tool call, prefer `composio run`.\n\n`composio run` executes an inline ESM JavaScript/TypeScript snippet with authenticated `execute()`, `search()`, `proxy()`, and the experimental `experimental_subAgent()` helper pre-injected. No SDK setup required.\n\nChain multiple tools:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const me = await execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\");\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 1 });\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, fetchedEmails: !!emails.data });\n'\n```\n\nFan out with Promise.all:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const [me, emails] = await Promise.all([\n    execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\"),\n    execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 }),\n  ]);\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, emailCount: emails.data.messages?.length });\n'\n```\n\nFeed tool output into an LLM and get structured JSON back:\n```bash\ncomposio run --logs-off '\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 });\n  const brief = await experimental_subAgent(\n    `Summarize these emails and count them.\\n\\n${emails.prompt()}`,\n    { schema: z.object({ summary: z.string(), count: z.number() }) }\n  );\n  console.log(brief.structuredOutput);\n'\n```\n\n- Use top-level `execute --parallel` instead when the user only needs a few independent tool calls and does not need script logic.\n\n## Auth\n\n```bash\ncomposio whoami   # check current session\ncomposio login    # authenticate if whoami fails\n```\n\n## Escalate Only When Needed\n\nIf the user is stuck on top-level commands or needs fallback inspection commands, load [references/troubleshooting.md](references/troubleshooting.md).\n\nIf the user explicitly asks about developer projects, auth configs, connected accounts, triggers, logs, orgs, or projects, load [references/composio-dev.md](references/composio-dev.md). `composio dev` is not the default end-user path.\n---\nname: using-21nco-github\ndescription: Uses the thyaravind GitHub account for repositories under dev/n or with a 21nCo origin. Use for any GitHub CLI, pull request, issue, release, or repository operation in those repositories.\n---\n\n# Using 21nCo GitHub\n\nFor any repository whose path is under `dev/n` or whose Git remote belongs to `21nCo`:\n\n- Run every `gh` command with `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`.\n- Verify `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh auth status` reports `thyaravind` as the active account before making changes.\n- Never use the default GitHub account or silently fall back to another account.\n- Use the existing `21nCo` remote for pushes. If `thyaravind` lacks permission, stop and report the denial instead of creating a fork or alternate remote.\n148:- git worktree list --porcelain, gh pr list, GH_CONFIG_DIR, 21nCo/super-functions, 21nCo/superfunctions, docs/http-decode-params, sfns-4-rex-upstream, prunable-worktree, PR #188, PR #179, PR #170\n158:- At the September 17 snapshot, `superfunctions-dev`/`docs/http-decode-params` matched clean PR #188; SFNS-4/`sfns-4-rex-upstream` matched dirty PR #179; `/private/tmp/pr170-refetch-fix.h2yq4m` was missing and prunable, associated with PR #170. [Task 1]\n247:# Task Group: September 16 Skillplane PR #38 bounded DataFn date-cursor remediation\n248:scope: Use for the exact bounded `pr-review-fix` workflow or date-valued DataFn HTTP cursor pagination defects in Skillplane.\n301:- SFNS-4, PR #179, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, adapterNamespace, Retry-After, tenantId, SecFn readJson, e200f50d, 511c02a\n320:# Task Group: September 16 Skillplane SKI-17 read-only review and Linear acceptance completeness\n321:scope: Use for strict read-only review of Skillplane DataFn upgrade work, especially when local code looks complete but acceptance criteria may require deployed-region verification.\n349:# Task Group: September 16 Skillplane Linear issue verification and published-contract blocker\n380:- When direct Skillplane/Linear MCP fails with `oauth_token_invalid_grant`, retrieve the hosted workflow via Composio and read the issue through `orca linear issue --current --full --json`; do not substitute cached instructions. [Task 1][Task 2]\n389:# Task Group: September 15 Superfunctions PR #170 bounded remediation and dirty-dev reconciliation\n390:scope: Use for live hosted `pr-refetch-fix` work on McpFn PR #170, or scoped publication/reconciliation from a dirty, stale Superfunctions dev checkout.\n393:## Task 1: Harden PR #170 redaction/export invariants in a bounded hosted round, success\n403:- PR #170, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gate:mcpfn-release, McpFnRemoteCredentialLease, cleanupPhase, redaction, 8b23aaa, 1e45b794\n417:- when the user invokes `use-skillplane PR #170 pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e retrieve the exact live hosted workflow; make one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, verify the required head once, then stop without refetching reviews, polling CI, or merging. [Task 1]\n423:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub commands. Page every GraphQL `reviewThreads` cursor: PR #170’s first 100-thread result had `hasNextPage: true`; only a three-page, 284-thread snapshot was complete. [Task 1]\n465:## Task 1: Diagnose Skillplane MCP visibility and Codex MCP removal, partial\n469:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-15T07-58-31-nkj1-codex_mcp_removal_and_skillplane_tool_availability.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/15/rollout-2026-09-15T13-28-31-01a0a413-1d4e-75b2-b09c-73af08420dc3.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T08:05:33+00:00, thread_id=01a0a413-1d4e-75b2-b09c-73af08420dc3, configured Skillplane was not model-visible)\n490:- when the user asks to check Skillplane MCP tools -\u003e distinguish enabled/configured registration from model-visible callable tools; use direct MCP first and Composio CLI fallback if unavailable. [Task 1][ad-hoc note]\n496:- `codex mcp list`/`get` only establish configuration; tool inventory establishes model visibility. If direct Skillplane MCP is unavailable, retrieve the live hosted workflow with the configured Composio fallback rather than cached local instructions. [Task 1][ad-hoc note]\n503:- Do not claim Skillplane access from a configured endpoint alone. `curl: (6) Could not resolve host: mcp.skillplane.dev` was sandbox-only evidence, and the escalation was aborted. [Task 1]\n673:# Task Group: September 13 Skillplane release, PR remediation, worktree sync, and reuse boundaries\n674:scope: Use for Skillplane tagged CLI publication, bounded live-review remediation, approved worktree cleanup, or deciding whether Superfunctions/CliFn is contract-compatible.\n685:- PR #23, reviewThreads(first:100), GH_CONFIG_DIR, pr-refetch-fix, projections, SQLite writer lock, SkillEditor, CompositionPanel, SonarQube, 7715373, 83058246\n719:- when the user says “splane pr review fix” -\u003e use live direct Skillplane MCP when available, otherwise Composio; classify current findings, take one bounded review/fix/verify/push round, and stop without post-push polling. [Task 1]\n722:- when the user asks whether Skillplane can use Superfunctions or says “how about clifn?” -\u003e inspect actual package contracts and preserve product-owned security/domain behavior rather than equating similar plumbing. [Task 4]\n730:- Prefer `@superfunctions/observability` and possibly `@superfunctions/http-hono` as narrow infrastructure candidates. Do not replace Skillplane search/storage/MCP/CLI security contracts: signed authorization-filtered cursors, immutable digest-verified bundles, Keychain/Secret Service credentials, `O_NOFOLLOW` filesystem safety, and transport-error redaction are product-owned. CliFn fits repeated scripts more than the local-runtime command graph. [Task 4]\n736:- Do not use CliFn credentials or generic runner errors as-is: its INI API-key store and thrown-message serialization conflict with Skillplane's secret-store and redaction requirements. [Task 4]\n791:# Task Group: September 13 Skillplane worktree cleanup and PR #21 synchronization\n792:scope: Use for evidence-backed Skillplane worktree inventory/removal or reconciling stale primary-checkout changes with a squash-merged PR.\n847:scope: Use for live hosted Skillplane PR-quality review, one bounded remediation round, or an explicitly authorized Superfunctions merge.\n858:- splane, PR #174, PR #177, SFNS-3, OAuth invalid_grant, Composio CLI, 21n/pr-quality-analysis@1.0.0, pr-refetch-fix, gate:reviewfn-release, package-lock, d4de3791\n862:- when direct Skillplane MCP fails with OAuth `invalid_grant` -\u003e retrieve the exact hosted workflow through Composio CLI before declaring access blocked; do not substitute cached local instructions. [Task 1]\n906:# Task Group: September 12 Skillplane hosted PR quality analysis and publication\n907:scope: Use for `splane pr-quality-analysis` of open Skillplane PRs, exact-head probes, Notion publication, and review provenance.\n910:## Task 1: Review open Skillplane PRs #17, #20, and #21, success\n920:## Task 2: Remediate open Skillplane review findings in one bounded round, success\n938:- If direct Skillplane MCP is unavailable, discover Composio schemas once, retrieve the live skill with `CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE`, and reuse the resolved tool shapes. Separate reviewer runtime from PR author/implementation provenance. [Task 1]\n948:# Task Group: September 12 Skillplane local-first runtime, native composition, and Linear document retrieval\n949:scope: Use for SKI-15/SKI-3 local-first and composition work, or when retrieving Skillplane Linear documents through Composio.\n973:## Task 3: Inventory and remove only requested safe Skillplane worktrees, success\n986:- when the user invokes `splane` or requests communication with Skillplane -\u003e prefer the direct Skillplane MCP; if unavailable or unreachable, use Composio CLI to discover schemas and communicate through the intended connected account. Do not substitute a local cached skill merely because direct MCP is unavailable. [ad-hoc note]\n1123:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-10T14-20-26-2rMD-mcp1_transport_neutral_conformance_pr_170.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/10/rollout-2026-09-10T19-50-26-01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-10T15:17:36+00:00, thread_id=01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3, PR #170 local release gate passed; hosted CI queued)\n1206:# Task Group: September 11 Skillplane PR #14 exact-head remediation, conditional merge, and worktree cleanup\n1207:scope: Use for a bounded hosted PR remediation, conditional exact-head merge, and separately authorized removal of merged Skillplane worktrees.\n1666:# Task Group: September 8-15 Superfunctions PR quality, issue-grounded review, and PR #170 convergence\n1667:scope: Use for broad read-only Superfunctions PR review, comparative findings/quality reporting, follow-up assessment, and root-cause remediation convergence across DataFn/UIFN/McpFn.\n1670:## Task 1: Review requested live Superfunctions PRs and diagnose remediation convergence, partial\n1674:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-07T09-15-30-XKOk-pr_review_remediation_convergence_and_skill_amendment.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/07/rollout-2026-09-07T14-45-31-01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T07:43:24+00:00, thread_id=01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0, complete comparative report was not delivered; PR #170 symptom-patching convergence failure documented)\n1678:- GH_CONFIG_DIR, gh pr list, PR #144, PR #145, PR #149, PR #150, PR #151, PR #152, PR #153, PR #155, PR #170, reviewThreads, Sonar, convergence, root-cause\n1690:## Task 3: Evaluate PR #170 and all subsequent fixes, partial\n1698:- PR #170, MCP-1, transport-neutral conformance, aa08ef72e, reviewThreads(first:100), isOutdated, JUnit, --visible-tools, redaction, ESM/CJS, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n1703:- when the user asks to evaluate “PR #170 and all subsequent PR fixes” -\u003e assess both history and exact current head, enumerate all commits/review surfaces/checks, and finish the requested verdict before pursuing unrelated PR material. [Task 3]\n1704:- when remediation repeatedly finds adjacent variants, the user asked to “strive for convergence and broader look” -\u003e reset to the shared invariant/subsystem rather than publish another one-example symptom patch. [Task 1]\n1714:- For PR #170, paginate `pullRequest.reviewThreads(first:100,after:$endCursor)` and separate `isOutdated` from current unresolved threads; snapshot base/head OIDs, commits, local exact-head source, and hosted checks independently. The captured head `aa08ef72e` was open/mergeable but `UNSTABLE`, with 34 current unresolved threads and 10 pending checks; do not call it merge-ready from focused tests or resolved threads. [Task 3]\n1725:# Task Group: September 8-9 Skillplane PR #8 review-fix quality, migration hardening, and chronology\n1726:scope: Use for a bounded current-review remediation or a timestamped quality comparison of Skillplane PR #8 fixes; distinguish cumulative-head evidence from commit-specific proof.\n1880:# Task Group: September 3 Skillplane Factory MCP Unicode-regex schema compatibility\n1881:scope: Use to diagnose a client rejecting Skillplane's generic MCP tool catalog before tool invocation.\n1892:- Skillplane___skills_search, `^[\\\\p{L}\\\\p{N}._:-]+$`, Factory Droid, JSON Schema, Zod, `rg -F`, disabledTools, skills_list, skill_retrieve\n1901:- Literal `\\\\p{L}` searches can make ripgrep parse an invalid Unicode class; use `rg -F`. Do not misdiagnose this as Skillplane search or PR access failure. [Task 1]\n2159:- splane, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #133, DocsFn, GH_CONFIG_DIR, release-gate, OAuth, authorization-server issuer, @next/swc-darwin-arm64, SvelteKit\n2173:- when the user says `splane` -\u003e retrieve the exact hosted Skillplane workflow live through MCP rather than substituting local instructions or memory. [Task 1]\n2175:- for `pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e perform one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push round; do not refetch reviews or poll CI afterward. [Task 1]\n2187:- Symptom: repeated Skillplane login prompts. Cause: refresh state lacks the authorization-server issuer. Fix: one `codex mcp logout skillplane`, then one `codex mcp login skillplane`. [Task 1]\n2225:- splane, pr-refetch-fix, PR #139, applyOmit, applyOmitToArrayValue, nested arrays, userId, datafn/server/src/execution/query/select.ts, ff833823\n2249:- when the user invokes `splane` -\u003e resolve the exact hosted Skillplane workflow live; this rollout used `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` rather than local instructions. [Task 1]\n2312:- A dirty checkout 83 commits behind `origin/dev` cannot establish current repository truth. Inspect live remote code and do not treat the private traversal or Skillplane duplicate helper as a shipped canonical API. [Task 1]\n2315:scope: Use for bounded PR #569 review/merge work, Skillplane OAuth recovery, or a read-only Nucleus worktree removal-safety classification.\n2318:## Task 1: Run one hosted Skillplane PR #569 review-fix round and diagnose OAuth churn, success\n2326:- splane, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #569, OAuth refresh credentials, authorization server issuer, codex mcp logout skillplane, codex mcp login skillplane, sslmode, sslrootcert, resolveClientIp, 400ee32b\n2346:- 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #569, reviewThreads, 49d13ae6, merge-async, gh-other, 9971f77, SonarCloud, CircleCI, Vercel\n2350:- when the user writes `splane` -\u003e resolve the exact hosted Skillplane workflow live through MCP rather than using local instructions or memory. [Task 1]\n2353:- when hosted Skillplane OAuth failed, the user said “use local cached version of the skill” -\u003e use that exact cached published workflow only after explicit authorization; a no-change round does not authorize edits or polling. [Task 3]\n2358:- A `pr-refetch-fix` invocation is one review snapshot, narrow fixes, proportionate verification, at most one commit/push, and one head comparison; preserve a divergent local PR branch under a backup ref and use a clean exact-head worktree. PR #569 fixed duplicate `sslmode`/`sslrootcert` values with `URL.searchParams.getAll(...)`, and supplied Node AuthFn `resolveClientIp` preferring `cf-connecting-ip`, then first `x-forwarded-for`. [Task 1]\n2359:- Stale Skillplane OAuth refresh state missing the authorization-server issuer, rather than normal expiry, caused repeated prompts. The durable reset is one `codex mcp logout skillplane` followed by one `codex mcp login skillplane`; desktop, CLI, and IDE share this MCP configuration. [Task 1]\n2391:- splane pr refetch fix, PR #570, reviewThreads, GraphQL, df11df18, merge-async, direct_merge, f849c684, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1\n2408:# Task Group: August 31 Skillplane MCP schema, OAuth compatibility, production release, and DevFn local-development fit\n2409:scope: Use for strict MCP `skills_list` failures, OAuth redirect compatibility, production-only Skillplane releases, durable local verification, or assessing DevFn local-development fit.\n2432:## Task 3: Push and deploy Skillplane to production only, success\n2442:## Task 4: Assess DevFn for Skillplane local development, uncertain\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed3c8c8c87d097933cbedda68a04","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Checking direct CLI availability for skillplane**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_2xfjRsY44hrrS0bOqzRQjQlz","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed3f8e5c87d0b985814c41298886","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"for candidate in skillplane splane; do if command -v \\\"$candidate\\\" \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261; then printf '%s=%s\\\\n' \\\"$candidate\\\" \\\"$(command -v \\\"$candidate\\\")\\\"; else printf '%s=unavailable\\\\n' \\\"$candidate\\\"; fi; done\\ncomposio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE --get-schema\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":20000}); text(r.output); if (r.session_id) text(`SESSION_ID=${r.session_id}`);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979963.504102,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_2xfjRsY44hrrS0bOqzRQjQlz","id":"ctco_01a0c31e-d9c3-7382-a7a1-b2eba75298ca","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979974.083917,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"skillplane=unavailable\nsplane=unavailable\n{\n  \"slug\": \"CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE\",\n  \"version\": null,\n  \"schemaPath\": \"/Users/serro/.composio/tool_definitions/CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE.json\",\n  \"inputSchema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n    \"required\": [\n      \"skill\",\n      \"caller\"\n    ],\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"skill\": {\n        \"anyOf\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"id\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"id\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"maxLength\": 200,\n                \"minLength\": 1\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"workspaceSlug\",\n              \"skillSlug\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"skillSlug\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"pattern\": \"^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$\",\n                \"maxLength\": 120,\n                \"minLength\": 1\n              },\n              \"workspaceSlug\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"pattern\": \"^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$\",\n                \"maxLength\": 120,\n                \"minLength\": 1\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          }\n        ]\n      },\n      \"caller\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\n          \"agentId\",\n          \"agentName\",\n          \"modelProvider\",\n          \"modelName\",\n          \"modelVersion\",\n          \"clientName\",\n          \"clientVersion\",\n          \"runId\",\n          \"sessionId\",\n          \"conversationId\"\n        ],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"runId\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 200,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"agentId\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 160,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"agentName\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 120,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"modelName\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 160,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"sessionId\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 200,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"clientName\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 160,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"modelVersion\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 120,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"clientVersion\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 120,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"modelProvider\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 120,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          },\n          \"conversationId\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"maxLength\": 200,\n            \"minLength\": 1\n          }\n        },\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      },\n      \"context\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\n          \"selector\"\n        ],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"selector\": {\n            \"anyOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\n                  \"id\"\n                ],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"id\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"maxLength\": 200,\n                    \"minLength\": 1\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\n                  \"slug\"\n                ],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"slug\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"pattern\": \"^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$\",\n                    \"maxLength\": 120,\n                    \"minLength\": 1\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              }\n            ]\n          },\n          \"knowledge\": {\n            \"oneOf\": [\n              {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\n                  \"selector\"\n                ],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"selector\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"const\": \"current\"\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\n                  \"selector\",\n                  \"revisionId\"\n                ],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"selector\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"const\": \"revisionId\"\n                  },\n                  \"revisionId\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"maxLength\": 200,\n                    \"minLength\": 1\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              },\n              {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\n                  \"selector\",\n                  \"revision\"\n                ],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"revision\": {\n                    \"type\": \"integer\",\n                    \"maximum\": 9007199254740991,\n                    \"exclusiveMinimum\": 0\n                  },\n                  \"selector\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"const\": \"revision\"\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              }\n            ],\n            \"default\": {\n              \"selector\": \"current\"\n            }\n          },\n          \"includeNotes\": {\n            \"type\": \"boolean\",\n            \"default\": false\n          }\n        },\n        \"additionalProperties\": false\n      },\n      \"version\": {\n        \"oneOf\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"selector\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"selector\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"const\": \"current\"\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"selector\",\n              \"versionId\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"selector\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"const\": \"versionId\"\n              },\n              \"versionId\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"maxLength\": 200,\n                \"minLength\": 1\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"selector\",\n              \"semanticVersion\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"selector\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"const\": \"semanticVersion\"\n              },\n              \"semanticVersion\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"pattern\": \"^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:-[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?(?:\\\\+[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$\",\n                \"maxLength\": 160,\n                \"minLength\": 1\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          },\n          {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"required\": [\n              \"selector\",\n              \"revision\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"revision\": {\n                \"type\": \"integer\",\n                \"maximum\": 9007199254740991,\n                \"exclusiveMinimum\": 0\n              },\n              \"selector\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\",\n                \"const\": \"revision\"\n              }\n            },\n            \"additionalProperties\": false\n          }\n        ],\n        \"default\": {\n          \"selector\": \"current\"\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"additionalProperties\": false\n  }\n}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed4870e887d09ef6899901f98a74","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Preparing execution with unique run IDs**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_rS6AljiAchOsoS6L0Pz7VsZZ","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed4a3ef487d0b0fcdc866b8b9cc1","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\"skill\\\":{\\\"workspaceSlug\\\":\\\"21n\\\",\\\"skillSlug\\\":\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\"},\\\"version\\\":{\\\"selector\\\":\\\"current\\\"},\\\"caller\\\":{\\\"agentId\\\":\\\"codex-root\\\",\\\"agentName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"modelProvider\\\":\\\"OpenAI\\\",\\\"modelName\\\":\\\"GPT-5\\\",\\\"modelVersion\\\":\\\"2026-09-21\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"Codex Desktop\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"1\\\",\\\"runId\\\":\\\"pr170-20260921-round12\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\",\\\"conversationId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\"}}'\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r.output); if (r.session_id) text(`SESSION_ID=${r.session_id}`);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979974.656943,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_rS6AljiAchOsoS6L0Pz7VsZZ","id":"ctco_01a0c31f-1bfa-7aa0-8786-9bb95cb9df9c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979991.034642,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 9.3 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/\u003cn\u003e/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n\u003e One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:a45f2611-559e-42e9-8b78-27d6779d6afe\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_HJCDY723PlO0\"\n}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed5a53d087d09e3026a615a50af9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Assessing ledger and prechecks before fetch**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning sequential prechecks and partial PR fetch**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"Skillplane returned the same published workflow: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`, revision 4, digest `sha256:8781…4022`. I’ll now validate the PR-170 worktree, account, branch, ledger, and exact starting head before taking the single review snapshot.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed62b16087d0a6143fe3fd183929","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789979992.731637,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_YZSbGEWMz4R9gz6LgjkrrYPY","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed644b1887d0b11b915fedcff11f","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"git branch --show-current\\ngit status --short --branch\\ngit status --porcelain=v2 --branch\\ngit remote -v\\ngit worktree list --porcelain\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh auth status\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh pr view 170 --repo 21nCo/super-functions --json number,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,url\\nsed -n '1,260p' .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\\ntail -n 180 .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":40000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789979992.731637,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_YZSbGEWMz4R9gz6LgjkrrYPY","id":"ctco_01a0c31f-620b-7301-8666-9006126b524a","in