ations protocol: https\n  - Token: gho_************************************\n  - Token scopes: 'gist', 'read:org', 'repo', 'workflow'\n{\"baseRefName\":\"dev\",\"headRefName\":\"codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910\",\"headRefOid\":\"9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\",\"number\":170,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170\"}\n# PR 170 recurring remediation ledger\n\n## Workflow identity\n\n- Hosted skill: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`\n- Revision: `4`\n- Digest: `sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022`\n- Automation: `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82`\n- Repository / PR: `21nCo/super-functions#170`\n- Head branch: `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`\n\n## Current round\n\n- Round: `9`\n- Mode: `assessment` (credential-structure, report-failure taxonomy, and CLI runtime-output families recurred; coordinated reassessment completed before mutation)\n- Local pre-snapshot HEAD: `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad`\n- Snapshot / PR head: `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad`\n- Snapshot consistency: stable across all eleven review-thread pages, five review pages, fifteen raw-inline pages, two issue-comment pages, PR metadata, and the immediate check snapshot; all observed heads matched and every nested thread-comment connection was terminal\n- Review surfaces: 325 threads, 220 review records, 64 issue comments, and 364 raw inline comments\n- Automation state: disabled and verified after an initial retrieval-tool discovery failure; this manually retried round uses the subsequently retrieved and verified pinned workflow, but the schedule was not silently re-enabled\n- Commit / push budget: consumed exactly once for the round-9 repair batch\n- Round commit: `cf76637ada4ba0263e36f30c23a278826c48dd0a` (`fix(mcpfn): harden artifact failure boundaries`)\n- Push: one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`\n- One allowed post-push head comparison: local, remote, and PR head all `cf76637ada4ba0263e36f30c23a278826c48dd0a`; PR remained open\n\n## Broader subsystem assessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing MCP operations must preserve retryable resource ownership until cleanup succeeds, never expose opaque credential material at any consumer-visible boundary, and keep authored report/event structure intact. Conformance evidence must reject insecure OAuth token envelopes, regression tests must assert the behavior they claim, and CI bootstrap must not execute source-distribution setup code.\n- Producers and consumers: credential providers and authenticated targets produce leases and target-owned redaction; `McpFnClient` owns transports/events/diagnostics; testing suites and official conformance produce bounded reports; inspector produces snapshots/scenarios; `runCli` persists operator artifacts; `bin.ts` is the only process-termination boundary. Hosted OAuth servers produce redirect/token responses consumed by the authorization regression runner. GitHub Actions consumes Python bootstrap packages.\n- Failure boundaries: acquisition/open/initialize, inventory and scenario execution, payload/envelope projection, report serialization, protocol/transport/revoke/dispose cleanup, programmatic CLI error propagation, executable stderr flush/termination, authorization-code exchange and refresh, and package bootstrap in CI.\n- Coordinated repair: (1) persist any bounded cleanup-failure artifact, then propagate the owning cleanup error through `runCli`; retry once at the outer library boundary; only `bin.ts` prints a safe diagnostic and terminates for all three retryable cleanup error types; (2) force payload mode for every inspector snapshot projection; (3) validate JSON plus `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Pragma: no-cache` for both exchange and refresh success responses; (4) correct misleading/incorrect regressions; (5) require binary Python distributions in the failing Sonar-gated bootstrap step. These are complete enforcement points for the current findings rather than commented-line patches.\n- Verification criteria: focused old-behavior regressions for cleanup retry failure/success, executable diagnostics, snapshot payload collisions, key-only scenario redaction, exchange/refresh cache headers, and misleading retry configuration; then package typechecks/tests, the Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and one pre-push review of the complete diff. Hosted checks are observed only in the already captured immediate snapshot.\n\n### Round 2 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: consumer-visible reports and snapshots must never silently remain passing when credential safety or evidence completeness cannot be guaranteed, and executable termination must not discard already-produced evidence.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated target credential leases and the shared scrubber produce protected values and redacted artifacts; target suites, inspector and JUnit consume authored structural envelopes; official conformance produces captured streams; `serializeBoundedReport` persists bounded operator evidence; `runCli` writes reports; `bin.ts` owns forced process termination.\n- Failure boundaries: credential text colliding with authored discriminator values, final JSON recomposition, report-size truncation, asynchronous stdout backpressure, cleanup-owner propagation into the executable boundary, and non-enumerable `Error.message` / `cause` fields in regressions.\n- Coordinated repair: reject structural-value credential collisions at the shared scrubber while preserving authored schema keys; mark truncated conformance reports explicitly incomplete and force exit 1; await every CLI output write before cleanup retry/termination; assert cleanup error messages and reports separately.\n- Verification criteria: collision regressions for `pass` / `complete` and existing structural values; a successful oversized conformance result must persist as bounded `status: incomplete`, `ok: false`, exit 1; delayed stdout must complete before cleanup retry can reject; the cleanup assertion must inspect both message and report. Then package typechecks/tests, the full Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 3 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: a retryable cleanup failure must retain the exact resource owner until cleanup succeeds; later output, diagnostic, persistence, or sanitization failures may be reported only after ownership is safely preserved. Public-client OAuth fixtures must distinguish protocol-compliant token rejection from an authentication challenge.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated conformance and target suites produce retryable cleanup-owner errors; `McpFnInspector` owns an `McpFnClient` transport; command actions produce stdout/stderr and persisted reports; `runCli` performs the one bounded retry; `bin.ts` is the final process boundary. Hosted authorization fixtures produce token exchange/refresh rejections consumed by the conformance runner.\n- Failure boundaries: inspector close after operation/output failure, conformance and target-suite output/report persistence after a cleanup artifact exists, outer diagnostic output before cleanup classification/retry, and unauthenticated token exchange/refresh status validation.\n- Coordinated repair: add a retryable inspector cleanup owner; prioritize every cleanup owner over secondary output/persistence/diagnostic failures; preserve that owner through the bounded retry and executable termination boundary; and require HTTP 400 for `invalid_client` from public-client exchange and refresh fixtures.\n- Verification criteria: regressions must show inspector ownership survives two failed closes and can later be retried; conformance and target-suite owners must survive rejecting output callbacks and report persistence failures; executable classification must include inspector cleanup; public-client `invalid_client` must pass at 400 and fail at 401 for exchange and refresh. Then focused tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 4 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: while a target owns credentials, every consumer-visible dynamic value—including reconstructed schema discriminators—must be proven safe by that target's redactor before emission; if mandatory structure collides with a credential, the producer must fail closed or emit a safe marked omission. Any abstraction that exposes `close()` must retain and return the retry owner when close fails.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient` produces diagnostic and client-event envelopes consumed directly and by `McpFnInspector`; the inspector reconstructs snapshot, timeline, and scenario discriminators for library and CLI consumers. `McpFnTestClient` owns a production `McpFnClient`; direct callers and `mcpfn test` consume its public `close()` contract, while `runCli` already recognizes `McpFnTestClientCleanupError`.\n- Failure boundaries: structural values reconstructed after payload redaction (`kind`, `phase`, `outcome`, `clientState`, target kind, scenario side effect), safe omission fallback construction, inspector snapshot/export assembly, and post-connect local test-client shutdown after successful operations.\n- Coordinated repair: add one client structural-safety enforcement point backed by the active target redactor; apply it to event/diagnostic discriminators and all inspector-reconstructed discriminators, dropping only when no schema-safe omission can be produced; then make `McpFnTestClient.close()` wrap retained session cleanup in the existing retryable owner while preserving connection-failure cause semantics during failed connect cleanup.\n- Verification criteria: credentials equal to client/inspector structural values must never appear in direct events, timelines, snapshots, or scenario exports; safe omissions must be marked and counted, and mandatory snapshot/export collisions must reject with credential-free errors. A post-connect test-client close must retain the same owner through repeated failure and later succeed, and the CLI's existing owner retry boundary must remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 5 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every emitted event or diagnostic must preserve both credential confidentiality and schema semantics. A timestamp may be emitted only as the exact generated ISO string after the active target redactor proves that string safe; redaction must never turn it into a malformed timestamp.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient.emitEvent()` and diagnostic dispatch produce `at`; direct event/diagnostic listeners, `McpFnInspector` timelines/snapshots, target-suite timelines, CLI serialization, and mixed package consumers retain it.\n- Failure boundaries: timestamp generation, active-target structural safety validation, construction of a marked omission fallback, and the no-safe-fallback case where every ISO timestamp collides with a credential.\n- Coordinated repair: enforce one ISO timestamp structural validator in the shared client producer; use it for normal client events, normal diagnostics, and both omission fallbacks. On collision, route through existing omission accounting; emit a marked fallback only with a safe valid ISO timestamp, otherwise drop the artifact while retaining the omission count.\n- Verification criteria: an exact timestamp credential must never appear and must produce a marked omission whose `at` round-trips through `Date.toISOString()`; a credential present in every ISO representation must emit no malformed artifact and must increment the appropriate omission counter. Then client tests/typecheck, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 6 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every terminal cleanup failure must retain both its retry owner and its actual failure phase. Transport shutdown is `transport-close`; OAuth revocation is `token-revocation`; disposal after successful revocation returns to `transport-close`. A generic outer cleanup boundary must not overwrite a more specific trusted phase.\n- Producers / consumers: the authenticated HTTP target owns the credential lease and produces target diagnostics; `McpFnClient` coordinates handle/target cleanup and produces retryable client errors; `McpFnTestClient` retains the close owner; target-suite diagnostics, bounded reports, JUnit, CLI, and direct diagnostic consumers classify the resulting phase and failure layer.\n- Failure boundaries: raw transport close before credential release, OAuth `revoke()`, provider `dispose()`, client cleanup-drain wrapping, test-client error wrapping, and final suite report normalization.\n- Coordinated repair: throw a phase-bearing safe cleanup error from the credential lease; emit the lease phase only after transport shutdown succeeds; validate and preserve `token-revocation` through the client cleanup coordinator; and normalize the retained inner cleanup error in the target suite so a later generic close diagnostic cannot replace the terminal phase.\n- Verification criteria: direct target diagnostics must report transport failure as `transport-close`, OAuth revoke failure as `token-revocation`, and post-revocation disposal failure as `transport-close`; suite reports must map those phases to `authorization-server` and `mcpfn-preflight` respectively while retaining a retryable cleanup owner. Then client/testing focused tests and typechecks, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 7 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential cleanup errors may expose only a trusted phase, safe fixed text, and retry ownership; raw provider failures must not cross the lease, target, client, suite, inspector, or CLI boundary. Every independently reflectable credential component, including structured Cookie values, must be registered before artifacts are emitted. Target-suite post-execution artifact I/O failures are runtime failures and must not replace a retained cleanup owner.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers produce header material and revoke/dispose failures; `credentialValues` and target secret scopes produce the redaction set; credential leases and authenticated targets own retryable cleanup; `McpFnClient` coordinates target/handle cleanup; suites, JUnit, inspector, and CLI serialize consumer-visible evidence. The target suite produces reports consumed by `runCli`, whose output writers and executable boundary classify failures.\n- Failure boundaries: structured Cookie parsing, pre-handle header validation/open failure, revoke/dispose rejection, target-level pending-release retries, client cleanup-drain/close wrapping, report/JUnit/stdout persistence, and final exit-code classification.\n- Coordinated repair: extract and validate Cookie credential components at the shared secret-discovery point; sanitize lease cleanup failures and client cleanup wrappers while preserving the trusted phase separately; rethrow the retained safe rejection from target-level cleanup instead of synthesizing `transport-close`; and convert target-suite artifact/output failures into a safe runtime error without displacing any cleanup owner.\n- Verification criteria: a reflected Cookie value must be absent from direct redaction and JSON/JUnit artifacts; a provider error containing a credential must not be reachable through cleanup cause chains; repeated OAuth revoke failure during a pre-handle open failure must remain `token-revocation` through client/suite reporting and later retry; target report/JUnit/stdout write failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with safe text, while owner-priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 8 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every target-controlled value that occupies authored report structure must be proven unchanged by the active live-session redactor before emission; a structural collision must make the report safely incomplete. Every post-execution conformance output or persistence failure is an operational failure with fixed safe text and exit 1, while an existing retryable cleanup owner remains authoritative.\n- Producers / consumers: custom targets produce arbitrary `describe().kind` values and target-owned redaction; the live `McpFnClient` owns the active redactor and structural-preservation check; the target suite reconstructs bounded JSON/JUnit artifacts consumed by libraries and `mcpfn test-target`. Official conformance produces captured streams and bounded reports consumed by `runCli`, stdout/stderr callbacks, and report persistence.\n- Failure boundaries: suite projection after scenario execution but before credential cleanup; reinsertion of the raw target discriminator after descriptor payload redaction; final JSON/JUnit serialization; conformance stdout/stderr callbacks and report-file writes; cleanup-owner priority over secondary I/O failures.\n- Coordinated repair: validate the dynamic target kind with the live client's shared `preserveArtifactStructure` enforcement before reconstructing the descriptor so a collision sets the existing projection-failure path; apply the existing cleanup-aware safe runtime-output wrapper to the complete conformance stdout/stderr/report operation with a conformance-specific fixed message.\n- Verification criteria: a custom target whose redactor treats its arbitrary kind as a credential must return a bounded incomplete report with the raw kind absent; conformance report, stdout, and stderr failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with fixed safe text and omit raw I/O details, while the existing cleanup-owner priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks/builds, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 9 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing artifacts may preserve authored schema only when both structural keys and discriminator values are proven safe; if a credential colli…9384 tokens truncated…Actionable | round 10 | round 10 | Coordinated key-boundary repair selected; regression uses credential `name` |\n| `MCP170-OAUTH-CACHE-DIRECTIVE-PARAMS` | Hosted token validation discards directive parameters, so `no-store=false` and `no-cache=false` are accepted | Actionable | round 10 | round 10 | Exact directive-token repair selected for exchange and refresh |\n| `MCP170-STRUCTURAL-COLLISION-CLEANUP-OWNER` | Suite finalization throws a safe structural error before transferring an already-retained cleanup owner | Actionable; recurring cleanup-ownership family | round 10 | round 10 | Dedicated reportless retry-owner repair selected for direct and CLI consumers |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REDACTION-DECOMPOSITION` | Redaction/credential helpers contain duplicated bounds, regex, and deeply nested traversal/lifecycle logic | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 11 findings grouped under shared credential/redaction contract |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-BASE64-REGEX` | Base64 token patterns use avoidable character-class forms | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 10 | round 10 | 2 findings grouped under token grammar preservation |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-SUITE-ORCHESTRATION` | Suite orchestration contains dense comparisons, fallbacks, and control flow | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 5 findings grouped under report/lifecycle behavior preservation |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-HOSTED-OAUTH` | Hosted OAuth assessment and rejection validation contain dense conditional construction | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 5 findings grouped under authorization evidence preservation |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REPORT-JUNIT` | Failure normalization and JUnit mapping contain avoidable branching/spread/ternary complexity | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 3 findings grouped under stable report serialization |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-CLI` | CLI dispatch and portable-identifier regex trigger maintainability findings | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 10 | round 10 | 2 findings grouped under unchanged exit/output classification |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-NODE-WEB-HEADERS` | Two Node-to-Web header adapters duplicate nested ternary conversion | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 2 findings grouped under identical multi-value header semantics |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-INSPECTOR` | Secret-marker generation and export fallback selection contain avoidable constructs | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 2 findings grouped under replayable placeholder behavior |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-CONFORMANCE` | Official conformance completion mutates a parameter-derived exit code | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 1 finding; preserve incomplete/failure classification |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-CUSTOM-TARGET-TYPE` | Custom-target redactor field redundantly combines optional syntax with `undefined` | Valid, coordinated analyzer work | round 1 | round 10 | 1 type-contract cleanup |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-THROW-THIS` | Four analyzer findings flag intentional retry-owner self-rejection in cleanup error classes | Invalid analyzer interpretation | round 10 | round 10 | Existing retry identity is required; suite occurrence is removed incidentally by shared helper, other owner classes remain valid intentional behavior |\n\n- Snapshot check state: all 18 contexts terminal; 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. Sonar Quality Gate passed and reported 38 new issues; `reviewDecision` was empty.\n- Convergence state: not clean. Three new review roots, the recurring cleanup-ownership family, and the now-assessed analyzer families reset the clean streak to `0` even though the snapshot head was stable and required checks were terminal.\n\n## Round 10 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable: 4 new unresolved GitHub threads, deduplicated to 3 root causes. `MCP170-SCENARIO-KEY-STRUCTURE`, `MCP170-OAUTH-CACHE-DIRECTIVE-PARAMS`, and `MCP170-STRUCTURAL-COLLISION-CLEANUP-OWNER` are repaired by `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1`; the threads remain unresolved and were not refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 87 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions. The four round-9 roots are already repaired at the assessed head; outdated status alone was not used as evidence.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the older property-key-redaction finding remains invalid based on observed payload-key behavior. The analyzer's retry-owner `throw this` family is invalid because object identity is the ownership token; the suite instance was removed incidentally by a shared retry helper, while the three remaining instances preserve required owner identity. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. The 34 valid Sonar findings were attempted as ten coordinated subfamilies in this batch; their hosted analyzer disposition remains pending until a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, nested comments, or analyzer findings.\n\n## Round 10 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 28 inspector credential-artifact tests and 145 hosted-authorization/remote-target tests passed after the three review-root repairs. The broader testing/report/suite set passed 189 tests after decomposition, and the CLI reportless-owner regression passed with the target-suite cleanup suite.\n- Complete affected package suites: 45 `@mcpfn/client`, 234 `@mcpfn/testing`, 38 `@mcpfn/inspector`, and 54 `@mcpfn/cli` tests passed (371 total).\n- Typechecks: `@mcpfn/client`, `@mcpfn/testing`, `@mcpfn/inspector`, and `@mcpfn/cli` passed under Node 22; `@mcpfn/testing` rebuilt successfully before downstream CLI verification.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported package test/typecheck/build, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check` and the staged diff check passed. The ledger remained untracked and was never staged or committed.\n- Required-check snapshot: the one pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate passed while reporting 38 new issues. No hosted checks were fetched after push.\n- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, `origin/codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`, and PR 170 head all equal `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1`; the PR remained open.\n\n## Round 10 convergence\n\n- Trend: improving but nonterminal. The deferred analyzer inventory is no longer Not Attempted, all three new review roots have coordinated repairs, and no local regression remains; hosted reviewers/analyzers have not yet assessed the new head.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots plus 10 valid analyzer subfamilies / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted verification pending a future round.\n- New defects: top-level scenario key leakage, parameterized anti-cache directive acceptance, and cleanup-owner loss during structural report collision.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and cleanup ownership recurred; both were repaired at their shared producer/consumer boundaries.\n- Fix-induced regressions: none found by 371 complete affected-package tests, four typechecks, rebuilt package consumers, whole-batch diff review, or the Node 22 release gate.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed PR head after the assessment).\n- Head stability: the snapshot remained at `cf76637ada4ba0263e36f30c23a278826c48dd0a` during assessment; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 10 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled clean assessment must verify the new head, review dispositions, and analyzer/check state; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\n- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 11 assessment\n\n- Hosted workflow: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`, revision `4`, digest `sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022` (published identity verified through Skillplane via Composio before repository mutation).\n- Mode: assessment reset. Round 11 is a separately invoked single review round; the prior clean streak remains `0`.\n- Snapshot / head: local, upstream, every review page, and PR head were stable at `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1`. The complete one-time snapshot contains 332 threads (93 unresolved: 33 current and 60 outdated-only), 228 reviews, 64 issue comments, 373 raw inline comments, 11 current Sonar issues, and 18 check contexts. All thread and nested-comment pagination was terminal.\n- Shared invariant: a credential-bearing typed artifact may emit only the authored keys and discriminator values actually present, after the live target redactor proves each exact structural string safe; a projection failure must never bypass transport cleanup, and analyzer-only refactors must preserve credential, lifecycle, ownership, and report semantics.\n- Producers / consumers: inspector scenario export produces replay artifacts; custom targets and live client sessions produce redacted descriptors, diagnostics, scenario results, and cleanup ownership; target-suite projection/finalization produces direct-library, JSON, JUnit, and CLI evidence; shared credential scrubbers and report normalizers feed all of those consumers.\n- Failure boundaries: optional scenario fields that are validated but never emitted; custom redaction state cleared during close; fallback descriptor access through hostile getters after connect; structural collisions in fixed report keys/discriminators; base64 comparison, recursive scrub traversal, failure normalization, and suite orchestration maintainability changes.\n- Coordinated repair: validate optional inspector keys only at their emission branches; capture live custom-redactor safety for exact report structural candidates before close and enforce it against only the final emitted structure; place projection and structural capture behind unconditional cleanup; then repair the eight valid current Sonar findings without changing observable contracts. A structural collision must retain any cleanup owner and reject safely when no typed report exists.\n- Verification criteria: focused inspector optional-key regressions, pre-connect and connected custom-redactor structural collisions, hostile post-connect getter cleanup, report/redaction regressions, affected package tests/typechecks/builds, complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, whole-batch diff review, and one final local/remote/PR-head comparison. Hosted review/check surfaces will not be fetched again this round.\n\n### Round 11 defect families\n\n| Stable family | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Repair / evidence state |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` | Inspector rejects a safe complete export when a credential equals an optional key that is not emitted | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Exact-emission key validation selected |\n| `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` | A custom target that fails before connection can bypass its live redactor, exposing a credential through fixed report structure | Actionable; recurring structural-safety family | round 11 | round 11 | Pre-close structure-safety snapshot and final exact-structure enforcement selected |\n| `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` | A hostile target getter can throw during post-connect fallback construction before the sole close call | Actionable; recurring cleanup-ownership family | round 11 | round 11 | Unconditional projection-finalization cleanup selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-BASE64-PADDING` | Two trailing-padding regex operations have avoidable super-linear analysis risk | Valid analyzer finding | round 11 | round 11 | Deterministic padding-strip helper selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REDACTION-DECOMPOSITION` | Credential scrub traversal remains too complex and contains a nested role-selection conditional and escaped replacement literal | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Role selection and traversal helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-SUITE-ORCHESTRATION` | Cleanup retry callback and suite orchestration exceed analyzer maintainability limits | Valid analyzer findings; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Identity-preserving throw callback and report-building helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-REPORT-NORMALIZATION` | Failure normalization exceeds analyzer complexity threshold | Valid analyzer finding; recurring | round 1 | round 11 | Cause/detail extraction helpers selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-THROW-THIS` | Three remaining findings flag intentional retry-owner self-rejection | Invalid analyzer interpretation | round 10 | round 11 | Owner identity is the cleanup token; preserve behavior |\n\n- Snapshot dispositions before mutation: 3 new unresolved current review threads deduplicate to 3 actionable root causes. Eleven current Sonar issues deduplicate to 8 valid maintainability roots and 3 invalid retry-owner findings. Older valid blockers and dispositions remain preserved; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Required-check snapshot: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. Sonar Quality Gate passed; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n- Convergence state: not clean. Three new/actionable roots, recurring structural-safety and cleanup families, and eight valid analyzer findings reset the clean streak to `0` despite the stable snapshot head and terminal successful required checks.\n\n## Round 11 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Fixed: 3 new review root causes and 8 valid Sonar findings. `MCP170-SCENARIO-OPTIONAL-KEY-AVAILABILITY` now validates `status`, `incompleteReason`, and `variables` only in the branches that emit them. `MCP170-CUSTOM-FALLBACK-STRUCTURE` snapshots exact fixed-string safety through the live target scrubber and validates only the final emitted report structure, including bounded/fallback changes. `MCP170-PROJECTION-CLEANUP-BYPASS` places projection and structure capture behind unconditional close and safely handles a hostile post-connect `kind` getter. The valid analyzer set received deterministic Base64 padding removal, `String.raw` replacement syntax, role/traversal helpers, an identity-preserving throw callback, suite orchestration decomposition, and report-normalization helpers.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 90 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions (30 current and 60 outdated-only at this snapshot). Outdated status and one new Pullfrog acknowledgement comment were not counted as proof or new root causes.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 remaining Sonar `throw this` findings are invalid because the exact error object is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots and all 8 valid current Sonar findings were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer/analyzer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\n\n## Round 11 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 36 target-suite tests, 88 remote-target tests, 10 report tests, and 31 authenticated-inspector artifact tests passed (165 total). New cases prove absent optional scenario keys do not overreject, pre-connect custom fallback structure fails closed, a post-connect hostile kind getter cannot bypass close, and fixed report-key/discriminator collisions reject safely.\n- Complete affected-package suites: all 236 `@mcpfn/testing` tests and all 41 `@mcpfn/inspector` tests passed. Both packages typechecked and built successfully under Node 22.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported package test/typecheck/build, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check` and the staged diff check passed. Whole-batch review covered all six code/test files. The ledger remained untracked and was never staged or committed.\n- Required-check snapshot: the single pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate passed while its current issue inventory contained 8 valid and 3 invalid findings. No hosted checks or reviews were fetched after push.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first focused run exposed four stale expectations that permitted unproven fixed structure; those regressions now assert safe rejection. The broader remote-target run exposed that a safely projected dynamic-kind fallback was being rejected before fallback selection; finalization now validates the actually emitted fallback, preserving the established incomplete-report contract. All subsequent focused, complete-package, and release-gate runs passed.\n- Commit / push: one commit `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` (`fix(mcpfn): close structural report gaps`) and one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`.\n- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, remote branch head, and PR 170 head all equal `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f`; PR 170 remained open.\n\n## Round 11 convergence\n\n- Trend: improving but nonterminal. All selected review and valid analyzer roots have coordinated repairs, no local regression remains, and the immediate check snapshot was terminal/successful; hosted reviewers and analyzers have not yet assessed the new head.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots plus 8 valid analyzer findings / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted verification pending a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- New defects: optional-key overvalidation, custom-redactor fallback structure bypass, and projection-before-cleanup escape.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and cleanup ownership recurred; both were repaired at shared producer/finalization boundaries rather than at individual symptom sites.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed PR head after assessment).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `80ba026172a774ea95277393becffa35c9892fc1` during assessment; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 11 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled clean assessment must verify the new head, review dispositions, and analyzer/check state; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\n- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed6afda887d0a04215c214e434fd","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning comprehensive review pagination**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Assessing safe review snapshot storage**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing reliable paginated gh API fetch**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed7bbfb887d08e086999ef452961","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning manual pagination with jq**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing multi-source PR data fetching**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Adding PR head consistency check**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed8b83c487d0bde3d0873752aafa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning SonarCloud issue retrieval**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing GitHub API pagination handling**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"Preflight is clean and exact: branch and PR head are both `9ee45f438…`, the only worktree change is the untracked ledger, `origin` is correct, PR 170 is open, and `thyaravind` is active. I’m taking the single complete review/check snapshot now, including every thread page and nested pagination state.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed963b6087d0bf0b4198aa73a15d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789980009.616515,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_x1B1fuECeyMuq5pj1gjKIZxm","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0ed98465087d0b1a48aa2e97cbbe8","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"snapshot_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/pr170-round12.XXXXXX)\\nprintf '%s\\\\n' \\\"$snapshot_dir\\\"\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh pr view 170 --repo 21nCo/super-functions --json number,state,url,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,updatedAt,statusCheckRollup,reviews,comments \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/pr-snapshot.json\\\"\\ncursor=''\\npage=1\\nwhile true; do\\n  if [ -n \\\"$cursor\\\" ]; then\\n    GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -F owner=21nCo -F name=super-functions -F pr=170 -F endCursor=\\\"$cursor\\\" -f query='query($owner:String!,$name:String!,$pr:Int!,$endCursor:String){repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){pullRequest(number:$pr){headRefOid reviewThreads(first:100,after:$endCursor){nodes{id isResolved path comments(first:100){nodes{id body author{login} createdAt updatedAt outdated url diffHunk originalLine line} pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor}}} pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor}}}}}' \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/threads-$page.json\\\"\\n  else\\n    GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh api graphql -F owner=21nCo -F name=super-functions -F pr=170 -f query='query($owner:String!,$name:String!,$pr:Int!,$endCursor:String){repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){pullRequest(number:$pr){headRefOid reviewThreads(first:100,after:$endCursor){nodes{id isResolved path comments(first:100){nodes{id body author{login} createdAt updatedAt outdated url diffHunk originalLine line} pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor}}} pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor}}}}}' \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/threads-$page.json\\\"\\n  fi\\n  has_next=$(jq -r '.data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.pageInfo.hasNextPage' \\\"$snapshot_dir/threads-$page.json\\\")\\n  cursor=$(jq -r '.data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.pageInfo.endCursor // empty' \\\"$snapshot_dir/threads-$page.json\\\")\\n  if [ \\\"$has_next\\\" != 'true' ]; then break; fi\\n  page=$((page + 1))\\ndone\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate --slurp repos/21nCo/super-functions/pulls/170/reviews \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/reviews-pages.json\\\"\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate --slurp repos/21nCo/super-functions/issues/170/comments \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/issue-comments-pages.json\\\"\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate --slurp repos/21nCo/super-functions/pulls/170/comments \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/inline-comments-pages.json\\\"\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh pr view 170 --repo 21nCo/super-functions --json state,headRefOid \u003e \\\"$snapshot_dir/pr-end.json\\\"\\nprintf 'thread_pages=%s\\\\n' \\\"$page\\\"\\nprintf 'thread_count=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq -s '[.[].data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.nodes[]] | length' \\\"$snapshot_dir\\\"/threads-*.json)\\\"\\nprintf 'nested_pending=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq -s '[.[].data.repository.pullRequest.reviewThreads.nodes[].comments.pageInfo.hasNextPage] | map(select(. == true)) | length' \\\"$snapshot_dir\\\"/threads-*.json)\\\"\\nprintf 'page_heads=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq -s '[.[].data.repository.pullRequest.headRefOid] | unique | join(\\\",\\\")' \\\"$snapshot_dir\\\"/threads-*.json)\\\"\\nprintf 'reviews=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq 'add | length' \\\"$snapshot_dir/reviews-pages.json\\\")\\\"\\nprintf 'issue_comments=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq 'add | length' \\\"$snapshot_dir/issue-comments-pages.json\\\")\\\"\\nprintf 'inline_comments=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq 'add | length' \\\"$snapshot_dir/inline-comments-pages.json\\\")\\\"\\nprintf 'snapshot_head=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq -r '.headRefOid' \\\"$snapshot_dir/pr-snapshot.json\\\")\\\"\\nprintf 'end=%s\\\\n' \\\"$(jq -c . \\\"$snapshot_dir/pr-end.json\\\")\\\"\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":20000}); text(r.output); if (r.session_id) text(`SESSION_ID=${r.session_id}`);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789980009.616515,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_x1B1fuECeyMuq5pj1gjKIZxm","id":"ctco_01a0c320-b1f3-7170-84be-d803a7d4b220","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789980094.963911,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 20.7 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"/tmp/pr170-round12.qTceXS\nthread_pages=4\nthread_count=334\nnested_pending=0\npage_heads=\"9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\"\nreviews=232\nissue_comments=64\ninline_comments=376\nsnapshot_head=9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\nend={\"headRefOid\":\"9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f\",\"state\":\"OPEN\"}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0edc1009087d0a10f49588e8a8531","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"summary":[{"text":"**Analyzing new and unresolved threads**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Extracting and classifying recent unresolved threads**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_PaemUoBIpKuF9KNNC42n9zjx","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab0edcbd32087d089162256ddebe8ff","input":"const