{"timestamp":"2026-09-21T17:35:17.463Z","ordinal":6196,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b","turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d09eb4d8-c57a-492c-a3ce-9853f381dbea","process_id":"62335","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\"skill\":{\"workspaceSlug\":\"21n\",\"skillSlug\":\"pr-refetch-fix\"},\"version\":{\"selector\":\"semanticVersion\",\"semanticVersion\":\"1.2.0\"},\"caller\":{\"agentId\":\"codex-root\",\"agentName\":\"Codex\",\"modelProvider\":\"openai\",\"modelName\":\"gpt-5\",\"modelVersion\":\"2026-09-21\",\"clientName\":\"codex\",\"clientVersion\":\"1\",\"runId\":\"pr170-round13\",\"sessionId\":\"pr170-convergence\",\"conversationId\":\"pr170\"}}'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\"skill\":{\"workspaceSlug\":\"21n\",\"skillSlug\":\"pr-refetch-fix\"},\"version\":{\"selector\":\"semanticVersion\",\"semanticVersion\":\"1.2.0\"},\"caller\":{\"agentId\":\"codex-root\",\"agentName\":\"Codex\",\"modelProvider\":\"openai\",\"modelName\":\"gpt-5\",\"modelVersion\":\"2026-09-21\",\"clientName\":\"codex\",\"clientVersion\":\"1\",\"runId\":\"pr170-round13\",\"sessionId\":\"pr170-convergence\",\"conversationId\":\"pr170\"}}'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/<n>/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n> One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:b329f7e2-e25d-48f5-96d3-46e706d90380\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_CIs-V0wVrejP\"\n}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/<n>/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n> One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:b329f7e2-e25d-48f5-96d3-46e706d90380\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_CIs-V0wVrejP\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":7,"nanos":105295042},"formatted_output":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/<n>/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n> One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:b329f7e2-e25d-48f5-96d3-46e706d90380\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_CIs-V0wVrejP\"\n}\n"},"started_at_ms":1790012110356,"completed_at_ms":1790012117462}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-21T17:35:17.598Z","ordinal":6197,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0c509-525e-7cb2-afcd-f87d47911d29","call_id":"call_iaLqWz2KLph4ILwxIjsRZfUB","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 7.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/<n>/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n> One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:b329f7e2-e25d-48f5-96d3-46e706d90380\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_CIs-V0wVrejP\"\n}\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb","create_time":1790012117.598354}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
