The tests distinguish padded token68 from structured auth parameters and prove both accepted and colliding target-authored phases.\n   686\t- External fixture: `node scripts/test-mcpfn-external-server.mjs` passed against the real child server. A malformed `Host: [invalid` received 400, a subsequent `/not-found` request received 404, and the same process then completed two authenticated scenarios plus the expected unauthenticated failure.\n   687\t- Complete affected-package suite: all 244 `@mcpfn/testing` tests passed. The package typechecked and built successfully under Node 22; both changed `.mjs` files passed `node --check`.\n   688\t- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported dependency/package test, typecheck, and build; Playwright; the external authenticated server probe; mixed-entry redaction; official conformance; examples; ESM/CJS imports; dry-run packs; packed-consumer installation; and round trips. The first attempt identified only the ephemeral missing Playwright headless-shell cache; reinstalling the pinned runtime resolved that environment gap, and the unchanged batch passed.\n   689\t- `git diff --check` passed. Whole-batch review covered all five code/test files, their request/error boundaries, and the credential/report consumers. The ledger remained untracked and will not be staged or committed.\n   690\t- Required-check snapshot: the single pre-mutation snapshot contained 18 terminal contexts: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped; Sonar Quality Gate, CodeRabbit, Cubic, and Pullfrog were successful. No hosted checks or reviews were fetched after the snapshot.\n   691\t- Fix-induced regressions: none found by focused tests, the real fixture process, the complete testing package suite, typecheck/build, syntax checks, whole-diff review, or the release gate.\n   692\t\n   693\t## Round 13 convergence\n   694\t\n   695\t- Trend: improving but nonterminal. All 3 new review roots have coordinated repairs and no local regression remains, but this code-changing round cannot be clean and hosted reviewers have not assessed the resulting head.\n   696\t- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 3 new review roots / 0 deliberately deferred valid code roots, with hosted confirmation pending a future separately scheduled assessment.\n   697\t- New defects: token68/auth-param grammar ambiguity, missing fail-closed evidence for dynamic failure phases, and an unguarded external-fixture request parse.\n   698\t- Recurring families: credential grammar and structural safety recurred; both were repaired at their shared classification/evidence boundaries. The external fixture defect was repaired at the complete request boundary rather than only moving one expression.\n   699\t- Clean streak: `0` (reset by new/actionable roots, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the pending post-publication assessment).\n   700\t- Head stability before mutation: the complete snapshot stayed at `d12812dfae99cc0905aa93365f9b9ed5c42065f2` throughout assessment and local verification. Final local/remote/PR head evidence will be recorded after the one allowed push and one allowed comparison.\n   701\t\n   702\t## Round 13 completion\n   703\t\n   704\t- Commit / push: one commit `1da218dd32d80731fb3d59349e83bd58656f97d4` (`fix(mcpfn): close transport edge cases`) and one successful push through existing `origin` to `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`.\n   705\t- Head verification (one comparison only): local head, `origin/codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`, and PR 170 head all equal `1da218dd32d80731fb3d59349e83bd58656f97d4`; PR 170 remained open. The branch was `+0/-0`, and the only remaining worktree item was the untracked `.conduct/` ledger directory.\n   706\t- Head stability: the assessed head remained `d12812dfae99cc0905aa93365f9b9ed5c42065f2` through selection and verification; the single commit and push advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `1da218dd32d80731fb3d59349e83bd58656f97d4` in the one allowed post-push comparison.\n   707\t- Result: nonterminal recurring run. A future separately scheduled assessment must inspect the new head's complete review/check surfaces once; this code-changing round cannot count toward the clean streak.\n   708\t- Automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` remains disabled from the earlier retrieval-discovery stop and was not silently re-enabled during this manual round.\n   709\t- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab16ed4062487d091e64fd894087b30","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_ekqcjlLrqHEVVZGjyvnYeAws","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab16ed535b887d080bd8e82a2aa6bd4","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"nl -ba /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | sed -n '609,665p;1842,1875p'\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":9000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790013134.730343,"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_ekqcjlLrqHEVVZGjyvnYeAws","id":"ctco_01a0c518-fef8-7010-9bbd-89c4e558120b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790013144.824444,"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"   609\t# Task Group: September 15 Superfunctions PR #170 bounded remediation and dirty-dev reconciliation\n   610\tscope: Use for live hosted `pr-refetch-fix` work on McpFn PR #170, or scoped publication/reconciliation from a dirty, stale Superfunctions dev checkout.\n   611\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev and /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170; reuse_rule=PR threads, heads, hosted checks, and checkout state must be revalidated live; preserve unrelated dirty state.\n   612\t\n   613\t## Task 1: Harden PR #170 redaction/export invariants in a bounded hosted round, success\n   614\t\n   615\t### rollout_summary_files\n   616\t\n   617\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-15T12-55-27-j4bW-pr_170_bounded_redaction_cleanup_remediation.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/15/rollout-2026-09-15T18-25-27-01a0a522-f7e4-7422-a0fb-002c30b622cc.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-16T12:17:41+00:00, thread_id=01a0a522-f7e4-7422-a0fb-002c30b622cc, Node 22 release gate passed; PR remained DIRTY)\n   618\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-16T12-42-40-ubr7-pr170_refetch_fix_single_round_cleanup_contracts.md (cwd=/private/tmp/pr170-refetch-fix.h2yq4m, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/16/rollout-2026-09-16T18-12-40-01a0aa3d-a004-7df3-b812-85a576729d2d.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-16T15:30:05+00:00, thread_id=01a0aa3d-a004-7df3-b812-85a576729d2d, 1e45b794 pushed; PR API head stale)\n   619\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-15T10-22-33-ZW8E-pr_170_bounded_review_remediation.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/15/rollout-2026-09-15T15-52-33-01a0a496-fa30-7f70-ac42-1e7080963f28.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T11:26:48+00:00, thread_id=01a0a496-fa30-7f70-ac42-1e7080963f28, earlier bounded remediation and full release gate)\n   620\t\n   621\t### keywords\n   622\t\n   623\t- PR #170, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gate:mcpfn-release, McpFnRemoteCredentialLease, cleanupPhase, redaction, 8b23aaa, 1e45b794\n   624\t\n   625\t## Task 2: Push only generated Entire hooks, then reconcile stale dev safely, success\n   626\t\n   627\t### rollout_summary_files\n   628\t\n   629\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-15T13-26-45-jo2H-isolated_entire_hooks_push_and_dev_worktree_reconciliation.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/15/rollout-2026-09-15T18-56-46-01a0a53f-a1e6-7cc3-b676-4786df1a293f.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T14:02:01+00:00, thread_id=01a0a53f-a1e6-7cc3-b676-4786df1a293f, explicit allowlist pushed; unique SFNS-2 evidence preserved before fast-forward)\n   630\t\n   631\t### keywords\n   632\t\n   633\t- origin/dev, explicit allowlist, .factory/settings.json, .opencode/plugins/entire.ts, .pi/extensions/entire/index.ts, git merge --ff-only, SFNS-2, /usr/bin/trash\n   634\t\n   635\t## Task 3: Complete round 11 structural redaction and cleanup remediation, success/nonterminal\n   636\t\n   637\t### rollout_summary_files\n   638\t\n   639\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T10-00-27-D3ej-pr170_round11_structural_redaction_cleanup_remediation.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-30-27-01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-19T12:35:50+00:00, thread_id=01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b, one verified commit/push; convergence deferred)\n   640\t\n   641\t### keywords\n   642\t\n   643\t- 9ee45f4385f3b354f0e27396fe0e86dafa5ee41f, credential-redaction, structural-collision, cleanup-ownership, 332 threads, 93 unresolved, gate:mcpfn-release, .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\n   644\t\n   645\t## User preferences\n   646\t\n   647\t- when the user invokes `use-skillplane PR #170 pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e retrieve the exact live hosted workflow; make one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, verify the required head once, then stop without refetching reviews, polling CI, or merging. [Task 1]\n   648\t- when the user says “Commit and push directly to origin dev only those changes” -\u003e use a clean worktree and an explicit file allowlist; never stage the whole dirty checkout. [Task 2]\n   649\t- when the user asks what to preserve versus delete and then says “proceed” -\u003e classify every path, preserve unique evidence, and use recoverable Trash only for clearly obsolete artifacts. [Task 2]\n   650\t- for convergence requests, count root causes rather than comments, preserve deferred findings in a compact untracked ledger, and terminate after the one authorized hosted round. [Task 3]\n   651\t\n   652\t## Reusable knowledge\n   653\t\n   654\t- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub commands. Page every GraphQL `reviewThreads` cursor: PR #170’s first 100-thread result had `hasNextPage: true`; only a three-page, 284-thread snapshot was complete. [Task 1]\n   655\t- Treat credential-safe redaction, omission accounting, structural integrity, and replayable inspector exports as one contract. Reject variable-expanded and scrubbed duplicate keys before `Object.fromEntries()` silently drops data; never echo a resolved key that could be a credential; export a collision-free `${UPPER_CASE_VARIABLE}` marker or an explicitly incomplete safe artifact. [Task 1]\n   656\t- `npm run gate:mcpfn-release` is the validated Node 22 release gate. Local green gate and matching pushed heads do not prove hosted CI or merge readiness. [Task 1]\n   657\t- Track credential cleanup dynamically: revoke failures are `token-revocation`, disposal failures after successful revoke are `transport-close`; redact reconstructed fields including target `kind`, and keep CLI failure safe when cleanup has no report. [Task 1]\n   658\t- For a stale dirty dev checkout: fetch `origin/dev`, create a clean detached worktree, stage the exact allowlist, verify the commit parent equals the fetched tip, push `HEAD:dev`, verify remote SHA, and explain that the original checkout remains stale until separately reconciled. Compare apparent changes byte-for-byte to remote before treating them as unique. [Task 2]\n   659\t- Complete `reviewThreads` pagination and verify stable head before edits. In round 11, optional inspector keys were checked only when emitted, custom redactor/fallback safety was checked against final output, and suite projection/structure capture moved behind unconditional cleanup; Node 22 full `gate:mcpfn-release` passed before the sole commit/push. [Task 3]\n   660\t\n   661\t## Failures and how to do differently\n   662\t\n   663\t- Do not treat `hasNextPage: true` as a complete review snapshot, use Jest-only `--runInBand` with Vitest, weaken an invariant for a colliding fixture, or claim merge readiness while post-push checks remain pending. A branch-remote push can precede the PR API head; record that state as unverified/stale. [Task 1]\n   664\t- Do not reformat installer-managed generated hook files without authorization. Do not use `rm -rf` for cleanup; archive meaningful evidence and use `/usr/bin/trash` for recoverable deletion. [Task 2]\n   665\t- Do not declare convergence after a code-changing round: round 11 ended clean-streak 0 with PR open and reviewer/analyzer confirmation deferred. Update stale tests to reject unprovable structure, but validate an actually emitted dynamic fallback rather than unused candidates. [Task 3]\n  1842\t# Task Group: September 8-15 Superfunctions PR quality, issue-grounded review, and PR #170 convergence\n  1843\tscope: Use for broad read-only Superfunctions PR review, comparative findings/quality reporting, follow-up assessment, and root-cause remediation convergence across DataFn/UIFN/McpFn.\n  1844\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=review method is reusable, but exact heads, threads, CI, issue state, and merge readiness must be rechecked live.\n  1845\t\n  1846\t## Task 1: Review requested live Superfunctions PRs and diagnose remediation convergence, partial\n  1847\t\n  1848\t### rollout_summary_files\n  1849\t\n  1850\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-07T09-15-30-XKOk-pr_review_remediation_convergence_and_skill_amendment.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/07/rollout-2026-09-07T14-45-31-01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T07:43:24+00:00, thread_id=01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0, complete comparative report was not delivered; PR #170 symptom-patching convergence failure documented)\n  1851\t\n  1852\t### keywords\n  1853\t\n  1854\t- GH_CONFIG_DIR, gh pr list, PR #144, PR #145, PR #149, PR #150, PR #151, PR #152, PR #153, PR #155, PR #170, reviewThreads, Sonar, convergence, root-cause\n  1855\t\n  1856\t## Task 2: Evaluate PR #144 and its follow-up fixes, success\n  1857\t\n  1858\t### rollout_summary_files\n  1859\t\n  1860\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-08T07-39-19-NzVc-pr_144_code_quality_follow_up_review.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/08/rollout-2026-09-08T13-09-19-01a07ff5-06bb-73e2-8e7c-f4c4a0ffa83e.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-08T07:42:07+00:00, thread_id=01a07ff5-06bb-73e2-8e7c-f4c4a0ffa83e, final head not merge-ready)\n  1861\t\n  1862\t### keywords\n  1863\t\n  1864\t- PR #144, DataFn, null-normalization, aggregate-query, date-bounds, timezone, better-sqlite3, `TZ=Asia/Kolkata`, field-builders\n  1865\t\n  1866\t## Task 3: Evaluate PR #170 and all subsequent fixes, partial\n  1867\t\n  1868\t### rollout_summary_files\n  1869\t\n  1870\t- rollout_summaries/2026-09-15T06-58-36-nU0n-pr_170_subsequent_fixes_quality_review.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/15/rollout-2026-09-15T12-28-36-01a0a3dc-41d4-7c01-b6af-ad11344d98ef.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T07:58:13+00:00, thread_id=01a0a3dc-41d4-7c01-b6af-ad11344d98ef, read-only assessment was started but no final verdict delivered)\n  1871\t\n  1872\t### keywords\n  1873\t\n  1874\t- PR #170, MCP-1, transport-neutral conformance, aa08ef72e, reviewThreads(first:100), isOutdated, JUnit, --visible-tools, redaction, ESM/CJS, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n  1875\t\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab16edb128087d091b51d0badd99365","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"Round 13 is complete. The pinned hosted workflow was retrieved and verified through Skillplane via Composio.\n\n- **Fixed:** Three root causes: padded token68 credentials, missing fail-closed dynamic-phase coverage, and malformed Host handling in the external fixture.\n- **Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale):** 92 older unresolved threads retained their evidence-backed dispositions.\n- **Not Attempted:** 0 valid code root causes.\n- **Invalid / Accepted Risk:** Three Sonar `throw this` findings remain invalid because error identity owns cleanup retries. No accepted risks.\n- **Unassessed:** 0 snapshot items.\n- **Verification:** 132 focused tests and all 244 testing-package tests passed. The real external-server probe confirmed malformed Host → 400 and subsequent server survival. Typecheck, build, syntax checks, Playwright, official conformance, packaging, consumer round trips, and the complete Node 22 release gate passed.\n- **Commit:** `1da218dd32d80731fb3d59349e83bd58656f97d4`\n- **PR:** https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170\n- **Heads:** Local, `origin`, and PR head match; PR remains open.\n- **Convergence:** Improving but nonterminal. Valid blocking roots went from 3 to 0, with no detected regressions. Clean streak remains `0` because this was a code-changing round; a later scheduled assessment must evaluate the new head.\n- **Automation:** Remains disabled and was not silently re-enabled.\n\nThe updated handoff is [pr-170-remediation-ledger.md](/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170/.conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md:651).\n\nOne review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\n\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:609-665|note=[PR 170 bounded workflow, pagination, ledger, and convergence rules]\nMEMORY.md:1842-1875|note=[PR 170 broader convergence assessment context]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b\n01a0a522-f7e4-7422-a0fb-002c30b622cc\n01a0aa3d-a004-7df3-b812-85a576729d2d\n01a0a496-fa30-7f70-ac42-1e7080963f28\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab16eec973487d0ba9c46c33b2eb464","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1790013145.622526,"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ab-280c-7411-a12d-e3972c5f994d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1790056278.028145,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab2175db57887d0b1a2219727fbee6c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"I’m using `use-skillplane` to retrieve the hosted workflow live before any repository action. I’ll verify the pinned version/revision/digest again, then execute exactly one bounded PR 170 assessment with the existing ledger and no post-push review loop.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab21761a90087d0b8f1bceef29276c5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1790056281.754212,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_0zBvyE6gbY9F4nl1jzK1ipor","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab2176400d887d0a16d06850fc59f87","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/skillplane/SKILL.md \u0026\u0026 sed -n '1,280p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/composio-cli/SKILL.md \u0026\u0026 sed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.agents/skills/using-21nco-github/SKILL.md \u0026\u0026 rg -n \\\"PR #170|pr-refetch-fix|clean streak|convergence|GH_CONFIG_DIR\\\" /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -n 70\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056281.754212,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_0zBvyE6gbY9F4nl1jzK1ipor","id":"ctco_01a0c7ab-7c9f-7113-a311-e797e142c1e7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056299.679307,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---\nname: use-skillplane\ndescription: Use skillplane to resolve skill\n---\n\n# Skillplane\n\nSkillplane is user's hosted skills platform. Retrieve user's skill using the slug or skill name provided and use that to execute the requested task.\n\nNOTE: Always retrieve and read the hosted skill first before working on the task. If the skill is not found, return an error message indicating the skill could not be located.\n\n## Using skillplane\nTry these in this order to communicate with user's Skillplane for skill retrieval, creation, amend etc.\n1. Direct Skillplane MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Direct Skillplane CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n\n## Using tools mentioned in skillplane skills\nWhen executing a skill retrieved from Skillplane, use the tools referenced within the skill's definition to complete the task. Example tools: Linear, Notion, etc. Tool/Integrations resolution order:\n1. Direct integrations or MCP if available and authenticated\n2. Via Orca CLI if available and authenticated\n3. Via Composio CLI if available and authenticated\n4. Via Rex CLI if available and authenticated\n---\nname: composio-cli\ndescription: Help users operate the published Composio CLI to find the right tool, connect accounts, inspect schemas, execute tools, subscribe to trigger events with `composio listen`, script workflows with `composio run`, and call authenticated app APIs with `composio proxy`. Use when the user asks how to do something with `composio`, wants to run a known tool slug, needs to discover a slug with `composio search`, fix a missing connection with `composio link`, inspect tool inputs with `--get-schema` or `--dry-run`, troubleshoot top-level CLI flows, or explicitly needs `composio dev` guidance.\n---\n\n\u003c!-- AUTO-GENERATED: edit skills-src/composio-cli/index.ts and rebuild --\u003e\n\u003c!-- release-channel: stable --\u003e\n\n# Composio CLI\n\n## Default Workflow\n\n1. Start with `composio execute \u003cslug\u003e` whenever the slug is known.\n2. If several independent tool calls must happen at once, use `composio execute -p/--parallel` with repeated `\u003cslug\u003e -d \u003cjson\u003e` groups.\n3. If `execute` says the toolkit is not connected, run `composio link \u003ctoolkit\u003e` and retry.\n4. If the arguments are unclear, run `composio execute \u003cslug\u003e --get-schema` or `--dry-run` before guessing.\n5. Reach for `composio search \"\u003ctask\u003e\"` only when the slug is unknown. `search` accepts one or more queries, so batch related discovery work into a single command when useful.\n\n## `execute` - Run A Tool\n\nUse `execute` when the tool slug is already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER -d '{}'\n```\n\nInspect required inputs without executing:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --get-schema\n```\n\nPreview safely:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE --skip-connection-check --dry-run -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug report\", body: \"Steps to reproduce...\" }'\n```\n\nPass data from a file or stdin:\n```bash\ncomposio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d @issue.json\ncat issue.json | composio execute GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d -\n```\n\nUpload a local file:\n```bash\ncomposio execute SLACK_UPLOAD_OR_CREATE_A_FILE_IN_SLACK \\\n  --file ./image.png \\\n  -d '{ channels: \"C123\" }'\n```\n\nRun independent tool calls in parallel:\n```bash\ncomposio execute --parallel \\\n  GMAIL_SEND_EMAIL -d '{ recipient_email: \"a@b.com\", subject: \"Hi\" }' \\\n  GITHUB_CREATE_AN_ISSUE -d '{ owner: \"acme\", repo: \"app\", title: \"Bug\" }'\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--get-schema`: Inspect required arguments without executing the tool.\n- `--dry-run`: Preview the request shape without performing the action.\n- `--file`: Inject a local file path into a tool that exposes exactly one uploadable file argument.\n- `--parallel`: Execute multiple independent tool calls in the same invocation.\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id when multiple accounts exist for the same toolkit.\n\n- `--file` only works when the tool exposes a single uploadable file input. Otherwise use explicit `-d` JSON.\n\n## `search` - Find The Slug\n\nUse `search` only when the tool slug is not already known.\n\n```bash\ncomposio search \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"send an email\" --toolkits gmail\ncomposio search \"send an email\" \"create a github issue\"\ncomposio search \"my emails\" \"my github issues\" --toolkits gmail,github\n```\n\n- Batch related discovery work into one `search` invocation, then move back to `execute` once the correct slugs are known.\n\n## `link` - Connect An Account\n\nUse `link` when `execute` reports that a toolkit is not connected, or when the user explicitly wants to authorize an account.\n\n```bash\ncomposio link gmail\ncomposio link googlecalendar --no-browser\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--alias`: Assign an alias to the connected account. Required when creating an additional account for the same toolkit.\n\n- Retry the original `execute` command after linking succeeds.\n\n## `proxy` - Raw API Access\n\nUse `proxy` when a toolkit supports a raw API operation that is easier than finding a dedicated tool slug.\n\n```bash\ncomposio proxy https://api.github.com/user --toolkit github --account work --method GET \u003c/dev/null\n```\n\nKey flags:\n- `--account`: Select which connected account to use by alias, word_id, or account id.\n\n## `run` - Scripting, LLMs, and Programmatic Workflows\n\nFor programmatic calls, loops, output plumbing, or anything beyond a single tool call, prefer `composio run`.\n\n`composio run` executes an inline ESM JavaScript/TypeScript snippet with authenticated `execute()`, `search()`, `proxy()`, and the experimental `experimental_subAgent()` helper pre-injected. No SDK setup required.\n\nChain multiple tools:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const me = await execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\");\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 1 });\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, fetchedEmails: !!emails.data });\n'\n```\n\nFan out with Promise.all:\n```bash\ncomposio run '\n  const [me, emails] = await Promise.all([\n    execute(\"GITHUB_GET_THE_AUTHENTICATED_USER\"),\n    execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 }),\n  ]);\n  console.log({ login: me.data.login, emailCount: emails.data.messages?.length });\n'\n```\n\nFeed tool output into an LLM and get structured JSON back:\n```bash\ncomposio run --logs-off '\n  const emails = await execute(\"GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS\", { max_results: 5 });\n  const brief = await experimental_subAgent(\n    `Summarize these emails and count them.\\n\\n${emails.prompt()}`,\n    { schema: z.object({ summary: z.string(), count: z.number() }) }\n  );\n  console.log(brief.structuredOutput);\n'\n```\n\n- Use top-level `execute --parallel` instead when the user only needs a few independent tool calls and does not need script logic.\n\n## Auth\n\n```bash\ncomposio whoami   # check current session\ncomposio login    # authenticate if whoami fails\n```\n\n## Escalate Only When Needed\n\nIf the user is stuck on top-level commands or needs fallback inspection commands, load [references/troubleshooting.md](references/troubleshooting.md).\n\nIf the user explicitly asks about developer projects, auth configs, connected accounts, triggers, logs, orgs, or projects, load [references/composio-dev.md](references/composio-dev.md). `composio dev` is not the default end-user path.\n---\nname: using-21nco-github\ndescription: Uses the thyaravind GitHub account for repositories under dev/n or with a 21nCo origin. Use for any GitHub CLI, pull request, issue, release, or repository operation in those repositories.\n---\n\n# Using 21nCo GitHub\n\nFor any repository whose path is under `dev/n` or whose Git remote belongs to `21nCo`:\n\n- Run every `gh` command with `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`.\n- Verify `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh auth status` reports `thyaravind` as the active account before making changes.\n- Never use the default GitHub account or silently fall back to another account.\n- Use the existing `21nCo` remote for pushes. If `thyaravind` lacks permission, stop and report the denial instead of creating a fork or alternate remote.\n251:- 21nCo/super-functions, PR #183, PR #184, PR #185, PR #186, PR #187, PR #188, PR #189, PR #198, GH_CONFIG_DIR, reviewThreads(first:100), isResolved, isOutdated, SonarCloud, Node 18\n269:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for this private repository. Query paginated GraphQL `pullRequest.reviewThreads(first:100, after:$cursor)`; current unresolved (`isResolved == false \u0026\u0026 isOutdated == false`) threads are blockers, while outdated unresolved threads are separate. [Task 1]\n279:scope: Use when creating or verifying recurring Orca automations for a PR remediation workflow, especially when the user requests bounded convergence rather than an open-ended loop.\n282:## Task 1: Create a recurring PR #170 bounded remediation automation, success\n286:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T09-30-34-zqBz-create_pr_fix_automation_and_reusable_pr170_convergence_sche.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-00-34-01a0b3da-775b-7a23-99f7-756289048919.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-18T10:24:08+00:00, thread_id=01a0b3da-775b-7a23-99f7-756289048919, automation created and read back)\n290:- Orca, orca automations create, --workspace-mode existing, --reuse-session, pr-refetch-fix, PR-170, rrule, Asia/Kolkata\n296:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T09-30-34-zqBz-create_pr_fix_automation_and_reusable_pr170_convergence_sche.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-00-34-01a0b3da-775b-7a23-99f7-756289048919.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-18T10:24:08+00:00, thread_id=01a0b3da-775b-7a23-99f7-756289048919, thin pinned-dependency skill published)\n304:- when the user requested `every 30 min $use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix PR 170 until convergence` -\u003e authorize one bounded remediation round per activation with explicit convergence/self-disable rules, not an open-ended review loop. [Task 1]\n306:- when the user said “refer existing pr-refetch-fix skill instead of copying” and required existing-session reuse -\u003e compose against the pinned hosted skill and make `--reuse-session` an invariant. [Task 2]\n318:# Task Group: September 18 Superfunctions PR #179 and #133 blocked convergence assessments\n319:scope: Use for one bounded hosted remediation/convergence assessment when the remaining queue exposes cross-cutting root causes; preserve the untracked ledger and disable automation when coordinated repair is not safe.\n322:## Task 1: Assess PR #179 convergence without symptom patches, partial\n326:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T11-00-28-3lkV-sfns_4_pr179_convergence_blocked_assessment.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/SFNS-4, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T16-30-28-01a0b42c-c4ea-7b61-a3b7-c7fffb0ed31d.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-18T11:13:16+00:00, thread_id=01a0b42c-c4ea-7b61-a3b7-c7fffb0ed31d, broad root-cause queue; no mutation)\n330:- PR #179, SFNS-4, pr-refetch-fix, 321 threads, 60 current unresolved, root-causes, SonarQube, pr179-remediation-ledger.json, automation disabled\n332:## Task 2: Assess PR #133 convergence, disable automation, and set dependency-gated next steps, partial\n336:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T11-00-30-gEnj-pr133_convergence_blocked_next_steps.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-133, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T16-30-30-01a0b42c-ce2e-7173-9c5a-e6070f3586d5.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-21T08:40:34+00:00, thread_id=01a0b42c-ce2e-7173-9c5a-e6070f3586d5, no mutation; dependency-gated plan and automation disablement verified)\n340:- PR #133, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0, revision-4, digest, config-loader-semantics, dev-watch-output-ancestor, search-scope-override-validation, PR #170, PR #171, remediation-ledger\n345:- when requesting convergence, count deduplicated root causes, inspect recurring/deferred families and shared invariants, preserve a compact untracked ledger, and disable automation when repair is blocked. [Task 1][Task 2]\n356:- Do not infer convergence from an earlier local zero count or classify unassessed threads as fixed/stale. When coordinated repair cannot be established, stop without edits/commits/pushes and retain the ledger. [Task 1][Task 2]\n372:- git worktree list --porcelain, gh pr list, GH_CONFIG_DIR, 21nCo/super-functions, 21nCo/superfunctions, docs/http-decode-params, sfns-4-rex-upstream, prunable-worktree, PR #188, PR #179, PR #170\n390:- Derive the repository slug from `git remote -v`: this checkout is `21nCo/super-functions`, not `21nCo/superfunctions`. For every `dev/n` GitHub command use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`. [Task 1]\n392:- At the September 17 snapshot, `superfunctions-dev`/`docs/http-decode-params` matched clean PR #188; SFNS-4/`sfns-4-rex-upstream` matched dirty PR #179; `/private/tmp/pr170-refetch-fix.h2yq4m` was missing and prunable, associated with PR #170. [Task 1]\n508:- pr-review-fix, PR #38, SKI-17, @datafn/server@0.1.3, cursor-pagination, Date, HTTP-serialization, createCursorStableReadAdapter, combined, regional, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n519:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` as `thyaravind` for `dev/n`/21nCo GitHub work; do not fork if permissions fail. [Task 1]\n550:- SFNS-4, PR #179, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, adapterNamespace, Retry-After, tenantId, SecFn readJson, e200f50d, 511c02a\n640:# Task Group: September 15 Superfunctions PR #170 bounded remediation and dirty-dev reconciliation\n641:scope: Use for live hosted `pr-refetch-fix` work on McpFn PR #170, or scoped publication/reconciliation from a dirty, stale Superfunctions dev checkout.\n644:## Task 1: Harden PR #170 redaction/export invariants in a bounded hosted round, success\n654:- PR #170, pr-refetch-fix, reviewThreads, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gate:mcpfn-release, McpFnRemoteCredentialLease, cleanupPhase, redaction, 8b23aaa, 1e45b794\n670:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-18T10-00-27-D3ej-pr170_round11_structural_redaction_cleanup_remediation.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-30-27-01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-19T12:35:50+00:00, thread_id=01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b, one verified commit/push; convergence deferred)\n678:- when the user invokes `use-skillplane PR #170 pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e retrieve the exact live hosted workflow; make one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, verify the required head once, then stop without refetching reviews, polling CI, or merging. [Task 1]\n681:- for convergence requests, count root causes rather than comments, preserve deferred findings in a compact untracked ledger, and terminate after the one authorized hosted round. [Task 3]\n685:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub commands. Page every GraphQL `reviewThreads` cursor: PR #170’s first 100-thread result had `hasNextPage: true`; only a three-page, 284-thread snapshot was complete. [Task 1]\n696:- Do not declare convergence after a code-changing round: round 11 ended clean-streak 0 with PR open and reviewer/analyzer confirmation deferred. Update stale tests to reject unprovable structure, but validate an actually emitted dynamic fallback rather than unused candidates. [Task 3]\n710:- PR #584, --match-head-commit, GH_CONFIG_DIR, thyaravind, git worktree remove --force, 8c7cb4da, 2321c49d, nucleus-review-fixes\n718:- Verify exact PR head and matching worktree branch/head, tracked and untracked state, and active cwd processes before removal. The correct merge command used `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh pr merge 584 --repo 21nCo/nucleum --squash --match-head-commit \u003chead\u003e`. [Task 1]\n915:- git worktree list --porcelain, PR #584, GH_CONFIG_DIR, unpushed commits, client/layout, client/application, Focus timer, DataFn migration, 5ed651b5\n949:- PR #23, reviewThreads(first:100), GH_CONFIG_DIR, pr-refetch-fix, projections, SQLite writer lock, SkillEditor, CompositionPanel, SonarQube, 7715373, 83058246\n1095:- git worktree remove --force, SFNS-3, ReviewFn, PR #177, lsof, .conduct, git worktree list --porcelain, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n1122:- splane, PR #174, PR #177, SFNS-3, OAuth invalid_grant, Composio CLI, 21n/pr-quality-analysis@1.0.0, pr-refetch-fix, gate:reviewfn-release, package-lock, d4de3791\n1192:- PR #15, PR #17, PR #18, PR #19, reviewThreads, exact-head, AuthFn 0.2, no post-push polling, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n1387:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-10T14-20-26-2rMD-mcp1_transport_neutral_conformance_pr_170.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/10/rollout-2026-09-10T19-50-26-01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-10T15:17:36+00:00, thread_id=01a08bb0-f9fe-7951-aed1-965679939fc3, PR #170 local release gate passed; hosted CI queued)\n1482:- SKI-9, PR #14, mdfn, reviewThreads, exact-head, 0f7e368, pnpm mdfn:verify, Sonar, new_duplicated_lines_density, GH_CONFIG_DIR, git worktree remove\n1503:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub operations here. Merge only with `gh pr merge ... --match-head-commit \u003cverified-sha\u003e` after local/remote head equality and current mergeability/check review. [Task 2]\n1873:# Task Group: September 8-15 Superfunctions PR quality, issue-grounded review, and PR #170 convergence\n1874:scope: Use for broad read-only Superfunctions PR review, comparative findings/quality reporting, follow-up assessment, and root-cause remediation convergence across DataFn/UIFN/McpFn.\n1877:## Task 1: Review requested live Superfunctions PRs and diagnose remediation convergence, partial\n1881:- rollout_summaries/2026-09-07T09-15-30-XKOk-pr_review_remediation_convergence_and_skill_amendment.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/07/rollout-2026-09-07T14-45-31-01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-15T07:43:24+00:00, thread_id=01a07b26-bb2c-7b32-95a4-8fb6b484fad0, complete comparative report was not delivered; PR #170 symptom-patching convergence failure documented)\n1885:- GH_CONFIG_DIR, gh pr list, PR #144, PR #145, PR #149, PR #150, PR #151, PR #152, PR #153, PR #155, PR #170, reviewThreads, Sonar, convergence, root-cause\n1897:## Task 3: Evaluate PR #170 and all subsequent fixes, partial\n1905:- PR #170, MCP-1, transport-neutral conformance, aa08ef72e, reviewThreads(first:100), isOutdated, JUnit, --visible-tools, redaction, ESM/CJS, GH_CONFIG_DIR\n1910:- when the user asks to evaluate “PR #170 and all subsequent PR fixes” -\u003e assess both history and exact current head, enumerate all commits/review surfaces/checks, and finish the requested verdict before pursuing unrelated PR material. [Task 3]\n1911:- when remediation repeatedly finds adjacent variants, the user asked to “strive for convergence and broader look” -\u003e reset to the shared invariant/subsystem rather than publish another one-example symptom patch. [Task 1]\n1917:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`; narrow GitHub queries, fetch exact heads, inspect targeted diffs/logs, and classify CI, source inspection, and runtime probes as distinct evidence. A bounded review collection does not cap analysis completeness: when related blockers recur, inspect sibling paths and repair the shared invariant before the one authorized publication step. [Task 1][Task 2]\n1921:- For PR #170, paginate `pullRequest.reviewThreads(first:100,after:$endCursor)` and separate `isOutdated` from current unresolved threads; snapshot base/head OIDs, commits, local exact-head source, and hosted checks independently. The captured head `aa08ef72e` was open/mergeable but `UNSTABLE`, with 34 current unresolved threads and 10 pending checks; do not call it merge-ready from focused tests or resolved threads. [Task 3]\n2365:- splane, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #133, DocsFn, GH_CONFIG_DIR, release-gate, OAuth, authorization-server issuer, @next/swc-darwin-arm64, SvelteKit\n2381:- for `pr-refetch-fix` -\u003e perform one snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push round; do not refetch reviews or poll CI afterward. [Task 1]\n2385:- Include unresolved GraphQL review threads, review bodies, issue comments, and inline comments in one snapshot; validate each finding against the exact current head before editing. Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` for GitHub operations. [Task 1]\n2431:- splane, pr-refetch-fix, PR #139, applyOmit, applyOmitToArrayValue, nested arrays, userId, datafn/server/src/execution/query/select.ts, ff833823\n2451:- gh-other, GH_CONFIG_DIR, gh pr merge, --match-head-commit, MERGEABLE, CLEAN, fc5243995f6b48f2d1de8f112333e92e7971bbc8, origin/dev\n2455:- when the user invokes `splane` -\u003e resolve the exact hosted Skillplane workflow live; this rollout used `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1` rather than local instructions. [Task 1]\n2462:- Use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other` and the exact PR worktree. Before merging, confirm the approved live head and use `gh pr merge \u003cnumber\u003e --merge --match-head-commit \u003csha\u003e`; verify the merged head is an ancestor of `origin/dev`. [Task 3]\n2532:- splane, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #569, OAuth refresh credentials, authorization server issuer, codex mcp logout skillplane, codex mcp login skillplane, sslmode, sslrootcert, resolveClientIp, 400ee32b\n2552:- 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #569, reviewThreads, 49d13ae6, merge-async, gh-other, 9971f77, SonarCloud, CircleCI, Vercel\n2564:- A `pr-refetch-fix` invocation is one review snapshot, narrow fixes, proportionate verification, at most one commit/push, and one head comparison; preserve a divergent local PR branch under a backup ref and use a clean exact-head worktree. PR #569 fixed duplicate `sslmode`/`sslrootcert` values with `URL.searchParams.getAll(...)`, and supplied Node AuthFn `resolveClientIp` preferring `cf-connecting-ip`, then first `x-forwarded-for`. [Task 1]\n2597:- splane pr refetch fix, PR #570, reviewThreads, GraphQL, df11df18, merge-async, direct_merge, f849c684, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1\n2687:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-29T14-45-30-EDVy-ski_13_multi_region_review_fixes.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/29/rollout-2026-08-29T20-15-30-01a04dfb-9d6e-7000-a3ce-3006e2ed565c.jsonl, updated_at=2026-09-01T15:20:17+00:00, thread_id=01a04dfb-9d6e-7000-a3ce-3006e2ed565c, 31 intended files pushed once; full integration retained unrelated attribution failures and PR-head convergence was unverified)\n2721:- Full integration retained two caller-attribution expectation failures (`Authenticated OAuth client`/`unknown` versus `Codex`/`gpt-5.6`); treat them as outside the SKI-13 delta unless another fix round is authorized. After pushing, local/remote matched `a52f368` but the immediate PR API still showed `cbac7dd`; do not claim PR-head convergence without a permitted later check. [Task 1]\n2857:- splane pr-review-fix, 21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.0.1, PR #567, PR #568, 69723719, 9c576dc, 41e1efbb, reviewThreads, legacy-product-keys, svelte-check\n2899:- sf-next/next, origin/dev, git worktree add -b, DocsFn, MailFn, MDFN, SendFn, release-packages.json, package-lock.json, GH_CONFIG_DIR, PR #130, PR #131, PR #132, PR #133\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab2176d9ca487d0b570301a5ea21c5a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_vTZnhNeNB362rT1jQ34OKbqo","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab21770357087d0b966200284b806b1","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"command -v skillplane || true; command -v splane || true; command -v composio || true; command -v rex || true\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":2000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056300.474798,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_vTZnhNeNB362rT1jQ34OKbqo","id":"ctco_01a0c7ab-9bb0-73b0-a03e-b029dff1a8a1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056307.632817,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"/Users/serro/.local/bin/composio\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"content":[{"text":"Direct Skillplane MCP and local Skillplane CLIs are unavailable, so the skill is directing me to the authenticated Composio fallback. I’m retrieving `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0` live and will stop without repository mutation if revision 4 or the pinned digest has drifted.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab21777c29c87d0b427cf85ba8304ac","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1790056308.246899,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_mtpo29tJ4oaxeWsuArLitroB","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab2177a7adc87d0aa02bec76257eeed","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\"skill\\\":{\\\"workspaceSlug\\\":\\\"21n\\\",\\\"skillSlug\\\":\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\"},\\\"version\\\":{\\\"selector\\\":\\\"semanticVersion\\\",\\\"semanticVersion\\\":\\\"1.2.0\\\"},\\\"caller\\\":{\\\"agentId\\\":\\\"codex-root\\\",\\\"agentName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"modelProvider\\\":\\\"openai\\\",\\\"modelName\\\":\\\"gpt-5\\\",\\\"modelVersion\\\":\\\"2026-09-22\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"codex\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"1\\\",\\\"runId\\\":\\\"pr170-round14\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\",\\\"conversationId\\\":\\\"pr170\\\"}}'\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056308.246899,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_mtpo29tJ4oaxeWsuArLitroB","id":"ctco_01a0c7ab-f018-7c03-bb04-06dc1646a1ee","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790056329.240435,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 8.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/\u003cn\u003e/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n\u003e One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:d374061f-9b47-48d4-822a-f7c1502bd3ab\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_u1eZTHDWkvAY\"\n}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016ab2178c753487d0acb55f88bf3d9e94","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The hosted workflow still matches exactly: semantic version `1.2.0`, revision `4`, and the pinned digest. I’m validating the dedicated worktree, branch, full Git state, 