, convergence trend, and clean-streak state.\n- Because the user requested convergence, perform the hosted workflow's broader subsystem assessment immediately on the first scheduled run before selecting a patch. State the shared invariant, producers/consumers, failure boundaries, coordinated repair, and verification criteria in the ledger. On later reset triggers, switch the ledger mode to assessment and reassess before further mutation. Do not publish another symptom patch if a complete repair cannot be established.\n- Count root causes rather than comments. Preserve older valid blockers and deferred findings. Never infer convergence from missing history, an outdated hunk, resolved thread count, test count, or one clean snapshot.\n\nConvergence and stopping rules:\n\n1. Reset the clean streak whenever the complete snapshot has an Actionable defect, a valid Not Attempted code defect, a new defect, a recurring family, a fix-induced regression, a changed PR head between assessments, or any required CI/reviewer check is pending or failing in the single allowed immediate check snapshot.\n2. A clean assessment requires: no code change or push in that round; zero Actionable root causes; zero valid Not Attempted code root causes; no unassessed review items; no recurring/deferred blocker; the PR head stayed stable; and all required CI/reviewer checks are terminal and successful in the one allowed immediate snapshot.\n3. Declare convergence only after two consecutive clean scheduled assessments at the same PR head. Then update the ledger, disable automation `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82` with `orca automations edit 4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82 --disabled --json`, verify with `orca automations show 4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82 --json`, report convergence, and stop.\n4. If PR 170 is merged or closed, record the terminal state, disable and verify this automation, report that the schedule stopped, and stop without mutation.\n5. If a reset-trigger assessment cannot establish a complete coordinated repair, or an external dependency blocks further progress, record the blocker, disable and verify this automation so it cannot repeat unsafe mutations, report the blocker, and stop.\n\nEnd every nonterminal run with the hosted workflow's explicit result buckets and: `One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.`","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b4d1-8e3d-7fa0-aff8-3c98a86b009a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789740027.453582,"turn_id":"01a0b4d1-89b2-74e3-a6c3-9629e0e8826a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"skillplane should be accessible via Composio CLI - retry","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b4f9-6516-7721-ab05-6fcbef4ee5dc","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789742638.358609,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Why is the automation disabled and what is the current state?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b89c-bcf6-75a3-a3be-d160db8b1c2b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789803674.870486,"turn_id":"01a0b89c-b76f-7790-a974-cf2788b054e3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"whats the convergence state?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b906-a4cb-7512-907c-42a7f5a06786","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789810615.499491,"turn_id":"01a0b906-9ffe-70d2-a60f-bf5df14e1e07"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what does the deferred family contain?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b90d-78e4-7c10-9f7f-fe2191a86db2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789811063.0122,"turn_id":"01a0b90d-72f4-76d0-8550-1d4d61d74375"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"proceed with the next recommended step","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b914-b440-7902-aa9f-04757b7c4d66","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789811536.960287,"turn_id":"01a0b914-adb8-75d2-a690-fafe68c720b9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"proceed with the next recommended step","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b91a-0892-7e41-b2c0-4e69b2a09551","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789811886.226737,"turn_id":"01a0b91a-027b-7173-bd75-a52040008bc7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"what's next","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b992-0a25-7df2-b3b5-c2b48a8c0525","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789819750.949395,"turn_id":"01a0b992-00c3-7a93-91f5-56dd7dab8b34"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"proceed","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0b992-a13c-7c10-a938-ada655598277","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789819789.628867,"turn_id":"01a0b992-9248-7613-95d3-df05acb573b9"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c31e-70a0-7731-96e3-a8145486556a","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789979947.168672,"turn_id":"01a0c31e-6a96-7383-bda9-a191f2448a61"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c3f2-094d-7540-9c7d-a42eae9d90dc","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789993814.349076,"turn_id":"01a0c3f2-008a-7f12-b07c-9f0dbdb39b21"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c508-5c25-7dd1-b0cc-6f5684aa715b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1790012054.56565,"turn_id":"01a0c508-53ab-7801-a347-b8e3419372fb"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -\u003e specific):\n\n- /Users/serro/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- /Users/serro/.codex/memories/skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   /Users/serro/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally \u003c= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n\u003coai-mem-citation\u003e\n\u003ccitation_entries\u003e\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n\u003c/citation_entries\u003e\n\u003crollout_ids\u003e\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n\u003c/rollout_ids\u003e\n\u003c/oai-mem-citation\u003e\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `\u003cfile\u003e:\u003cline_start\u003e-\u003cline_end\u003e|note=[\u003chow memory was used\u003e]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `\u003crollout_ids\u003e` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in /Users/serro/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `\u003ctimestamp\u003e-\u003cshort slug\u003e.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in /Users/serro/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nWorks across Superfunctions, Nucleus, Skillplane, btnextjs, and local agent-workspace projects on macOS/zsh. Values explicit authorization boundaries, semantic architecture, preserved dirty worktrees, and live PR/issue evidence. Expects focused/local validation to be clearly separated from unverified browser, hosted CI, deployment, migration, or acceptance layers. Uses hosted Skillplane workflows and direct Orca CLI where applicable.\n\n## User preferences\n\n- “Thorough read-only code review” means snapshot committed, staged, unstaged, untracked, and branch-relative state; trace contracts, permissions, and security; report only—never edit, format, stage, commit, push, deploy, or create artifacts.\n- Preserve dirty worktrees and the index; distinguish the local overlay from pushed/committed branch changes. After explicit “fix the remaining issues,” make only local changes unless publication is separately authorized.\n- For PR usefulness/mergeability, deliver a decision-oriented per-PR table with exact head, checks, current unresolved findings, and recommended action; do not drift beyond the requested range.\n- For bounded hosted PR remediation, retrieve the exact live workflow, make one complete snapshot/fix/verify/commit/push pass, then stop without post-push refetch, CI polling, merge, or extra loop.\n- `splane \u003cskill\u003e` means retrieve/follow the exact live hosted Skillplane skill: direct MCP first, Composio CLI fallback, never cached local instructions. [ad-hoc note]\n- Prefer semantic colocation over mechanism-based god-directories such as `stores/`, `state/`, or `shared`.\n- For native Outright planning/backlog artifacts, keep the capability native, omit an explicitly excluded external project name, and do not imply implementation authorization.\n- When a requested backlog item likely overlaps an existing issue or has no resolved project/destination, clarify whether to create or extend before mutating Linear.\n- Treat Linear numbering as creation order unless separately shown to be dependency-aware implementation order.\n- Use a non-Serro personal Aside Browser identity, never Serro/u1/`aravind@serro.ai`, unless explicitly overridden. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## General Tips\n\n- Search `MEMORY.md` by cwd, PR/issue, command, error, or API; requery mutable heads, threads, checks, worktrees, browser state, and deployment state live.\n- In 21nCo/dev/n GitHub repos use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`; derive the exact repo slug from remotes.\n- Treat green focused tests as focused evidence only; name unverified browser, cloud, migration, native, production, or acceptance layers plainly.\n- For runtime-owned subprocesses, test queued authorization immediately before spawn, selected-context race guards, process-group shutdown, and dev-server disposal—not only unit paths.\n- For recurring Orca automations, use direct `orca automations` CLI, `--workspace-mode existing --reuse-session`, pinned hosted dependencies, and stored-config read-back.\n- Architecture checks are structural evidence, not semantic or runtime proof; for ownership/deletion work inspect responsibility, callers, dynamic/external entry points, and lifecycle semantics.\n\n## What's in Memory\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs\n\n#### 2026-09-21\n\n- Gantt, milestone, risk, and authorization local-change review: `getApiContext`, `X-Organization-ID`, `findActiveMembership`, `private_v2`, `programRelevanceFilter`, `program_risks_changed`\n  - desc: Read-only review of the staging local overlay; search first for the release-blocking cross-organization route gap and related visibility/cache defects.\n  - learnings: Newly member-readable routes require active-membership validation; “not private” leaks `private_v2`/`draft`; socket updates must refresh Gantt DTOs.\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/outright\n\n#### 2026-09-21\n\n- Outright Orca-parity review and runtime/UI remediation: `onorca.dev`, `agent-manager`, `process groups`, `terminal switching`, `Vite closeBundle`, `OUT-1`, `OUT-32`\n  - desc: Local-first ADE parity surface, P1 race/shutdown remediation, and dependency-aware Linear implementation order.\n  - learnings: Revalidate trust/target before queued spawn and await process-group/Vite shutdown; local tests/build/browser fixtures are not paid-provider or production proof.\n\n- Native durable messaging backlog capture: `native messaging`, `agent conversations`, `SQLite`, `OUT-7`, `add-to-backlog`, `oauth_token_invalid_grant`\n  - desc: Search for evidence-backed Outright conversation-to-conversation message scope and the paused hosted-Linear workflow.\n  - learnings: The required result is one Backlog issue plus linked rationale/delivery documents and read-back; ask separate issue versus extend `OUT-7` before writes.\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-133\n\n#### 2026-09-21\n\n- PR #133 blocked convergence and next steps: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`, `revision 4`, `config-loader-semantics`, `PR #170`, `PR #171`, `remediation-ledger`\n  - desc: Exact hosted bounded-round procedure, complete review snapshot, disabled automation, and dependency-gated loader plan.\n  - learnings: Do not symptom-patch the loader or call convergence while `DIRTY`; finish #170/#171, rebase, then run a maintained-loader compatibility spike before coordinated repair.\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus\n\n#### 2026-09-21\n\n- Client ownership consolidation and organization leftovers: `client/utils`, `shell/layers`, `actionMap`, `session.store`, `capture.store`, `check:architecture`, `type-ownership`\n  - desc: Semantic ownership migration plus a read-only scan of remaining catch-alls, misleading owners, split candidates, naming, and cleanup leads.\n  - learnings: Split `client/utils` and remote facades by responsibility; zero-incoming imports and passing architecture checks do not prove safe deletion or semantic placement.\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n\n#### 2026-09-19\n\n- PR #183-#199 usefulness and mergeability review: `reviewThreads(first:100)`, `isOutdated`, `SonarCloud`, `GH_CONFIG_DIR`\n  - desc: Authenticated live-review method, captured blockers, and the missed comparative-report handoff.\n  - learnings: Separate threads, checks, draft status, and `MERGEABLE`; deliver the requested table and stop at the requested range.\n\n#### 2026-09-18\n\n- Scheduled PR remediation automation and PR #179 convergence: `orca automations`, `--reuse-session`, `pr179-remediation-ledger.json`, `root-causes`\n  - desc: Direct-CLI bounded recurring repair and safe stop rules for broad unresolved queues.\n  - learnings: Pin dependencies, reuse sessions, classify root causes rather than comments, and self-disable when safely blocked.\n\n### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs-two\n\n#### 2026-09-18\n\n- Notification-center read-only review: `serro:status:incidents:v1`, `invoice.payment_failed`, `started_at`, `listSweepableAnnouncementsDb`\n  - desc: DB-authoritative Redis, Stripe, producer, seed, cursor, billing, and bulk-operation review.\n  - learnings: Focused tests do not establish environment isolation or producer/migration safety; preserve timestamp precision and page beyond 100 rows.\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs\n\n- SRO-800 and program UI reviews/remediation: `program_widget`, `KGQATurnContextType`, `retryProgramRiskRun`, `RiskCloseDialog`, `Node 22`\n  - desc: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/repos/btnextjs. Strict dirty-overlay review, local-only fixes, risk/milestone contracts, and baseline-noisy validation.\n\n#### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/workflows\n\n- Browser archives and Daily/Weekly Contem: `Ar@fyweb`, `archive/tmp`, `Daily Contem`, `source-first`, `byte-identical`\n  - desc: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/workflows. Chrome-profile preflight, validated public-feed capture, and self-contained reporting.\n\n#### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/skillplane\n\n- Skillplane PR, Linear, and release workflows: `splane`, `reviewThreads`, `createCursorStableReadAdapter`, `SKI-17`\n  - desc: Hosted workflow retrieval, bounded remediation, published-contract checks, release evidence, and worktree safety.\n\n#### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/{superfunctions,superfunctions-dev,worktrees/superfunctions/*}\n\n- Superfunctions implementation, release gates, and worktree safety: `gate:mcpfn-release`, `DataFn`, `AuthFn`, `McpFn`, `ReviewFn`, `git worktree`\n  - desc: Package contracts, exact-head PR remediation/merge, release validation, and guarded cleanup across Superfunctions checkouts.\n\n#### /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus\n\n- Nucleus migration, account-service, and Playwright evidence: `DataFn`, `AuthFn`, `account-service`, `Playwright`, `migration`\n  - desc: Migration audits, regression matrices, browser-suite inventory, and precise limits of focused validation.\n\n#### Other scoped archives\n\n- Reporting, Router, Diffpanel, public-source, and cross-repo reviews: `reporting`, `Router`, `Diffpanel`, `Conductor`, `Ask Serro`, `TARGET_ENV=test`\n  - desc: Search `MEMORY.md` by exact cwd and task phrase for scheduled reports, public-site audits, review artifacts, and browser-backed captures.\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/serro/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/serro/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r4` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/cloudflare/0.1.2/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/figma/11.0.0/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/notion/0.1.8/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r9` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/product-design/0.1.55/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sonarqube/2.5.0/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r12` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.909.61513/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Co (file: r2/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding a (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by defa (file: r2/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from anothe (file: r2/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- aside-browser: Read when you need a browser, or have to work across user's logged-in websites (e.g. Gmail, Slack, cloud consoles, etc.), or have to refer personal contexts like memory and browsing history. (file: r1/aside-browser/SKILL.md)\n- check-codex-resets: Check a signed-in Codex Desktop account's current Codex usage windows, rate-limit reset times, banked reset-credit count, and reset-credit expiry dates. Use when the user asks how many Codex (file: r0/check-codex-resets/SKILL.md)\n- chronicle: Allows you to view the user's screen as well as several hours of history. Use when the user makes a reference to their recent work, for which it'd be helpful to see the screen. This skill MUST (file: r0/chronicle/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:agents-sdk: Build AI agents on Cloudflare Workers using the Agents SDK. Load when creating stateful agents, durable workflows, real-time WebSocket apps, scheduled tasks, MCP servers, or chat applications (file: r4/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare: Builds AI agents on Cloudflare using the Agents SDK with state management, real-time WebSockets, scheduled tasks, tool integration, and chat capabilities. Generates production-ready agent c (file: r4/building-ai-agent-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare: Builds remote MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers on Cloudflare Workers with tools, OAuth authentication, and production deployment. Generates server code, configures auth providers, and d (file: r4/building-mcp-server-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:cloudflare: Comprehensive Cloudflare platform skill covering Workers, Pages, storage (KV, D1, R2), AI (Workers AI, Vectorize, Agents SDK), networking (Tunnel, Spectrum), security (WAF, DDoS), and infrast (file: r4/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:durable-objects: Create and review Cloudflare Durable Objects. Use when building stateful coordination (chat rooms, multiplayer games, booking systems), implementing RPC methods, SQLite storage, alarms, Web (file: r4/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:sandbox-sdk: Build sandboxed applications for secure code execution. Load when building AI code execution, code interpreters, CI/CD systems, interactive dev environments, or executing untrusted code. Co (file: r4/sandbox-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:web-perf: Analyzes web performance using Chrome DevTools MCP. Measures Core Web Vitals (FCP, LCP, TBT, CLS, Speed Index), identifies render-blocking resources, network dependency chains, layout shifts, (file: r4/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:workers-best-practices: Reviews and authors Cloudflare Workers code against production best practices. Load when writing new Workers, reviewing Worker code, configuring wrangler.jsonc, or checking for common Workers (file: r4/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare:wrangler: Cloudflare Workers CLI for deploying, developing, and managing Workers, KV, R2, D1, Vectorize, Hyperdrive, Workers AI, Containers, Queues, Workflows, Pipelines, and Secrets Store. Load before (file: r4/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- composio-cli: Help users operate the published Composio CLI to find the right tool, connect accounts, inspect schemas, execute tools, subscribe to trigger events with `composio listen`, script workflows w (file: r1/composio-cli/SKILL.md)\n- computer-use: OS/window-level inspection and input in visible local app windows through `orca computer`: native apps, external browser windows (Chrome, Edge, Safari), and app webviews. Not for Orca's embe (file: r1/computer-use/SKILL.md)\n- create-pr-ghother: Create or update a GitHub pull request using `GH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other`, especially when the current worktree is dirty or based on the wrong branch. Use this when the user wants a PR a (file: r1/create-pr-ghother/SKILL.md)\n- diffpanel-chapters: Generate and publish persistent Diffpanel review chapters for local Git worktrees, staged changes, branch or commit ranges, and repository-wide snapshots. Use when a user asks to organize co (file: r1/diffpanel-chapters/SKILL.md)\n- documents:documents: Create, edit, redline, and comment on `.docx`, Word, and Google Docs-targeted document artifacts inside the container, with a strict render-and-verify workflow. Use `render_docx.py` to genera (file: r11/documents/26.909.61513/skills/documents/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-code-connect: Creates and maintains Figma Code Connect template files that map Figma components to code snippets. Use when the user mentions Code Connect, Figma component mapping, design-to-code translation (file: r5/figma-code-connect/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-create-new-file: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `create_new_file` tool call. NEVER call `create_new_file` directly without loading this skill first. Trigger whenever the u (file: r5/figma-create-new-file/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-design-to-code: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE calling the `get_design_context` Figma MCP tool. You MUST trigger this skill whenever the user wants to implement, build, port, (file: r5/figma-design-to-code/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-design: Use this skill alongside figma-use when the task involves translating an application page, view, or multi-section layout into Figma. Triggers: 'write to Figma', 'create in Figma from code',  (file: r5/figma-generate-design/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-diagram: MANDATORY prerequisite — load this skill BEFORE every `generate_diagram` tool call. NEVER call `generate_diagram` directly without loading this skill first. Trigger whenever the user asks to (file: r5/figma-generate-diagram/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-library: Build or update a professional-grade design system in Figma from a codebase. Use when the user wants to create variables/tokens, build component libraries, create individual components with pr (file: r5/figma-generate-library/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generative-plugins: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — load this skill before calling `create_generative_plugin` or `update_generative_plugin`. Use when the user asks to create, author, change, fix, or extend a reusa (file: r5/figma-generative-plugins/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-implement-motion: Translates Figma motion and animations into production-ready application code. Use when implementing animation/motion from a Figma design — user mentions \"implement this motion\", \"add animat (file: r5/figma-implement-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-shaders: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — load this skill before calling `create_shader` or `update_shader`. Use when the user asks to create, author, change, fix, or iterate on a shader effect, shader fil (file: r5/figma-shaders/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-swiftui: SwiftUI ↔ Figma translation. Use whenever the user mentions Swift, SwiftUI, iOS, iPhone, or iPad — in EITHER direction — translating a Figma design into SwiftUI (design → code), or pushing S (file: r5/figma-swiftui/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `use_figma` tool call. NEVER call `use_figma` directly without loading this skill first. Skipping it causes common, hard-to (file: r5/figma-use/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-figjam: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the FigJam context. Can be used alongside figma-use which has foundational context for using the use_figma tool. (file: r5/figma-use-figjam/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-motion: Motion / animation context for the `use_figma` MCP tool — animating Figma nodes via manual keyframes, animation styles, easing, and timeline duration. Load alongside figma-use whenever a tas (file: r5/figma-use-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-slides: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the Slides context. Can be used alongside figma-use which has foundational context for using the use_figma tool. (file: r5/figma-use-slides/SKILL.md)\n- find-skills: Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how do I do X\", \"find a skill for X\", \"is there a skill that can...\", or express interest in extending capabilities. (file: r1/find-skills/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-docs: Prompt- and template-complete Google Docs creation and editing with explicit-instruction-authoritative structural preservation, including semantic roles, relationships, comparison dimensions, (file: r6/google-docs/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive: Use connected Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work. Use when the user wants to find, fetch, organize, share, export, copy, or delete Drive files, o (file: r6/google-drive/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-drive-comments: Write, reply to, and resolve Google Drive comments on Docs, Sheets, Slides, and Drive files with evidence-backed location context. Use when the user asks to leave comments, review a file with (file: r6/google-drive-comments/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-sheets: Analyze and edit connected Google Sheets with range precision. Use when the user wants to create Google Sheets, find a spreadsheet, inspect tabs or ranges, search rows, plan formulas, create  (file: r6/google-sheets/SKILL.md)\n- google-drive:google-slides: Route Google Slides authoring requests and derive a design system from a native template or reference deck. Use this skill when the user provides an existing native Google Slides deck as a te (file: r6/google-slides/SKILL.md)\n- notion:notion-knowledge-capture: Capture conversations and decisions into structured Notion pages; use when turning chats/notes into wiki entries, how-tos, decisions, or FAQs with proper linking. (file: r7/notion-knowledge-capture/SKILL.md)\n- notion:notion-meeting-intelligence: Prepare meeting materials with Notion context and supplemental research; use when gathering context, drafting agendas/pre-reads, and tailoring materials to attendees. (file: r7/notion-meeting-intelligence/SKILL.md)\n- notion:notion-research-documentation: Research across Notion and synthesize into structured documentation; use when gathering info from multiple Notion sources to produce briefs, comparisons, or reports with citations. (file: r7/notion-research-documentation/SKILL.md)\n- notion:notion-spec-to-implementation: Turn Notion specs into implementation plans, tasks, and progress tracking; use when implementing PRDs/feature specs and creating Notion plans + tasks from them. (file: r7/notion-spec-to-implementation/SKILL.md)\n- orca-cli: Operate Orca-managed worktrees, folder contexts, terminals, repos, automations, artifacts, skill sharing, worktree comments, and Orca's embedded browser through the `orca` CLI. Use when the  (file: r1/orca-cli/SKILL.md)\n- orca-linear: Linear ticket work through Orca's CLI. Use when working from a linked Linear issue, finishing work with a PR/MR link and a completion comment, moving a ticket through workflow states, sear (file: r1/orca-linear/SKILL.md)\n- orchestration: Coordinate supervised Orca workers: threaded messages, blocking ask/reply, task dispatch, worker_done/escalation waits, task DAGs, decision gates, coordinator loops, and decomposing work a (file: r1/orchestration/SKILL.md)\n- pdf:pdf: Read, create, inspect, render, and verify PDF files where visual layout matters, including fillable AcroForms. Use Poppler rendering plus Python tools such as reportlab, pdfplumber, and pypdf (file: r11/pdf/26.909.61513/skills/pdf/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially (file: r8/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r11/presentations/26.909.61513/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- product-design:audit: Audit or critique a product flow, journey, workflow, funnel, onboarding path, checkout path, settings path, screen, or multi-step product experience by capturing screenshots first, then rep (file: r9/audit/SKILL.md)\n- product-design:ideate: Generate image-based alternatives, design remixes, or new design directions from a Product Design brief. Use when the user asks for design variants, visual exploration, design remixes, or ima (file: r9/ideate/SKILL.md)\n- product-design:image-to-code: Implement a selected image, screenshot, mockup, or Image Gen reference as a faithful, responsive frontend. (file: r9/image-to-code/SKILL.md)\n- product-design:index: Use when Product Design is explicitly invoked, or when the user's main goal is to explore a design, research UX, audit or critique a UX, product or web design, faithfully clone a visual des (file: r9/index/SKILL.md)\n- product-design:url-to-code: Clone a live URL as a runnable frontend-only local app. (file: r9/url-to-code/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-analyze: Analyze a file for quality and security issues using SonarQube (file: r10/sonar-analyze/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-coverage: Find files with low test coverage and inspect uncovered lines in a SonarQube project (project key optional when MCP integration already defines the default project) (file: r10/sonar-coverage/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-dependency-risks: Search for software composition analysis (SCA) dependency risks in a SonarQube project (project key optional when MCP integration already defines the default project) (file: r10/sonar-dependency-risks/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-duplication: Find files with code duplications in a SonarQube project and inspect duplication blocks for a file (project key optional when MCP integration already defines the default project) (file: r10/sonar-duplication/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-fix-issue: Fix a specific SonarQube issue in code by rule key and location (file: r10/sonar-fix-issue/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-integrate: Installs sonarqube-cli if not already installed, authenticates, and integrates SonarQube with the current agent (installs analysis hooks \u0026 SonarQube MCP Server). Use when the user wants to  (file: r10/sonar-integrate/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-list-issues: Search and filter SonarQube issues for a project, branch, or pull request via sonarqube-cli (`-p` is always required on the CLI; resolve the key from user arguments or sonar-project.propert (file: r10/sonar-list-issues/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-list-projects: List SonarQube projects accessible to the current user (file: r10/sonar-list-projects/SKILL.md)\n- sonarqube:sonar-quality-gate: Show SonarQube quality gate status for a project — pass/fail and each condition (metric key, threshold, actual value). Project key optional when MCP integration already defines the default  (file: r10/sonar-quality-gate/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use skill when user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets with formulas, formatting, charts, tab (file: r12/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established  (file: r12/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- superset-10x: Personalized audit that teaches the advanced Superset features the user isn't using yet (automations, parallel agents, tasks, multi-host, terminal remote control, custom commands, MCP) and s (file: r1/superset-10x/SKILL.md)\n- superset-automate: Turn a recurring chore into a Superset automation. Drafts the agent prompt, confirms schedule and target, creates it with the CLI, and reviews the first run together. Use when the user wan (file: r1/superset-automate/SKILL.md)\n- superset-browser: Open, navigate, screenshot, read, click, and type in web pages from an agent. Use when the user asks to open a URL, preview or verify a running web app, check a page's console, fill a form,  (file: r1/superset-browser/SKILL.md)\n- superset-computer: Operate the user's real desktop apps and windows on macOS, Windows, or Linux. Use when the user asks to open or drive a native app, click or type in a desktop UI, inspect or arrange a wind (file: r1/superset-computer/SKILL.md)\n- superset-contribute: Set up a Superset open-source contribution, from forking and cloning superset-sh/superset through local dev setup and the repo's rules to a merge-ready PR. Use when the user wants to contr (file: r1/superset-contribute/SKILL.md)\n- superset-doctor: Diagnose and fix Superset problems such as connection failures, offline hosts, terminals not attaching, auth or update issues. Use when the user reports something broken or misbehaving in  (file: r1/superset-doctor/SKILL.md)\n- superset-feedback: Collect and submit feedback about Superset (bug reports, feature requests, or general feedback) privately to the Superset team or as a public GitHub issue. Use when the user wants to repor (file: r1/superset-feedback/SKILL.md)\n- superset-integrations: Discover and call the tools a connected integration exposes, such as Linear, GitHub, Sentry, or Notion, through `superset mcp`. Use when the user wants something done in a connected servic (file: r1/superset-integrations/SKILL.md)\n- superset-orchestrate: Coordinate several coding agents in parallel through Superset, each in its own workspace, with follow-ups, progress reads, dependency tracking, and structured results. Use when the user asks (file: r1/superset-orchestrate/SKILL.md)\n- superset-page: Build and publish a self-contained HTML page to Superset, then answer the comments readers pin to it. Use when the user asks to make or publish a page, turn a report, dashboard, chart, doc (file: r1/superset-page/SKILL.md)\n- superset-plugins: Install Superset plugins, connect the accounts they need, and call their MCP tools through the credential proxy. Use when the user wants a plugin installed, removed, enabled, or connected, a (file: r1/superset-plugins/SKILL.md)\n- superset-setup: Make a repository Superset-ready by authoring .superset/config.json with setup, teardown, and run scripts so every new workspace boots configured, then verifying with a real workspace. Use w (file: r1/superset-setup/SKILL.md)\n- superset-standup: Digest of what the user's Superset agents did while they were away, sweeping workspaces, tasks, and agent terminals to report what finished, what needs review, and what's blocked. Use when t (file: r1/superset-standup/SKILL.md)\n- sync-sf-next-to-dev: Inventory changes on local Superfunctions `next` that are relevant to local `dev`, limited to functions and shared packages already present on `dev`, ignoring `.conduct` artifacts and igno (file: r0/sync-sf-next-to-dev/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference docu (file: r11/template-creator/26.909.61513/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- use-skillplane: Use skillplane to resolve skill (file: r1/skillplane/SKILL.md)\n- using-21nco-github: Uses the thyaravind GitHub account for repositories under dev/n or with a 21nCo origin. Use for any GitHub CLI, pull request, issue, release, or repository operation in those repositories. (file: r1/using-21nco-github/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compa (file: r3/visualize/1.0.38/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capps_instructions\u003e\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n\u003c/apps_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cplugins_instructions\u003e\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\n### How to use plugins\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n\u003c/plugins_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ae-7c81-7b51-859e-3f152304feb2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["memories.instructions","host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","apps.instructions","plugins.usage_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"You are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ae-7c81-7b51-859e-3f2d8ba34051","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.usage_hint"],"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ae-7c81-7b51-859e-3f3349c92afa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-09-22\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAsia/Kolkata\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"disabled\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"unrestricted\" /\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ae-7c81-7b51-859e-3f4f6a3e5a2b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"$use-skillplane pr-refetch-fix","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0c7ab-280c-7411-a12d-e3972c5f994d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1790056278.028145,"turn_id":"01a0c7ab-235a-7900-93f6-a63dbefa6872"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"retained_context":{"incomplete":false,"next_order":0,"user_messages":[],"user_messages_incomplete":true,"verified_answers":[]},"window_id":"01a0c7ae-7c7d-7d82-928d-6c98d371756d","window_number":10}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-22T05:54:56.262Z","ordinal":6579,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{},"apps_instructions":true,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","instructions":"196577151ae691aee6e4add4dd0a75a5693c07da"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-09-22","timezone":"Asia/Kolkata","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"disabled\"><file_system type=\"unrestricted\" /></permission_profile></filesystem>"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/serro/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/serro/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r4` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/cloudflare/0.1.2/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/figma/11.0.0/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/google-drive/0.1.16/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/notion/0.1.8/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r9` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/product-design/0.1.55/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sonarqube/2.5.0/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r1