edgement evidence, not a substitute for current-code assessment; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 current Sonar `throw this` findings remain invalid because exact error identity is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\n\n## Round 13 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 38 target-suite tests and 94 remote-target tests passed (132 total). The tests distinguish padded token68 from structured auth parameters and prove both accepted and colliding target-authored phases.\n- External fixture: `node scripts/test-mcpfn-external-server.mjs` passed against the real child server. A malformed `Host: [invalid` received 400, a subsequent `/not-found` request received 404, and the same process then completed two authenticated scenarios plus the expected unauthenticated failure.\n- Complete affected-package suite: all 244 `@mcpfn/testing` tests passed. The package typechecked and built successfully under Node 22; both changed `.mjs` files passed `node --check`.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed every reported dependency/package test, typecheck, and build; Playwright; the external authenticated server probe; mixed-entry redaction; official conformance; examples; ESM/CJS imports; dry-run packs; packed-consumer installation; and round trips. The first attempt identified only the ephemeral missing Playwright headless-shell cache; reinstalling the pinned runtime resolved that environment gap, and the unchanged batch pass