values remain supported and are registered whole, while structured auth-param lists register every independently reflectable value or fail closed; report safety evidence must exercise both accepted and colliding dynamic structural values; an external conformance fixture must contain every request-derived parse/await failure inside one response-owning boundary so its Node listener never leaks a rejected promise.\n- Producers / consumers: credential headers produce either token68 or auth-param values consumed by target opening, redaction, reports, and JUnit; target-owned redactors produce normalized failure phases consumed by suite structure validation; Node request metadata and bodies feed the external Web `Request`, official SDK transport, CLI scenarios, release gate, and packed-consumer smoke tests.\n- Failure boundaries: padded token68 under custom schemes, Basic and Bearer compatibility, quoted/unquoted auth-param lists, dynamic failure phases that pass through or collide with target redaction, malformed Host values before body/auth work, oversized bodies, post-header errors, and fixture session cleanup.\n- Coordinated repair: recognize the RFC token68 alphabet and terminal padding before invoking auth-param parsing while retaining whole-token registration; keep the existing safe-phase regression and add the complementary collision rejection proof; replace the async Node listener with a synchronous delegator to one fully guarded async request handler, returning a controlled 400 for invalid request URLs and preserving existing 401/404/413/500 behavior; extend the real external-server probe so malformed Host input proves a 400 response and server survival.\n- Verification criteria: focused token68/auth-param and suite structural tests; direct external-fixture malformed-Host/server-survival execution; complete affected package suites/typechecks/builds; Node 22 release gate; `git diff --check`; whole-batch diff review; and one final local/remote/PR-head comparison. Hosted review/check surfaces will not be fetched again this round.\n\n### Round 13 defect families\n\n| Stable family | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Repair / evidence state |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-AUTHORIZATION-CREDENTIAL-GRAMMAR` | Non-Bearer token68 with `=` padding is mistaken for an auth-param list and rejected before transport | Actionable; recurring credential-discovery family | round 13 | round 13 | Full-grammar token68 discrimination selected at the authorization producer |\n| `MCP170-DYNAMIC-FAILURE-PHASE-EVIDENCE` | The safe dynamic-phase regression does not prove collision rejection, so removal of final phase validation could escape detection | Actionable test-evidence gap; recurring structural-safety family | round 13 | round 13 | Complementary target-redactor collision regression selected |\n| `MCP170-EXTERNAL-FIXTURE-REQUEST-BOUNDARY` | Request URL construction occurs outside the async fixture handler's error boundary, allowing malformed Host input to reject the listener promise | Actionable external-fixture resilience defect | round 13 | round 13 | Synchronous listener plus fully guarded handler and real malformed-Host probe selected |\n| `MCP170-SONAR-THROW-THIS` | Three findings flag intentional retry-owner self-rejection | Invalid analyzer interpretation | round 10 | round 13 | Exact error identity remains the cleanup ownership token; preserve behavior |\n\n- Snapshot dispositions before mutation: 3 new unresolved current threads deduplicate to 3 actionable root causes. Three current Sonar issues are the preserved invalid retry-owner family. Older valid blockers and dispositions remain preserved; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Required-check snapshot: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. Sonar Quality Gate passed, CodeRabbit was successful, and `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n- Convergence state: not clean. Three new/actionable roots and recurring credential-grammar and structural-safety families reset the clean streak to `0` despite the stable head and terminal successful check snapshot.\n\n## Round 13 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Fixed: 3 new review root causes. `MCP170-AUTHORIZATION-CREDENTIAL-GRAMMAR` now recognizes the complete token68 alphabet plus terminal padding before auth-param discovery while retaining whole-token redaction. `MCP170-DYNAMIC-FAILURE-PHASE-EVIDENCE` now exercises both safe pass-through and target-redactor collision rejection. `MCP170-EXTERNAL-FIXTURE-REQUEST-BOUNDARY` now delegates from a synchronous Node listener to one guarded async handler, returns 400 for an invalid request URL, and retains 401/404/413/500 behavior.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 92 older unresolved threads retain their prior current-code dispositions (31 current and 61 outdated-only at this snapshot). Pullfrog's no-new-issues review was acknowledgement evidence, not a substitute for current-code assessment; outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: the 3 current Sonar `throw this` findings remain invalid because exact error identity is the cleanup ownership token; the older payload-key-redaction finding retains its prior invalid disposition. No accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: 0 valid code root causes. All 3 new review roots were attempted in the single coordinated batch; hosted reviewer confirmation is deferred to a future separately scheduled snapshot because post-push refetch is forbidden.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured threads, nested comments, review bodies, issue comments, raw inline comments, or current Sonar issues.\n\n## Round 13 verification evidence\n\n- Focused regressions: 38 target-suite tests and 94 remote-target tests passed (132 total). The tests distinguish padded token68 from structured auth parameters and prove both accepted and colliding target-authored phases.\n- External fixture: `node scripts/test-mcpfn-external-serve